r the \npeople who failed to get employment; \n(d) If so, the State-wise details thereof?  \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a)  Under Skill India Mission, the Government is i mplementing more than 40 skill \ndevelopment schemes/programmes across 20 Central Mi nistries/Departments, for \nproviding a variety of skill development training p rogrammes on pan India basis in  \nwhich 556.1 Lakhs persons have been trained. Number  of persons trained under the \nvarious schemes being implemented by the Ministry o f Skill Development and \nEntrepreneurship under the auspices of Skill India Mission is as follows: -  \n \nEntity  Persons trained during 2015 -16 to till \ndate as per available data  \n[Number in lakhs)  \nPradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikash Yojana \n(PMKVY ) 1.0 and 2.0 \n126.82 \n \nFee based training by National Skill \nDevelopment Corporation (NSDC)  \n117.39 \n \nNational Apprenticeship Promotion Scheme \n(NAPS) \n10.73 \n \nJan Shikshan Sansthan (JSS) 15.10  \nIndustrial Training Institutes (ITIs) {Long term \ntraining through State Government and \nPrivate ITIs] \n64.01 \n \nTotal  334 .1 \n \n\n \n(b)    10,49,621 candidates have been provided empl oyment under PMKVY and \nNAPS during the last two years while JSS does not p rovide for any mandatory \nemployment. Similarly, Ministry does not monitor th e employment of students passing \nout of ITIs. \n \n(c ) &(d) Government has taken various steps for ge nerating employment in the \ncountry like encouraging various projects involving  substantial investment and \nincreasing public expenditure on schemes like Prime  Minister’s Employment Generation \nProgramme (PMEGP),  Pt. Deen Dayal Upadhyaya Gramee n Kaushalya Yojna  (DDU-\nGKY), Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guar antee Scheme (MGNREGS), \nand Deendayal Antodaya Yojana-National Urban Liveli hoods Mission (DAY-NULM) run \nby Ministry of Micro, Small & Medium Enterprises, M inistry of Rural Development and \nMinistry of Housing & Urban Affairs, respectively. Under PMKVY 2.0 scheme of MSDE, \ntraining Centres takes all efforts to facilitate pl acements of certified candidates through \nmultiple initiatives like Rojgaar Mela, Yuva Sampar k and Aatmanirbhar Skilled \nEmployees Employer Mapping (ASEEM) ’portal, collabo ration with industries and \nCouncil. In PMKVY 3.0 facilitation of wage employme nt / self-employment / \napprenticeship shall be provided to the certified c andidates under the STT component \nof the scheme. Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Kendras shall  act as nodal Centres at districts \nfor providing self-employment support. Training Par tner shall work in coordination with \nDSC / SSDM for placement of the candidates in addit ion Rozgar Melas to be organized \non regular interval at district and regional level by TP / DSCs / SSDMs / SSCs for \nplacement and apprenticeship. All candidates certif ied under PMKVY 3.0 would also be \nlinked to ASEEM portal to find job opportunities.  \n \n \n******* \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |15", "t": "Inclusion under 15th Finance Commission", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 15 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nIIT, Madras \n \n15.  SHRI T.R. BAALU: \n \nWill the Minister of Education be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that Indian Institute of Technology(IIT) \nMadras which is pride of Chennai is fast turning into a place for discrimination \nagainst students and faculty on caste and religious basis;  \n \n(b) the action taken by the Government and IIT Madras management to put an \nend to such inhumane and illegal practice enacted in IIT Chennai in the wake \nof the suicidal death of a girl student Ms Fathima in the hostel room in 2019;  \n \n(c) whether the Government is aware that atrocities against studen ts and \nFaculties including non-teaching staff belonging to SC, ST, non-Hindu religious \nminorities are continuing unabated as evidenced in the resignation of one \nAssistant Professor of Chennai IIT, recently; and  \n \n(d) if so, the action is going to be taken by the Central Government? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a) : IIT Madras does not discriminate on caste or religious basis. \n \n(b) : IIT Madras, through its students' outreach program, ensures the mental \nwell-being of every student/research scholar. The institutional \ncounselling service is being provided to the students/scholars on 24x7 \nbasis. There is a full -time functional Institute Hospital on campus to \nserve the students' health issues. Newly admitted students are inducted \nthrough an orientation program, wherein they interact with officials at \nvarious levels including Director of the Institute. The orientation program \naims to make the m comfortable in the Institute, whic h is a new \nenvironment for them. \n\n \n \n(c) & (d): Section 7 (1) of the Institutes of Technology Act, 1961 provides that \nthe Institute shall be open to persons of either sex and of whatever race, \ncreed, caste or class, and no test or condition shall be imposed as to \nreligious belief or profession in admitting or appointing members, \nstudents, teachers or workers or in any other connection whatsoever.  \n \n \n \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |150", "t": "High Airfare", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.150 \nTO BE ANSWERED 19th JULY, 2021 \n \nONLINE OF MUSEUM COLLECTIONS \n \n150.   SHRI BRIJENDRA SINGH:  \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Covid-19 pandemic has highlighted the need to make museum and gallery \ncollections     available for ‘open access’ online; \n(b) the number of artefacts at Government museums and galleries presently available for \nonline access as of June 2021; and  \n(c)  if so, the steps taken by the Government in this direction? \n  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER FOR CULTURE, TOURISM AND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH \nEASTERN REGION \n \n(G.KISHAN REDDY)     \n \n  (a) to (c): The Ministry of Culture has taken up the task of digitisation of collections of ten \n(10) museums under its domain through Jatan programme since April 2014, \neven  before the onset of Covid-19. Besides this, the Ministry also operates a scheme \nnamely ‘Museum Grant Scheme’ under which financial assistance is provided to State \nGovernments, Societies, Autonomous Bodies, Local Bodies and Trusts registered under \nthe Societies Act for digitisation of art objects in the museums across the country for \nmaking their images/catalogue available over the website for online access.  \n \n           The number of artefacts at Government museums and galleries under Ministry of \nCulture available for online access as of 30.06.2021 at the website \nhttp://museumsofindia.gov.in are as under: \n \nName of Museum                   No. of artefacts available on site \n \ni. National Museum, New Delhi   -  80997 \nii. Indian Museum, Kolkata    -  46326 \niii. Victoria Memorial Hall. Kolkata   -  26611 \niv. Salar Jung Museum, Hyderabad   -  47504 \nv. Allahabad Museum, Paryagraj   -  61642 \nvi. National Gallery of Modern Art, New Delhi -  12902 \nvii. National Gallery of Modern Art, Mumbai -  1460 \nviii. National Gallery of Modern Art, Bengaluru -  534 \nix. ASI Goa Museum     -  701 \nx. ASI, Nagarjunakonda Museum   -  8450 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |151", "t": "Status of Smart Cities Mission in Uttar Pradesh", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n \nLOK SABHA  \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †151  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nSKILL TRAINING DURING COVID PERIOD   \n†151. SHRI ARUN SAO: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware that technical  skill training is imparted to \nyouth in small chunks and it is not possible to bec ome skilled in this online training \nwithout any practical aspect and they can only get employment after attaining the \ntraining; \n \n(b)  if so, skill training centres for youth remain ed completely closed during the \ncovid period, the names of the schemes formulated b y the Government for imparting \nskill training of youth during such times along wit h the state-wise details of the \namount allocated therefor; \n \n(c)  the names of the schemes being finalized for i mplementation in Chhattisgarh \nState post-corona period; \n \n(d)  whether any proposal has been sent by the Gove rnment of Chhattisgarh to \nCentral Government during the corona period; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and the decision ta ken thereon? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a): There are industry job roles, particularly in the manufacturing sector, where \npractical component is important for completing ski ll training. However, in job roles \nwhere there can be partial delivery of skilling usi ng on-line modules, blended training \ncan be feasible. Such training using on-line mode c an have advantage such as \nincreasing the reach of training, less commutation for trainees, particularly women \ntrainees, etc. On the occasion of the World Youth S kill Day, on 15.07.2021, blended \nlearning in respect of six courses under the Crafts men Training Scheme (CTS) for \nlong-term training through the Industrial Training Institutes (ITIs) was launched. The \nsix courses are namely Electrician, Fitter, Welder,  Computer Operator & \nProgramming Assistant, Cosmetology and Diesel Mechanic.  \n \nThe National Skill Development Corporation, under t he Ministry of Skill Development \nand Entrepreneurship (MSDE), has created e-Skill In dia, a multi-lingual portal, \nproviding e-skilling opportunities to the Indian yo uth. e-Skill India works \ncollaboratively as an e-Learning aggregator portal by establishing strategic digital \nskilling partnerships with knowledge partners to br ing best-in-class digital \nopportunities for the Indian youth under one roof. The portal has over 900 e-courses \nand more than 7.5 lakh enrolments. Majority of e-co urse on e-Skill India are offered \nfor free. Likewise, the Bharat Skills portal, manag ed by DGT, is for pursuing long-\nterm training through the digital mode. \n \n\n(b): During the Covid period, a scheme named Garib Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyaan  \n(GKRA) was launched in June 2020 to address the imp act of Covid on migrant \nworkers in India. On the lines of GKRA, MSDE has un dertaken demand-driven \nskilling / orientation for sustainable livelihood o pportunities under Pradhan Mantri \nKaushal Vikas Yojana  (PMKVY). This program is being implemented across 119 \ndistricts of 6 States namely Assam, Bihar, Odisha, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh and \nUttar Pradesh. The total number of migrant workers trained and assessed, State-\nwise, as on July 10, 2021 is as under: \n \nState  Trained  Assessed  \nAssam 714  494  \nBihar 31,619  28,334  \nMadhya Pradesh  10,598  6,628  \nOdisha 1,690  1,233  \nRajasthan 12,478  11,227  \nUttar Pradesh 13,724  11,972  \nTotal  70,823  59,888  \n \n(c) to (e): The Ministry has not received any skill ing proposal from Government of \nChhattisgarh during the Covid period. However, the Ministry under the scheme \nPMKVY 3.0 has allocated physical target of 17,584 t o the State of Chhattisgarh with \na financial allocation of Rs. 4.86 crore. \n \n****** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |152", "t": "Road Accidents", "head": "������������������� �\n������������������� �\n��������������������� �\n�\n������\n��������� �\n�\n������������������������� �\n� �\n������������������������������� �� ������������ �\n������������������������������������������������������������������������ �\n�\n������������������������� �\n�\n�����������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������ �������� ��� ����� ���� ������� �������� ���� ����������� ��������� ��� �������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��� ������� ���� ������� ���� ���������� ������� ����� ������� ��������� ��� ������� ���� ��������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������\n������ �\n�\n����������������� ��������������������������� �\n�������������������� �\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����� ������������ ��� ���� ��������������� ��� ���� �������� ��� ���� ����� �������� ����� ����\n���������������������\n���� ���� �����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������\n������� ������������ �������� � �����������������\n��� �����������������������������\n�������������������\n�� � �����������\n��� ����������������������������� ��� � �����������\n��� ��������� ������ ��� ��������\n���������\n�� � �����������\n��� ��������������� ��� � �����������\n�\n���� ���� ��������� ������ ��� ������ ���� ��������� ���� ��������� ��� ���� ��������������� ���\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������\n��������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |153", "t": "Ground Water Pollution in Jharkhand", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 153 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nOnline education  \n153. SHRI G.S. BASAVARAJ:  \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether there have been large number of drop outs in primary classes in rural India due to \ninability of poor families mostly of migrant labour to send their children to schools as discovered \nduring the commencement of Academic Session 2021-22 and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether due to rising unemployment in rural areas, poor families find it difficult to provide \ntheir children in primary classes with smartphones and also to arrange for internet facilities at \nhome and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) whether the Government would consider re -opening of primary schools wherever the \npandemic conditions have abated and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a): Education is in the Concurrent List of the Co nstitution. In order to prevent drop outs, lower \nenrolments and loss of learning, the Ministry of Education has issued guidelines for \nidentification, smooth admission process and continued education of migrant children on 13 th \nJuly, 2020, inter -alia, reque sting the States to identify and enrol all such children without any \nprocedural formalities and maintain a database of children so admitted.  \nFurther, to ensure that children have access to education with quality and equity and to minimize \nthe impact of th e pandemic on school education in the country, the Department of School \nEducation has shared guidelines dated 7 th January, 2021 with all States/UTs which, among \nothers, include identification of out of school children from age 6 -18 years, enrolment drives and \nawareness generation, student support while schools are closed, continued Education for children \nwith Special Needs (CWSN), student support on school reopening and Teacher capacity \nbuilding.  \n\nAlso, a comprehensive Covid action plan   has been shared with the States and UTs on  4th May \n2021,  outlining the role of local bodies, formation of nodal group at village/town level, \nconducting door -to-door/helpdesk-based/app based survey t o identify out of school children,  \ntheir mainstreaming and resource sharing.   \n(b): An Alternative Academic Calendar has been prepared for learning solutions grade 1 to 12 \nfor both - a child with device and one without device.  Also, where the internet facil ity is not \navailable, Ministry of Education has taken many initiative s like One Class One Channel of \nSWAYAM PRABHA to impart education through TV and one DTH channel specifically for \nhearing imp aired students in sign language, Community Radio Stations and a podcast called \nShiksha Vani of CBSE, Textbooks, worksheet supplied to residence of learners, Handbook on \n21st Century Skills, supporting setting up of mobile school, virtual studios, virtual class rooms in \nschools, etc.  \n(c): The Ministry of Home Affairs  vide Order No. 40 -3/2020-DM-I(A) dated 30 th September, \n2020 has issued guidelines for re -opening of schools, after 15 th October,2020, in a graded \nmanner, for which the State/ UT Government may take a decision in consultation with the \nrespective school/ in stitution management, based on their assessment of the situation. \nAccordingly, this Department has prepared and disseminated Standard Operating Procedure/ \nguidelines for Health and Safety protocols for Reopening of Schools and Learning with physical/ \nsocial distancing to all the States and UTs.  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |154", "t": "Khadi Prakritik Emulsion Paint", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *154\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 29.07.2021\n \n \nKhadi Prakritik Emulsion Paint \n \n \n*154. \n \n \nSHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS: \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND\n \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES be \npleased to state:\n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has taken initiative for providing assistance to \nstart business of Khadi Prakritik Emulsion Paint from cow dung under Khadi \nand Village Industries Commission (KVIC) and Micro, Small and M\nedium \nEnterprises (MSMEs); and \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO THE PARTS (a)&\n(b) OF \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION No.*154 FOR ANSWER\n \nON \n29.07.2021\n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nYes Sir.  The Government has taken the following initiatives \nthrough Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC) for providing \nassistance to start business of Khadi Prakritik\n \nEmulsion Paint from cow dung:\n \n \n \n(i)  KVIC through its Kumarappa National Handmade Paper Institute \n(KNHPI), Sanganer, Jaipur has developed the technology for \nmanufacturing of Khadi Prakritik Paint and providing \ntechnical\n \ntraining to interested Entrepreneurs to set\n-\nup their enterprises of \nmanufacturing of Khadi Prakritik Paint. \n \n \n(ii)\n \nA new production cum training unit of Khadi Prakritik Paint has been \nset up in the premises of Kumarrapa National Handmade Paper \nInstitute w\nith the production capacity of 1000 litres per day.\n \n \n(iii)\n \nKNHPI has started training programmes under manufacturing of Khadi \nPrakritik Paint since January, 2021 and upto 15.07.2021, 418 \ncandidates have been trained.  State/UT\n-\nwise number of candidate \ntrai\nned during the period from 18.01.2021 to 15.07.2021 is \nAnnexed\n.\n \n \n(iv) \n \nTransfer of Technology: \nKNHPI also transfers all the expertise and \ninformation of manufacturing of \nKhadi Prakritik Paint, \nsuch as \nsketches, drawing, materials, formulae, etc. to prospec\ntive \nentrepreneurs and also assists in selecting & installing machine and \nequipment.\n \n \n \n* * *\n \n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n \n \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a)&(b) of the Lok Sabha Starred \nQuestion No.*154 for answer on 29.07.2021\n \n \nState/UT\n-\nwise number of candidate trained by \nKumarappa National Handmade \nPaper Institute (KNHPI), Jaipur\n \nduring the period from 18.01.2021 to \n15.07.2021.\n \n \nSr.\n \nNo.\n \n \nName of State/UT\n \nNo. of Candidates \ntrained \n \n1\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n7\n \n2\n \nAssam\n \n2\n \n3\n \nBihar\n \n30\n \n4\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n4\n \n5\n \nDelhi\n \n18\n \n6\n \nGujarat\n \n15\n \n7\n \nHaryana\n \n16\n \n8\n \nJharkhand\n \n4\n \n9\n \nKarnataka\n \n13\n \n10\n \nKerala\n \n1\n \n11\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n21\n \n12\n \nMaharashtra\n \n128\n \n13\n \nOdisha\n \n2\n \n14\n \nPunjab\n \n1\n \n15\n \nRajasthan\n \n89\n \n16\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n5\n \n17\n \nTelangana\n \n4\n \n18\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n49\n \n19\n \nUttrakhand\n \n2\n \n20\n \nWest Bengal\n \n7\n \n \nTOTAL\n \n418\n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |155", "t": "Pollution in Satluj and Beas Rivers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *155\nANSWERED ON 29.07.2021\nPOLLUTION IN SATLUJ AND BEAS RIVERS\n*155. SHRI BHAGWANT MANN\nSHRI HANUMAN BENIWAL\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is aware of the fact that the waste water effluents and pollutants are being\nreleased into Satluj and Beas rivers and their tributaries thereby increasing pollution therein;\n(b) if so, the specific action taken by the Government in this regard during the last three years;\n(c) whether the Government is aware that people in many districts of Rajasthan and Punjab use this water\nfor drinking and if so, the details of diseases caused by polluted drinking water in these areas;\n(d) whether the Government is contemplating to formulate a concrete scheme to check the same and take\naction against the industrial units and urban authorities responsible for discharg ing waste water effluents\ninto the rivers and canals; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof along with the time line fixed to stop this water pollution?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI\n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT)\n(a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n*****\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED T O IN REPLY TO PART s (a) TO ( e) OF LOK SABHA STARR ED\nQUESTION NO.*155 TO BE ANSWERED ON THURS DAY, THE 29 TH JULY, 2021 ON\n‘POLLUTION IN SATLUJ AND BEAS RIVERS’ RAISED BY SHRI BHAGWANT MANN & SHRI\nHANUMAN BENIWAL.\n(a) to (e) The Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) in association with Pollution Control\nBoards/Committees in different States/Union Territories (UTs), has been monitoring water quality of rivers\nand other water bodies in the country through a network of monitorin g stations under the National Water\nQuality Monitoring Programme. Based on water quality monitoring results, pollution assessment of rivers\nhas been carried out by CPCB from time to time. As per the latest report published by CPCB in September\n2018, 351 po lluted stretches were identified on 323 rivers based on monitoring results in terms of Bio -\nchemical Oxygen Demand, an indicator of organic pollution. These included one stretch each on Satluj and\nBeas rivers and Kaliben (tributary of river Beas) which conf luence upstream of Harike Barrage from where\nRajasthan Feeder/Indira Gandhi Canal and Ferozpur Feeder originate.\nRivers in the country, including identified stretches of rivers Satluj and Beas in Punjab, are polluted\nmainly due to discharge of untreated or partially treated sewage from cities/towns and industrial effluents in\ntheir respective catchments, problems in operation and maintenance of sewage/effluent treatment plants,\nlack of dilution and other non -point sources of pollution. Rapid ur banization and industrialization have\ncompounded the problems.\nCleaning of rivers is an ongoing process. It is the responsibility of the States/UTs and local bodies to\nensure required treatment of sewage and industrial effluents to the prescribed norms before discharging into\nwater bodies or land to prevent and control of pollution therein. For conservation of rivers excluding Ganga\nand its tributaries, the Central Government has been supplementing efforts of the States/UTs by providing\nfinancial and technical assistance for abatement of pollution in identified stretches of rivers/tributaries in the\ncountry through the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of National River Conservation Plan (NRCP). Under\nNRCP, pollution abatement projects for conservation of Satluj and Beas rivers have been implemented in 14\ntowns of Punjab, namely, Banga, Bholath, Dasuya, Hoshiarpur, Jalandhar, Kapurthala, Ludhiana, Moga,\nMukerian, Nawanshehar, Phagwara, Phillaur, Sultanpur Lodhi and Tanda at a total cost of Rs.717.32 crore\nwith sewage treatment capacity of 648.20 million litres per day (MLD) created in these towns.\nBuddha nallah, carrying untreated sewage, industrial effluents and other wastes from Ludhiana town,\nremains the major source of pollution in Satluj r iver. To address the issue of pollution, Buddha Nallah\nRejuvenation Project has been launched in December, 2020 at a total estimated cost of Rs. 840 crore with\nimplementation period of 2 years. The project envisages construction of 2 new sewage treatment p lants\n(STPs) at Jamalpur (225 MLD) and at Balloke (60 MLD), refurbishment of 4 existing STPs of 418 MLD\ntotal capacity, Common Effluent Treatment Plants (CETPs) of capacity 40 MLD, 50 MLD and 15 MLD for\ncluster of small/medium scale dyeing industries, two Effluent Treatment Plants (ETPs) for 6 MLD waste\nfrom two dairy complexes, etc. To address the gap between sewage generation and treatment for remaining\ncatchment areas of rivers Sutlej and Beas, more STPs have been proposed.\nContd…P/2\n\n//2//\nDischarge of industrial effluents is monitored by CPCB and the respective State Pollution Control\nBoards/Committees through the provisions of the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 and th"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |156", "t": "Airport in Purnea", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 156 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19th July, 2021 \n \nYoga Education \n \n†156. SHRI RODMAL NAGAR: \n \nWill the Minister of Education be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to make Yoga,  a compulsory subject in schools and \ncolleges and appoint yoga teachers for this purpose ; and \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN ) \n (a) & (b):  National Curriculum Framework (NCF)  recommended Yoga as an integral part of \nHealth and Physical Education.  Health and Physical Education is a compulsory subject from \nClass I to Class X and optional from Class XI to XII.   The National Council of Educational \nResearch a nd Training (NCERT) ha s already developed integrated syllabi on Health and \nPhysical Education from Class I to Class X . In addition, Central Board of Secondary \nExamination (CBSE) has introduced a streamlined and well designed Health and Physical \nEducation (HPE) Program to mainstream health and physical education in schools for students of \nclass I - XII.  CBSE has made Health and Physical Education compulsory in all classes from I -\nXII.  The Board has directed schools to keep one period of HPE for classes I -XII each day.  The \nUniversity Grants Commission (UGC) has prepared guidelines for institutional fitness plan.  \nThese guidelines encourage higher educational institutions to adopt policies and practices \ntowards fitness and well being of students and staff.  The indicative guidelin es inter-alia suggests \ndenoting minimum one hour per day on fitness activities. \n\n  The Government has included Yoga training in the “Study in India” programme to \nencourage Yoga seekers from abroad to obtain authentic Yoga training in India. UGC has \napproved the establishment of an Inter University Centre -Yogic Science at Bengaluru and \nintroduced Yoga as a new National Eligibility Test (NET) subject from January 2017 UGC -NET \nonwards.  Yoga Departments have been established in nine Central Universities.  \nThe recruitment, service conditions and deployment of teachers including yoga teachers \nare under the jurisdiction of the State/Union Territory Governments and it is for the respective \nState/Union Territory Government to take a decision in such matters for thei r schools. However, \nas per UDISE 201 9-20 (provisional), the total number of Physical Education Teacher (PET) \nappointed are 72375. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |157", "t": "Development of Green Highways", "head": "������������������� �\n��������������������������������������� �\n��\n��������� �\n�������������������� ��� �\n������������ � � ��� ���� ������ �\n�\n��������������������� �������� �\n�\n������ ���������������������� ��\n������������������������������������� ��\n����������\n�������������������� ���������������������������� �\n�����������������������������������������������������\n�\n�������������������� �\n��\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ���� ���� ���������� ��� �������� ������\n��������� ��� ��������� ������������� ��� ���� ������ ��� ������� ���������\n��� �\n���� ��� �������� �������� �������� ������ ����� ���� ����� ������ ���� ���� ���� �����\n�������� �\n�\n������ �\n�\n������������������������������������������� �\n�\n��������������������������� �\n�\n��\n�\n��������� ��������������� ����������������������������������� �\n� �\n\n��������� ���������� ��� ��� ������ ��� ���� ������ �� �� �� ��� ���� ������\n�������� ��������� ������� ��������� ��� ����� ������ ����� ������ ���\n����� ����� ������� ������ ���� ����� ������� ��������� �������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������� � ������� ������������������ ��������� ��������� ������������ �\n��� ������ �������� ������ ��������� ��������� ������������ ����� �������������������\n������������ ������ ������ ��������� ��������� ��������� �������� ��������\n������� ��� ���� ��������� ����� ���� ����� ����������� ��� ������ ����� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��� � ������������ ����� ��� ���� � ��� ��� � �������� �� ���� ��������� ����� ���\n������������������������������ � ��� ������������� ������������������������� ����\n��������� �\n������� � ���������������� � ����������� � ���������������� �\n������������\n��\n������������������������� �\n�������������������������� � ����� � ������ �\n���\n����������������������������� �\n�������������������������� � ����� � ������ �\n����\n�������������������� �\n�������������������������� � ����� � ������ �\n�� �\n������������������� �\n����������������������� � ����� � ������ �\n� �\n��������������������������\n����������� �\n����������������� ������� �\n����� � ������ �\n�� �\n����������������������� �\n����������������������� � ����� � ������ �\n� ����� � ������ � ������� �\n�\n����� �"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |158", "t": "Dams and Water Projects in Gujarat", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *158\nANSWERED ON 29.07.2021\nDAMS AND WATER PROJECTS IN GUJARAT\n*158.       SHRI PATEL HASMUKHBHAI SOMABHAI\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has sanctioned a number of dams and water projects in Gujarat during the last\nthree years;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the details of funds sanctioned for these projects?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI\n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT)\n(a) to (c) A statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n******\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF STARRED QUESTION\nNO.*158 TO BE ANSWERED ON 29.07.2021 IN LOK SABHA REGARDING “DAMS AND\nWATER PROJECTS IN GUJARAT” RAISED BY SHRI PATEL HASMUKHBHAI SOMABHAI.\n(a) to (c) Water resources projects are planned, funded, executed, and maintained by the State\nGovernments themselves as per their own resources and priorities. Role of Government of India is limited\nto providing technical support and, in some cases, partial financia l assistance under the existing schemes\nbeing implemented by the Ministry of Jal Shakti.\nA cluster of 61 water bodies from Gujarat was included in the year 2018 -19 under Repair,\nRenovation and Restoration (RRR) of Water Bodies scheme of Pradhan Mantri Kr ishi Sinchayee Yojana-\nHar Khet Ko Pani (PMKSY -HKKP). Estimated cost of the scheme is Rs. 102.90 crore, with irrigation\npotential creation target of 11,364 hectare. Central Assistance (CA) of Rs. 8.81 crore has been released to\nthe scheme so far.\nFurther, a project with estimated cost of Rs. 163.29 crore has been sanctioned in 2019 -2020\nunder PMKSY -HKKP Ground Water Development component. The project envisages construction of\n2,512 large diameter dug wells, to create 3,768 hectare of irrigation command a rea by ground water.\nCentral assistance of Rs. 6.0 crore has been released for the project so far.\nFurther, Sardar Sarovar Project was included under Accelerated Irrigation Benefit Programme\n(AIBP) and Command Area Development and Water Management (CADWM ) components of PMKSY in\n2016. The central assistance released by Government of India during the last three years, i.e., from 2018 -\n19 to 2020-21 for the project is Rs.2,057.64 crore.\nAlso, since August 2019, Government of India in partnership with States is implementing Jal\nJeevan Mission (JJM) to make provision of tap water supply in adequate quantity and of prescribed\nquality, on regular and long -term basis through tap water connection to every rural household in the\ncountry by 2024. Since the launch of mission on 15 th August, 2019, an amount of Rs.2,226.04 crore has\nbeen released to Government of Gujarat. 13,45,495 rural households have been provided with tap water\nconnection in the State (as on 23.07.2021).\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |159", "t": "East West Corridor", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA \n    STARRED QUESTION NO. 159 \n    ANSWERED ON 29TH JULY, 2021 \n \nEAST WEST CORRIDOR \n \n*159.   DR. RAJDEEP ROY \n   Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n                  ������  ������������  ����  ��������������  �������� \n be pleased to state: \n(a) the present status of the work on the 25.2 kms stretch of the East -\nWest Corridor between Balacherra and Harangajao in Assam; and  \n(b) the present status of the Multi Modal Logistics Park proposed to \nbe constructed in Silchar? \n  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a)� & (b)   A statement is laid on the table of the House.- \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) & (b) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *159 FOR ANSWER ON 29.07.2021 ASKED BY DR. \nRAJDEEP ROY REGARDING EAST WEST CORRIDOR. \n \n(a)�  The physical progress for the four laning of Balacherra to \nHarangajao section of NH �� 29 (old NH �� 54 ) is 32.98% and the length \ncompleted till date is 10.36 km. \n \n(b)�    Ministry has assigned NHIDCL to carryout the DPR study for MMLP \n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\nhas been awarded to Consortium of M/s Ta ta Consulting Engineers Ltd. (TCE)  \nand M/s CBRE South Asia Private Limited.  The DPR work has commenced on \n09.04.2021 for the site (45.3 acres) proposed by Govt of Assam vide letter \ndated 01.04.2021.   \n \n***** \n \n�"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |16", "t": "Technical Education Quality Improvement Programme", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 16 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nTechnical Education Quality Improvement Programme \n \n*16. SHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is making continuous efforts to convince the Focused \nState Governments to prepare a sustainability plan for the Technical Education \nQuality Improvement Programme (TEQIP-III) of the Government to continue \nthe services rendered by the faculties engaged to upgrade the engineering \neducation in the focused States; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  the steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to safeguard this \ntechnical force engaged under TEQIP-III for which the programme period has \nended in March, 2021? \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n***** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 16 FOR 19.07.2021 ASKED BY \nSHRIMATI RAKSHA NIKHIL KHADSE, HON’BLE MEMBER OF \nPARLIAMENT REGARDING TECHNICAL EDUCATION QUALITY \nIMPROVEMENT PROGRAMME. \n \n(a to b) The Government of India has been making continuous efforts to ensure that \nthe focus State Governments prepare a sustainability plan for the Technical  \nEducation Quality Improvement Programme (TEQIP-III). Several communications \nhave been sent to the focus States requesting for preparation of a sustainability plan \nfor filling up vacant faculty positions through regular recruitments so as to avoid any \nacademic standstill beyond the project period i.e 30th September 2021. \n Ministry of Education has also written to the Chief Secretaries of Focus \nStates requesting to plan for continuation of activities undertaken during TEQIP-III, \nfor maintaining the quality of technical education. \n(c) Government of India has extended the TEQIP III project including the tenure \nof the temporary project faculties once from 30th September 2020 to 31st March 2021 \nand then finally up to 30th September 2021. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |160", "t": "Concessions for MRO Activities", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 160 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nResults of Board Exams \n \n160.  SHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND: \nSHRI GAURAV GOGOI:  \nSHRI MANICKAM TAGORE B.:  \nSHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA:  \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE:  \nDR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE: \nDR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR. S.:  \nSHRI THIRUNAVUKKARASAR SU:  \nSHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE:  \nDR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the C BSE cancelled class X and XII Board Exams 2021 due to pandemic \nsituation in the country and if so, the criteria adopted by the CBSE to determine the 12th \nclass result;  \n \n(b) whether the Government is aware that there is widespread discontentment among the \nstudents across the country about the CBSE evaluation criteria and if so, the steps \ntaken/propose to be taken by the Government; \n \n(c) whether the Government has taken into account the challenges faced by the students of \nclass 12 during academic year 2020-2021 and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(d) whether the average scores of students of class 12 in pre -board examination in many \nschools were lower than the previous years average and if so, the details thereof and the \nreasons therefor;  \n \n(e) whether the CBSE proposes to take into account the above facts while preparing class 12 \nresults; \n \n(f) whether only around 50 percent of the students had online connections for class 12 as \nresult of which online education could not reach all students affecting them adverse ly; \nand  \n\n(g) if so, whether the Government proposes to provide required infrastructure for online \neducation to students who are promoted to class 12? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a): Yes Sir. The annual examinations for classe s X and XII have been cancelled by the \nBoard.  Following criteria has been adopted by Central Board of Secondary Education \n(CBSE) to prepare the results of Class-XII:-  \n \n(i) The assessment of theory portion will be done by the school based on the following: \n \nClass XII Marks based on Unit Test/Mid-Term/ Pre-Board Exam 40 % \nClass XI Marks based on theory component of final exam 30 % \nClass X Marks based on average theory component of best three \nperforming subjects out of main 5 subjects. \n30 % \n \n(ii) The mark s of Practical / Internal Assessment etc. of class – XII will be on actual \nbasis as uploaded by the school on the CBSE portal. \n \n(b): Policy for preparation of results has been approved by the Hon’ble Supreme Court of \nIndia in the matter of Mamta Sharma Vs CBSE. \n \n(c) and (e): Following challenges were observed and addressed:- \n \n• Diversity in instructional methods, \n• Non-uniform evaluation process, \n• Multiplicity in types of assessment conducted by the schools and  \n• Conduct of online and offline classes.  \n  \n(d): The conduct of pre-board examination is the prerogative of individual schools.  No data \nof the conduct of pre-board examinations is collected by CBSE. \n \n(f) and (g): Online classes are being conducted at the schools by employing various digital tools \nin the fo rm of DIKSHA portal ( https://diksha.gov.in/), SWAYAM Portal \n(swayam.gov.in), SWAYAM Prabha, DD Channel, e -Pathshala \n(https://epathshala.nit.in/), NROER (National Repository o f Open Educational \nResources) portal (https://nroer.gov.in/welcome). \n  \nLearning programmes have also been started in the form of offline learning tools in \nthe form of alternative academic calendar by NCERT radio, community radio and \nCBSE podcasts, toll free numbers, missed call, SMS based requests for audio \ncontent, localized radio content for edutainment etc.  The medium of TV, radio  and \ntextbooks is also used for the students who do not have digital means. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |161", "t": "Pradhan Mantri Mahila Shakti Kendra Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.161 \nANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF RAMAYANA CIRCUIT \n \n161.  SHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA:  \nSHRI MANOJ TIWARI:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Go vernment has any proposal to develop \nRamayana Circuit in the near future;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(d)  whether any other religions circuit is also under consideration; \nand  \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF TOURISM            (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n(a) to (e): Ramayana Circuit is one of the identified thematic circuit \nof ‘Swadesh Darshan’ scheme of the Ministry of Tourism under \nwhich 2 projects have been sanctioned. The Ministry has also \nsanctioned projects under Buddhist, Tirthankar, Krishna and \nSpiritual Circuits.  \n \nIn addition, the Ministry of Tourism  provides  financial  \nassistance  to  State Governments / Union Territory  Administrations \nfor  integrated  development of  identified  pilgri mage and  heritage  \ndestinations  under  ‘National Mission on  Pilgrimage Rejuvenation \nand  Spiritual, Heritage Augmentation Drive (PRASHAD)’  scheme.  \n \nThe detail of projects sanctioned under Ramayana, Krishna,  \nSpiritual, Tirthankar and Buddhist Circuits  under  Swadesh Darshan  \nscheme  and  the  detail of  the  projects  sanctioned under  \n‘PRASHAD’  scheme is  annexed.     \n*******\n\n             ANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF L OK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 161 ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \nDEVELOPMENT OF RAMAYANA CIRCUIT. \n \nTHE DETAIL OF PROJECTS SANCTIONED UNDER RAMAYANA, \nBUDDHIST, TIRTHANKAR, KRISHNA AND SPIRITUAL CIRCUITS OF \nSWADESH DARSHAN SCHEME AND DETAIL OF PROJECTS \nSANCTIONED UNDER PRASHAD SCHEME.   \n       (Amt. in Rs. crore) \nS.  \nNo. \nName of \nState \nName of \nCircuit & \nYear \nProject Name Amt. \nSanctioned \nRAMAYANA CIRCUIT \n1. Uttar \nPradesh \n (2016-17) Development of \nChitrakoot and \nShringverpur \n69.45 \n2. Uttar \nPradesh \n (2017-18) Development of \nAyodhya 127.21 \nKRISHNA CIRCUIT \n1. Haryana  (2016-17) Development of \nTourism \nInfrastructures at \nBrahmsarovar, \nSanhitSarovar, \nNarkatari, Jyotisar in \nKurukshetra. \n97.35 \n2. Rajasthan  (2016-17) Integrated \nDevelopment of \nGovind Dev ji temple \n(Jaipur), KhatuShyam \nJi (Sikar) and \nNathdwara \n(Rajsamand). \n75.80 \nSPIRITUAL CIRCUIT \n1. Kerala  (2016-17) Development of \nSabarimala - Erumeli-\nPampa-Sannidhanam \nas a Spiritual Circuit \nin      District \nPathanamthitta, \n99.99 \n\nKerala. \n2. Manipur  (2016-17) Development of Shri \nGovindajee Temple, \nShri Bijoy Govindajee \nTemple – Shri \nGopinath Temple – \nShri Bungshibodon \nTemple – Shri Kaina \nTemple. \n53.80 \n3. Kerala \n \n (2016-17) Development of \nSreePadmanabha \nTemple, Aranmula - \nSabrimala \n92.22 \n \n4. Bihar \n \n (2016-17) Integrated \nDevelopment of \nKanwaria Route: \nSultanganj- Mojma- \nBanka. \n44.76 \n \n5. Uttar Pradesh  (2016-17) Development of \nSpiritual Circuit -I \n(Shahjahanpur-\nAllahabad-Basti-\n Ahar-Aligharh-\nKasgunj-Sarosi-\nPratapgarh-Unnao-\nKaushambi- Mirzapur-\nGorakpur- Kairana- \nDoamriyagunj- \nBagpat-Barabanki- \nAzamgarh) \n65.61 \n6. Uttar Pradesh  (2016-17) Development of \nBijnor- Meerut- \nKanpur- Kanpur \nDehat- Banda- \nGhazipur- Salempur- \nGhosi- Balia- \nAmbedkar Nagar - \nAligarh- Fatehpur- \nDeoria- Mahoba- \nSonbhadra- \nChandauli- Mishrikh- \nBhadohi \n67.51 \n\n7. Rajasthan  (2016-17) Development of \nChuru (Salasar \nBalaji)– Jaipur (Shri \nSamode Balaji, \nGhatke Balaji, \nBandheke Balaji) - \nAlwar (Pandupole \nHanumanji, \nBharathari)- \nViratnagar (Bijak, \nJainnasiya, Ambika \nTemple)- Bharatpur \n(Kaman Region) - \nDholpur (Muchkund) – \nMehandipur Balaji - \nChittorgarh \n(Sanwaliyaji) \n93.90 \n8. Puducherry  (2017-18) Development of \nKaraikal, Yanam and \nPuducherry \n34.96 \n9. Bihar  (2017-18) Development of \nMandar Hill &Ang \nPradesh. \n47.52 \n10. Maharashtra (2018-19) Development of \nWaki- Adasa- \nDhapewada- \nParadsingha- Chota \nTaj Bagh - \nTelankhandi- Girad \n54.01 \n11. Uttar Pradesh  (2018-19) Development of \nJewar-Dadri-\nSikandrabad-Noida-\nKhurja-Banda \n12.03 \n12. Uttar Pradesh  (2018-19) Development of \nGorakhnath Temple \n(Gorakhpur), \nDevipattan Temple \n(Balrampur) and \nVatvashni Temple \n(Domariyaganj) \n15.76 \n\n13. Kerala  (2018-19) Development \nSivagiriSree \nNarayana Guru \nAshram- \nArruvipuram- \nKunnumpara Sree \nSubrahmania-   \nChembazhanthi Sree \nNarayana Gurukulam \n69.47 \nTIRTHANKAR  \n1. Bihar \n \n (2016-17) Development of \nVaishali- Arrah- \nMasad- Patna- Rajgir- \nPawapuri- \nChampapuri. \n37.19 \n \nBUDDHIST CIRCUIT \n1. Madhya \nPradesh \n(2016-17) Development of \nSanchi-Satna-Rewa-\nMandsaur-Dhar. \n74.02 \n2. Uttar Pradesh  (2016-17) Development of \nSrawasti, Kushinagar, \n& Kapilvastu. \n99.97 \n \n3. Bihar  (2016-17) Construction of \nConvention Centre at \nBodhgaya \n98.73 \n4. Gujarat  (2017-18) Development of \nJunagadh- Gir \nSomnath- Bharuch-\nKutch- Bhavnagar- \nRajkot- Mehsana \n28.67 \n5. Andhra \nPradesh \n (2017-18) \n \nDevelopme"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |162", "t": "Licensing for AYUSH-64", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n  \nLOK SABHA  \n  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 162  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nBOARD FOR SKILL DEVELOPMENT \n \n162. DR. SANJAY JAISWAL: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is planning to set targ ets for percentage of people \nskilled as it is there in the case of literacy; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the rea sons therefor; \n \n(c)  whether the Government is planning to set up a  board for skill development \nthat is similar to the Central Board of Secondary E ducation to ensure the \nassessment and standard of skill development and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d)  whether there are any incentive mechanisms to ensure employers take part in \nactive recruitment and also in the process of skilling and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) & (b): Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana  (PMKVY) is the flagship skill \ntraining scheme of the Ministry of Skill Developmen t and Entrepreneurship (MSDE). \nUnder PMKVY 2.0 (2016-2020), a target to train one crore individuals was set. The \nnumber of candidates trained is 1.09 crore. Under P MKVY 3.0 (2020-2022), which \nwas launched on 15.01.2021, a target to train 8 lak h individuals has been set. The \nnumber of candidates trained so far is 1.2 lakh. \n \n(c): MSDE, vide notification dated 5 \nth  December 2018, has set up National Council \nfor Vocational Education and Training (NCVET) as an  overarching regulatory body \nfor the skill eco-system. It is mandated to regulate the functioning of entities engaged \nin vocational education and training, both long and  short-term, and establish \nminimum standards for the functioning of such entities. \n \n(d): Under PMKVY scheme, various initiatives are ta ken to ensure better placement \noutcomes for the trained/ certified candidates. Rojgar Melas  are conducted for \nplacement of youth by industry. MSDE has launched A atmanirbhar Skilled \nEmployees Employer Mapping (ASEEM) portal, which ac ts as a directory of skilled \nworkforce. The objective is to provide a platform t hat matches supply of skilled \nworkforce with the market demand, thereby facilitat ing better livelihood opportunities \nfor youth and availability of ready skilled manpower to employers. \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |163", "t": "Combating Land Degradation and Desertification", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 163 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nVacant posts in IITs and NITs \n \n†163. SHRI VIJAY BAGHEL: \n \nWill the Minister of Education be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of institutes where the post of chairman and other posts are \n lying vacant in the governing board of the IITs and NITs across the \n country;  \n \n(b) whether any time limit has been fixed for filling up these vacant posts;  \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(d)  if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a):   At present, 8 posts of Chairman at IITs and 21 posts of Chairman at \nNITs are vacant. Further, 5 posts of Directors in IITs and 5 posts in NITs are \nvacant.   \n(b) to (d ): The nomination/selection of Chairman and other posts is a \ncontinuous process.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |164", "t": "Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 164 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2021 \n \nJagdishpur-Haldia Gas Pipeline Project  \n \n164.    MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम और \u0000ाकृ\u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the status of Jagdishpur-Haldia Gas pipeline project; \n(b) the tentative timeline for supply of CNG for the city of Kolkata; and \n(c) the plans and timeline for supplying piped cooking gas in Kolkata? \n  \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ोिलयमऔर\u0000ाकृितकगैसमं\u0000ालयम\u0000रा\u0000यमं\u0000ी \n (\u0000ीरामे\u0000वरतेल\u0000) \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB) has authorized 2,655 Km long \nJagdishpur-Haldia-Bokaro-Dhamra Natural Gas Pipeline (JHBDPL) to GAIL (India) Limited to \nconnect eastern parts of the country. About 1,986 Km of pipeline has been laid as on 31.03.2021.  \n \n(b) & (c) Bengal Gas Company Limited (BGCL) has been authorized for the development of \nCity Gas Distribution (CGD) network in the Geographical Area (GA) of Kolkata Municipal \nCorporation and parts adjoining districts of North 24 Parganas, South 24 Parganas, Howrah, \nHooghly and Nadia. Supply of Natural Gas to the GA has started. Development of the CNG \nstations /PNG connections in a GA is done as per the terms and conditions of the authorization.  \n \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |165", "t": "Framework for Organ Transplantation", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.165\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 30TH JULY, 2021\nFRAMEWORK FOR ORGAN TRANSPLANTATION\n*165. DR. ARVIND KUMAR SHARMA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is aware that around four lakh people die in India every\nyear waiting for an organ transplant and if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether the Government has taken note of various difficulties being faced by the\nreceiver viz., finances for transplant, post transplant medication, legal complexities,\nnon uniform definitions, lack of necessary infrastructure, information gap and by t he\ndonor like lack of awareness;\n(c) if so, the details thereof;\n(d) whether the Government has formulated/proposes to formulate any robust\nframework to facilitate organ transplant; and\n(e) if so, the details thereof and the steps/measures taken in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI MANSUKH MANDAVIYA)\n(a) to (e) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 165* FOR 30TH JULY, 2021\n(a) to (e) The Government of India has taken note of difficulties posed by shortage of\navailability of organs for transplantation in the country and has taken various measures\nto address the same.\nThe Government has enacted Transplantation of Human Organs (Amend ment)\nAct 2011 to amend the Transplantation of Human Organs Act (THOA), 1994 and\nnotified Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Rules 2014. The aforesaid Act\nand Rules have various provisions for increasing the pool of organ donors and to do\naway with legal complexities as well as non-uniform definitions.\nGovernment is implementing the National Organ Transplant Programme\n(NOTP) for augmenting organ donation and transplantation in the country. A three\nlevel framework of networking organisations with NOTTO at National level, Regional\nOrgan and Tissue Transplant Organisation (ROTTO) at regional level and State Organ\nand Tissue Transplant Organisation (SOTTO) at State level, has been set up for\nproviding an efficient and organized system of organ and tiss ue procurement from\ndeceased donors and their allocation to the waiting recipients.\nSo far, NOTTO at New Delhi, 5 ROTTOs (at Chandigarh, Mumbai, Chennai,\nKolkata and Guwahati) and 14 SOTTOs (in the State of Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, Goa,\nGujarat, Haryana, Himachal Pradesh, Jammu & Kashmir, Jharkhand, Kerala, Madhya\nPradesh, Odisha, Punjab, Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh) have been established.  As on date\n595 centres (503 transplant centres and 50 retrieval centre and 42 tissue banks) are\nlinked with the National Registry maintained by NOTTO.\nNOTP has various provisions which include:\n Financial assistance for setting up of State Organ and Tissue Transplant\nOrganisations (SOTTOs) in each State/UT.\n Financial assistance for setting up of Bio-material centres.\n Financial assistance for establishing new Organ Transplant/retrieval facilities\nand strengthening of existing Organ Transplant/retrieval facilities.\n Training to Manpower including surgeons, physicians, anesthetists, transplant\ncoordinators, etc.\n Financial assistanc e for hiring of Transplant Coordinators to medical colleges\nand trauma centres.\n Financial assistance for maintenance of bodies of deceased donor.\n\n Financial assistance to Below Poverty Line (BPL) transplant recipients, who\nhave undergone transplant in a Government Hospital, for post -transplant\nmedications.\nA National Bio -material Centre has been set up at NOTTO, New Delhi and a\nRegional Bio -material centre has been established at ROTTO, Tamil Nadu. Further,\nfunds have been released to the State of Bihar a nd Maharashtra for establishment of\nBio-material Centre. Funds have also been released to the State of Bihar, Punjab for\nestablishing new Organ Transplant/retrieval facilities and strengthening of existing\nOrgan Transplant/retrieval facilities.\nFurther, under Rastriya Arogya Nidhi, financial assistance is being provided to\npoor patients who are suffering from life threatening diseases relating to heart, kidney,\nliver, etc. for their treatment including respective organ transplantation at Government\nhospitals/institutes having super specialties facilities.\nAs far as creating awareness activities are concerned the NOTTO, ROTTOs and\nSOTTOs carry out awareness and disseminate relevant information about organ\ndonation to the public. A website www.notto.gov.in and a 24x7 call centre with a toll\nfree helpline number (1800114770) have been made operational. A number of\nactivities, for generating awareness are carried out from time to time. These include\ncelebration of Indian Organ Donation Day ev ery year, seminars, workshops, debates,\ndisplay boards on organ donation awareness in the hospitals, sports events,\nwalkathons, participation in marathons, nukkad natak, etc. Social media is also being\nused to cre"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |166", "t": "Global Gender Gap Report", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 166 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.07.2021 \n  \nGLOBAL GENDER GAP REPORT \n  \n*166. SHRI PARVESH SAHIB SINGH VERMA: \nSHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI: \n  \n     Will the Minister of WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:       \n  \n(a)  whether the Government has taken note of ranking of India in Global Gender Gap \nreport and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  the steps taken by the Government to bridge the gender gap in India; and \n(c)  the steps taken to improve health as well as economic and political participation of \nwomen in India? \n  \n  \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF WOMEN AND CHILD DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRIMATI SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n  \n  \n (a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n  \n***** \n  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (C) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 166 FOR 30.07.21 BY SHRI PARVESH SAHIB SINGH \nVERMA AND SHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI REGARDING ‘GLOBAL \nGENDER GAP REPORT’ \n                                   \n(a). Yes, Sir. As per the Global Gender Gap Report 2021 published by the World \nEconomic Forum, India is ranked at 140 out of 156 countries with a score of 0.625 ( out of \n1). The Global Gender Gap Report  provides the scores on Global Gender Gap Index \n(GGGI) which examines the gap between men and women in four dimensions, namely, \nEconomic Participation and Opportunity, Educational Attainment, Health and Survival and \nPolitical Empowerment.  According to the Global Gender Gap Report 2021, India has \nscored 0.326 in Economic Participation and Opportunity, 0.962 in Educational Attainment, \n0.937 in Health and Survival and 0.276 in Political Empowerment. \n  \n(b) and (c) Government of India has given utmost priority to bridge the gender gap in India, \nreducing disparity between men and women, improving the health of women, increasing \ntheir socio-economic status and participation in various fields. Some major initiatives taken \nby Government of India for removing the gender gap in all aspect of social, economic and \npolitical life are as follows: \n \n \n  \nEconomic Participation \n  \no Beti Bachao Beti Padhao ensures the protection, survival and education of \nthe girl child. \no Scheme for Adolescent Girls aims to empower girls in the age group 11 -18 \nand to improve their social status through nutrition, life skills, home skills and \nvocational training \no Working Women Hostel ensures the safety and security for working women. \no The National Crèche Scheme ens ures that women take up gainful \nemployment through providing a safe, secure and stimulating environment to \nthe children. \no radhan Mantri Awaas Yojana aims to provide housing under the name of \nthe       woman also. \no Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana aims to enable a large number of \nIndian youths including women to take up industry -relevant skill training in \nsecuring a better livelihood. \no Deen Dayal Upadhyay National Urban Livelihoods Mission focuses on \ncreating opportunities for women in skill development, lea ding to market -\nbased employment. \no Sukanya Samriddhi Yojna - Under this scheme girls have been economically \nempowered by opening their bank accounts. \no Skill Upgradation & Mahila Coir Yojna  is an exclusive training programme of \nMSME aimed at skill development of women artisans engaged in coir \nIndustry. \no ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������-linked \nsubsidy programme aimed at generating self -employment opportunities \nthrough establishment of micro-enterprises in the non-farm sector. \no Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana provides access to institutional finance to \nmicro/small business. \n  \n\n \n \nHealth and Nutrition \n  \no Government has announced  Mission Poshan 2.0  to strengthen nutritional content, \ndelivery, outreach and outcomes with focus on developing pr actices that nurture \nhealth, wellness and immunity to disease and malnutrition. \no Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandna Yojna aims to provide maternity benefit to pregnant \nand lactating mothers. \no Implementation of Reproductive, Maternal, Newborn, Child, Adolescent Heal th plus \nNutrition (RMNCAH+N) \no Rollout of Comprehensive Primary Health Care including health promotion through \nAyushman Bharat-Health & Wellness Centers (AB-HWC) \no Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakaram (JSSK) to eliminate out -of-pocket expenses \nfor pregnant women delivering in public health institutions and sick infants \naccessing public health institutions for treatment. \no Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY) to provide financial assistance to pregnant women \nfor encouraging institutional delivery. \no Surakshit Matritva Aashwasan (SUMAN) to provide assured, dignified, respectful \nand quality healthcare at no cost and zero tolerance for denial of services \no Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (PMSMA) to provide comprehensive \nand quality ANC to pregn"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|STARRED   |167", "t": "Ban on Single Use Plastic", "head": "��\n����������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��\n��������������������\n��������������������������������������������