to state\n:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has categorized the farmers into small, marginal, Below \nPoverty Line (BPL) and Other Backward Class (OBC) farmers and if so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether\n \nthe average size of agricultural land holdings in the country is small and \nmarginal and also un\n-\nremunerative to the farmers and if so, the details thereof, State\n-\nwise \nalong with the steps taken to improve productivity thereof; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether as per India \nRural Development Report, the income from the farming sector is \ninsufficient for 85 per cent farmers households in the country and if so, the reaction of \nGovernment thereto; \n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether any low cost effective technique has been developed by Agriculture \nResearch \nInstitutes for small land holdings and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nthe\n \ndetails of the steps taken/being taken for providing such technique and assistance \nto the small and marginal farmers and the measures being taken to make small land holdings \nmore feasible?\n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI NARENDRA  SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n \n(a) to (\ne\n):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (\ne\n) OF L\nOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n318\n   \nDUE FOR REPLY ON\n \n10\nTH\n \nAUGUST, 2021\n.\n \n \n(a):   \n \nIn \nAgriculture Census, the operational holdings are categorized in three social groups \nviz., Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes and Others and in five size classes as given \nbelow:\n \nS. No.\n \nCategory \n \nSize\n-\nClass\n \n1.\n \nMarginal \n \nBelow 1.00 hectare\n \n2.\n \nSmall \n \n1.00 \n-\n \n2.00 hectare\n \n3.\n \nSemi\n-\nMedium\n \n2.00 \n-\n \n4.00 hectare\n \n4.\n \nMedium \n \n4.00 \n-\n \n10.00 hectare\n \n5.\n \nLarge \n \n10.00 hectare and  above\n \n \n(b) & (c):  \n \n \nAs per latest Agriculture Census 2015\n-\n16, average size of the land holdings in \nthe country was 1.08 hectares. State\n-\nwise details of the average size of operational holdings \nare given at \nAnnexure\n. To enhance the remuneration of small and marginal farmers, \nGovernment is implementing a number of Schemes like Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchai \nYojana (PMKSY), Paramparagat  Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY), Soil Health Card (SHC), \nNeem Coated Urea, Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY), National Agriculture \nMarket Sche\nme (e\n-\nNAM), National Food Security Mission (NFSM), Mission for Integrated \nDevelopment of Horticulture (MIDH), Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana (RKVY), Scheme for \npromotion of 10,000 Farmer Producer Organizations (FPOs), Implementation of National Bee \nKeeping \nHoney Mission (NBHM), Interest Subvention Scheme by providing Kisan Credit \nCard(KCC), enactment of the Farmers Produce Trade and Commerce (Promotion & \nFacilitation) Act, 2020 and the Farmers (Empowerment & Protection) Agreement on Price \nAssurance and Farm \nServices Act, 2020. Also, One lakh crore rupees ‘Agri Infrastructure \nFund’ has been provided for post\n-\nharvest infrastructure for farmers.\n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n \n\n \n-\n2\n-\n \n(d):\n \n \nIndian Institute of Farming Systems Research (IIFSR) under Indian Council of \nAgricultural Resear\nch (ICAR) implements All India Coordinated Research Project (AICRP) on \nIntegrated Farming Systems (IFS) for development and refinement of farm technologies for \nuse of small and marginal farmers in the country. Details of major farm technologies \nevaluated i\nn farming system mode and refined are detailed below:\n \n\n \nDeveloped 60 tailor made prototype Integrated Farming System Models suitable to 25 \nStates/UTs.\n \n\n \nRefined 63 farming systems in 19 States.\n \n \n(e):\n \n \nThe Government has taken several measures to improve productivity to make small \nholdings more viable. These include promoting adoption of modern technologies and \npractices like multiple cropping, intercropping and integrated farming systems. Integrated \nFa\nrming Systems (IFS) models developed for the States have been included in the package \nof practices of respective state Governments. These are popularized among farmers, \nincluding small and marginal farmers, through farmer participatory refinement and \ndemon\nstration of integrated farming systems. The Indian Council of Agricultural Research \n(ICAR) is also conducting research programme to develop location specific varieties and \ntechnologies for enhancing the production and productivity of farm holdings. \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n \n \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 318 due for Answer on \n10.08.2021\n \nState\n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |319", "t": "Schemes for Fisheries and Dairy Farming", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHIERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HISBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 319 \nTO ANSWERED ON 10th July, 2021 \n \nINCREASE IN STRAY CATTLE \n \n*319. SHRI FEROZE VARUN GANDHI: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपाऱन, पशुपाऱन और डेयरी मंत्री \n be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the number of stray cattle has been on the increase recently; \n(b) if so, the details thereof from 2012 to 2020, State-wise including Uttar Pradesh; \n(c) whether the Government is planning to use stray cattle as surrogates to produce high variety \ncattle; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nHon’ble Minister of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying \n (Shri ParshottamRupala)  \n \n(a) to (d) Statement is laid on the table of the house  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY OF LOKSABHA STARRED QUESTION No.319 \nPART (a) to (d) ON INCREASE STRAY CATTLE \n \n(a)& (b) The number of Stray Cattle as per Livestock Census2012 and 2019State -wise \nincluding Uttar Pradesh given at Annexure-I \n(c) & (d) Government of India has been implementing Rashtriya Gokul Mission under which \nIn Vitro Fertilization(IVF) technology have been promoted for faster genetic upgradation of \nbovine population along with various other components of the scheme. Following steps has \nbeen undertaken under the scheme for promotion of IVF technology in the country: \n \n(i) Funds have been sanctioned for establishment of 31 IVF labs including 3 labs in Uttar \nPradesh and out of sanctioned 31 labs 17 labs have been made operational.   \n \n(ii) Funds have been released under the scheme for getting assured pregnancy in low \nproducing animals maintained by farmers through IVF technology. Incentive at the rate of Rs \n5000 per pregnancy is available under the  scheme for participating farmers and balance \namount is to be met by the concerned farmer. Unproductive cattle may be used as surrogate \nmother if they are fit for breeding. \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nState wise stray cattle population as per livestock census 2012 and 2019 \nAs per Livestock Census 2012 and 2019 \nSl. No. States/UTs Stray Cattle (Rural + Urban) \n  2012 2019 \n1 A&N Islands 3695 1459 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 42518 73850 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 77 659 \n4 Assam 22913 40608 \n5 Bihar 262349 85276 \n6 Chandigarh 1978 1440 \n7 Chhattisgarh 138122 184993 \n8 D&N Haveli 1029 0 \n9 Daman & Diu 245 1296 \n10 Delhi 12163 12163 \n11 Goa 7183 9459 \n12 Gujarat 292462 343918 \n13 Haryana 117209 127917 \n14 Himachal Pradesh 32160 36311 \n15 Jammu & Kashmir 8365 13864 \n16 Jharkhand 26042 32773 \n17 Karnataka 64758 77075 \n18 Kerala 4681 3629 \n19 Lakshadweep 280 0 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 437910 853971 \n21 Maharashtra 154487 152565 \n22 Manipur 42 2 \n23 Meghalaya 2410 2344 \n24 Mizoram 0 70 \n25 Nagaland 0 115 \n26 Odisha 1139051 151703 \n27 Puducherry 95 67 \n28 Punjab 100991 140069 \n29 Rajasthan 946050 1272277 \n30 Sikkim 0 157 \n31 Tamil Nadu 67256 51150 \n32 Telangana - 36366 \n33 Tripura 4146 3361 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 1009436 1184494 \n35 Uttarakhand 13504 27453 \n36 West Bengal 373860 98733 \n All India 5287767 5021587 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |32", "t": "Relief Package and Funds under Loan Gaurantee Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF EXPENDITURE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 32 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, 19th July, 2021/ Ashadha 28, 1943 (SAKA) \n \nRELIEF PACKAGE AND FUNDS UNDER LOAN GAURANTEE SCHEME \n \n32.  SHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA \n       SHRI PARBATBHAI SAVABHAI PATEL \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the amount of funds announced for relief package by the Ministry recently to \nstrengthen Indian economy to fight against Covid-19 pandemic; \n(b) the amount of funds announced under Loan Guarantee Scheme for Covid affected \nareas; and \n(c) the funds allocated or likely to be allocated for affected areas of Gujarat? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a)  to (c)  On 28th June, 2021, the Government had announced a package of Rs 6,28,993 \ncrore to provide economic relief to people and business affected by COVID-19 pandemic. \nThe measures announced in the package were sector specific with no State wise earmarking \nof funds. \n  \nRs 1.1 lakh crore was announced for Loan Guarantee Scheme for Covid affected \nareas. This includes Rs 50,000 crore for health sector and Rs 60,000 crore for other sectors. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |320", "t": "Welfare of Senior Citizens", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*320 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 10TH AUGUST, 2021/ SRAVANA 19, 1943 (SAKA) \n \nCENSUS 2021 \n \n*320.     DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether there will be any changes or any  additional information \nrequired in the 2021 Census as  compared to previous editions and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n \n(b) whether information obtained from the 2021 Census will be used for the \npreparation of any othe r databases, such as the National Register of \nCitizens and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether this will be disclosed to respondents  before obtaining the \ninformation for the Census and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether there will be significant delay in the  release of the information \nthat is collected and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) the estimated release dates of the different sets  of data that are \ncompiled for the Census? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.S.Q.NO.*320 FOR 10.08.2021 \n \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *320 FOR \n10.08.2021 REGARDING ‘CENSUS 2021’ \n \n(a): In Census, data is collected on demographic and various socio -\neconomic parameters like education, SC/ST, religion, language, marriage, \nfertility, disability, occupation and migration of the individuals.  The \nforthcoming Census is to be the first digital Census  and there is a \nprovision for self -enumeration.  Mobile App for collection of data and a \nCensus Portal for management a nd monitoring of various Census related \nactivities have been developed. The questionnaire for the first phase of \nCensus was notified in the Gazette of India. Due to the outbreak of COVID -\n19 pandemic, Census 2021 and other Census related field activities have \nbeen postponed until further orders.  \n \n(b) & (c): The individual data  collected in Census under the Census Act, \n1948 are not made public as per the provisions contained in the Act.  The \nindividual data are not used for the preparation of any other database \nincluding National Register of Citizens.  Only the aggregated Census  data \nat various administrative levels are released.   \n \n(d) & (e): Special emphasis is given on scientifically designing the \nCensus questions for faster processing and early release of data. \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |321", "t": "Promotion of Seaweed Cultivation", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY & DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 321 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20th JULY, 2021 \n \n \nPromotion of Seaweed Cultivation \n \n \n321: SHRIMATI KANIMOZHI KARUNANIDHI AND SHRI KHAGEN MURMU:  \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपाऱन, पशुपाऱन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether it is true that currently, seaweed production in India is primarily confined to the Gulf of \nMannar and Palk Bay in Tamil Nadu;  \n \n(b) whether the Government has  initiated the implementation of Multi -purpose Seaweed Park in the \nState of Tamil Nadu, as proposed in  the union budget 2021-22; and  \n \n(c) if so, the steps taken by the Government to encourage seaweed  cultivation at a large scale across \nseveral coastal States  in the country keeping in mind that seaweed farming has minimum capital , \ntechnological requirements and provides important economic opportunities to marginal coastal \ncommunities with limited  livelihood options; and  \n \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor ?   \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY & DAIRYING: \n \n(DR. L. MURUGAN) \n(a) Seaweed production has been presently undertaken in Gulf of Mannar and Palk Bay in Tamil \nNadu. However, Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Andaman &  Nicobar Islands and Dadra \nand Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu have also initiated steps for seaweed cultivation. \n(b) Yes, Sir.  \n(c) Projects to the tune of Rs.349.68 lakh were approved during 2015 -19 to Governments of \nAndhra Pradesh, Gujarat , Karnataka, Tamil Nadu and Andaman and Nicobar Administration for \nseaweed development under the erstwhile Centrally Sponsored Scheme (CSS) Blue Revolution: \nIntegrated Development and Management of Fisheries  Scheme. Besides, projects to the tune of \nRs.2477.76 lakh has also been approved for seaweed development under Pradhan Mantri Matsya \nSampada Yojana ( PMMSY) to Governments of Andhra Pradesh, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Tamil \nNadu and Union Territory Administrations of Andaman & Nicobar Islands as well as Dadra  & \nNagar Haveli and  Daman & Diu during 2020-21. \n(d) Does not arise.  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |322", "t": "Crime Against Children", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 322 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 20TH JULY, 2021/ ASHADHA 29,1943 (SAKA) \n \nCRIME AGAINST CHILDREN \n \n322. SHRI D.K.SURESH: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether it is fact that crimes against children are  increasing in the \ncountry; \n \n(b) if so, the details of the number of cases registered during the last three \nyears, State-wise and year-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government has conducted any  survey to find the  reasons \nfor increase in the crimes against children, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether the Government has taken any steps to  put an end to the \ncrimes against children in the country; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n \n \n(a) National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) compiles and publishes  \nstatistical data on crimes in its publication “Crime in India”. The published \nreports are available till the year 2019 . Analysis of this data for cases \nregistered under various crime heads on crimes against children does not \nshow any uniform trend. \n(b) Does not arise in view of reply to part (a) above. \n-2/...  \n \n\n-2- \nLS.US.Q.NO.322 FOR 20.07.2021 \n \n(c) to (e): „Police‟ and „Public Order‟ are State subjects under the Seventh \nSchedule to the Constitution of India. The responsibilities to maintain law \nand order, protection of life and property of the citizens including \ninvestigation and prosecution of crime including crimes against children rest \nwith the respective State Governments. The State Governments are \ncompetent to deal with such offences under the extant provisions of laws. \nHowever, Government accords utmost priority to the safety of children. \nThe Ministry of Home Affairs has issued various advisories from time to time \non steps to be taken for effective prevention, detection, registration, \ninvestigation and prosecution of all crimes against Children, measures \nneeded to prevent trafficking and to trace chil dren, filing of FIR in case of \nmissing children, kidnapping  of minors, tackling of cyber crime relating to \nobscene content against children and safety and security of children living in \nChild Care Institutions. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |323", "t": "ONORC in Aassam", "head": "GOVERNTENT OF INDIA\niilNlsTRY OF CONSUTER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTnIBUTION\nDEPARTUENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIG DISTRIBUTION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 323\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2OTXJULY' 2O2I\nONORC IN ASSAIU\n323. SHRI KRIPANATH IUALLAH!\nWill the Minister of GOI{SUilER AFFATRS' FOOD AND PUBLIG\nDIsrRlBUTton sqghEr arrd, uro aik grffio fut{q d* be pteased to state:\n(a) ore sallent features of the one Nation one Ration card (oNoRG) Scheme;\n(b) the ftrnds sanctioned, allocated and uttlized under this scheme wlthln Assamt\nso far;\n(c) the target set and achievoments made wathin Assarn' so fa4\n(d) the response received under thts schemo durlng covlD-'|g poraod wlthln the\ncountqr; and\n(e) the steps belng taken by the Government to cover the wader s.ctaon of target\nbenefictarles ln the $tate?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR iIItNtSTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPIf,ENT AND CONSUTER\nAFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION\nSADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI)(\n(a): Through technology-driven (lne Nation One\nNatlonal Food Securlty Act (NFSA) beneliciary\nRation Gard (ONORC) systemr any\ncan llft the Part of ful! monthlY\nentifled foodgalns, from any electronlc Point of sale (ePos) enabled Falr Prlce shop\n(FPs)Inthecountry-byusingthehsameexlstingrationcardorAadhaarnumbor.\nThetr famlty members back home, If eny, can also lift th. pa?t/balanca foodgralns on\nthe same ratlon card.\n(b): under the scheme on lntegrated Management of Public Dlstrlbution system\nililpos), the main oblectava of whlch is to implement the one Naflon one Ratlon\ncard (oiloRc) ptan in atl stetes/uTs, the Department has released a total ot Rs.\n88.8 Lakh to the state of Assarn out of totat prolect cost of Rs.2.'16 Crore. 3o tart\nthe State has not fumtshGd the utlllzation certlficate of the funds relcased and has\natso not clalmed further central assistance from the Department'\n(c):ttlsexpectedloimplementtheONORGplanlnAssaminnextfcwmonths'\ndependlng upon thc technlcal readlness lncludlng Aadhar gensratlon and seedlng of\ntho State Govemment to enable the ratlon cards portabillty. So far' the requislte\nePoS devlce3 are not Installed ln the FPSs of the State'\n(d):Durlngthecovtl}lgPerlodsofar(t.e.fromAprit2o2oloJune2o2{)'more\nthan2lcro]Gportabltityt]ansactlonshavebeenrecordedunde]theoNoRGplanin\nthe country.\n(e):TheDepartmentiscontlnuouslypuEulngw.ththeremalnlng4gtates/UTof\nA\"\"\"-,chhattlsgarh'DelhlandWestBengaltoenablet|reoNoRGplanforProvldlnga\nPan.tndiacove]ageofnatlonalPo]tablrityofratlonGardstoaboutallS0crore\nbeneflciaries in all States/UTs under NFSA'"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |324", "t": "National Rural Livelihood Mission", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 324 \n  ANSWERED ON 20/07/2021 \nNATIONAL RURAL LIVELIHOOD MISSION \n \n324.  SHRI JASWANT SINGH BHABHOR: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the main objective of the National Rural Livel ihood Mission; \n(b)  the number of people of Gujarat State likely t o be covered under this scheme; and \n(c)  whether the scheme will be implemented in the tribal areas of Gujarat also and if so, the \ndetails thereof?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n \n(a) Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana – National Rural Liv elihoods Mission (DAY-NRLM) is \nbeing implemented since 2011 in a mission mode with  the objective of organizing the \nrural poor women into Self Help Groups (SHGs), and continuously nurturing and \nsupporting them to take economic activities till th ey attain appreciable increase in \nincome over a period of time to improve their quali ty of life and come out of abject \npoverty. The programme aims to ensure that at least  one woman member from each \nrural poor household (about 9-10 crore) is brought into women SHGs and their \nfederations within a definite time frame. The progr amme is being implemented in all the \nStates and Union Territories, except Delhi and Chandigarh.  \n \n(b) In Gujarat, about 36.53 lakh rural households a re likely to be covered under DAY-\nNRLM by the financial year 2023-24. The identificat ion of households is based on the \nSocio Economic Caste Census (SECC) (deprived and au to-included households) data \nand households identified through the process of Pa rticipatory Identification of Poor \n(PIP). Gujarat State has mobilized 26.03 lakh rural  households under the Scheme till \n30 th  June 2021, i.e. 71% of the total targeted households. \n \n(c) DAY-NRLM is being implemented in all 248 blocks  of 33 districts that includes tribal \nareas of Gujarat. As on 30 th  June 2021, a total of 7,67,625 women from Schedule d Tribe \n(ST) households are mobilised into 73,191 Self Help Groups (SHGs). \n \n******* \n  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |325", "t": "Annapurna Scheme", "head": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 325 \n  ANSWERED ON 20/07/2021 \nANNAPURNA SCHEME \n \n325.  SHRI MAHESH SAHOO: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware about the non release of central allocation of rice and \nfunds under Annapurna Scheme to Odisha during the F Y 2019-20 which have been \ndistributed to the beneficiaries from the State pool; and \n(b)  the time which the Government would release th e central allocation of rice and fund to \nOdisha under the scheme during the FY 2019-20 and FY 2020-21?  \n \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n  \n \n(a)  & (b)  During 2019-20, the Ministry of Consumer Af fairs, Food and Public Distribution has \nallocated 3888.00 MT of rice to the State of Odisha  under Annapurna Scheme. An amount of \nRs.552.61 lakh has been released to the State under  this Scheme during the financial year 2019-\n20.  The State of Odisha has been requested to subm it the revised food grain Utilization \nCertificates in the prescribed format for allocatio n of food grains and release of funds for the \nyears 2019-20 and 2020-21.   \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |326", "t": "Bio-Toilets on Fishing Vessels", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY & DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 326 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20th JULY, 2021 \n \nBio-Toilets on Fishing Vessels \n \n326. DR. BEESETTI VENKATA SATYAVATHI: \n SHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL: \n \n Will the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY & DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपाऱन, पशुपाऱन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state : \n \n(a) whether the Government has mandated the fishing vessels to shift to bio -toilets to \nensure marine environment clean and contamination of marine resources is prevented;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether any financial assistance is being provided to vessel owners to install bio -\ntoilets; and  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \n ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY DAIRYING \n \n(Dr. L. MURUGAN) \n \n(a) to (d) : The endeavor of Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal \nHusbandry and Dairying, Government of India is to promote environment friendly marine \nfishing practices among the fishers. The installation of bio -toilets on mechanical fishing \nvessels is one such activity. Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal \nHusbandry and Dairying, Government of India under the Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada \nYojana (PMMSY) provides financial support for fitment of Bio toilets in mechanized fishing \nvessels for maintaining hygiene, prevention and spread of diseases, prevention of pollution of \noceans and seas and protection  of their ecology.   Under the PMMSY, the Department of \nFisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying , during the financial year \n2020-21, accorded approval to the proposals of seven States/UTs namely Andaman and \nNicobar Islands, Andhra Pradesh, Goa, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Odisha and Puducherry for \nfitment of 2755 Bio-toilets in mechanized fishing vessels at a total cost Rs.1377.50 lakh with \na central assistance of Rs. 395.24 lakh.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |327", "t": "Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act, 1960", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO: 327 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2021 \n \nPREVENTION OF CRUELTY TO ANIMALS ACT, 1960 \n \n327. SHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMALHUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपाऱन, पशुपाऱन और डेयरी मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has received petitionsseeking amendment of ‘The  Prevention \nof Cruelty toAnimals Act, 1960’ (PCA Act), if so, the detailsthereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government agrees with the opinionthat the penalties prescribed in the PCA \nAct are minimaland do not act as a deterrent; \n \n(c) whether the Government is considering to amendPCA Act to inflation correct and revise \nthe prescribed penalities; \n \n(d) whether the Government is considering to amendSection 11(1) (a) to 11(1) (o) and \nsection 38 of the Actwhich recognises the cognizability of offences under theAct; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasonstherefor? \n  \n ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FISHERIES ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(DR. SANJEEV KUMAR BALYAN) \n \n(a)  Yes. Sir.  The Government has received large number of suggestions, petitions and requests \nfrom various sections of the societies to increase the existing penalties in the Prevention of Cruelty \nto Animal Act, 1960. \n(b) Yes. Sir. \n(c) to (e) The need for amending the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Act, 1960 by introducing \nmore stringent penalties has been recognized by the Government. The draft amendment worked out \nincludes increasing monetary penalties and punishment provisions including amendment of Section \n11 and Section 38 of the Act. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |328", "t": "Medical Officers in Para-Military Forces", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 328 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 20TH JULY, 2021/ ASHADHA 29, 1943 (SAKA) \n \nMEDICAL OFFICERS IN PARA-MILITARY FORCES \n \n328.     ADV. A.M. ARIFF: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken any steps for recruiting to the posts \nof Specialist Medical Officers and  Medical Officers in Central Para -Military \nForces, namely,  Central Reserve Police Force, Border Security Force,  \nAssam Rifles, Sashastra Seema Bal and Indo-Tibetan Border Police. \n \n(b) if so, the details of the un -filled vacancies in the  above category as on \n15th February 2021 force-wise; and \n \n(c) whether the Government will take any steps to  expedite the \nrecruitment process by appointing candidates  from reserve rank list of \ncandidates before its expiry? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI)  \n(a) & (b):  Recruitment of Specialist and General Duty Medical Officers in \nCentral Armed Police Forces & Assam Rifles is being made regularly \nthrough Medical Officers Selection Board (MOSB).  \n \nThe force -wise details of the un -filled vacancies of Specialist Medical \nOfficers (Spl. MO) and General Duty Medical Officers (GDMOs) as on 15th \nFebruary, 2021 is as under:- \n \n\n \n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO.328 FOR 20.07.2021 \n \n \nForce Spl.MOs GDMOs \nAR 04 31 \nBSF 51 93 \nCRPF 112 85 \nITBP* 08 70 \nSSB 18 53 \n*ITBP Cadre includes Medical Officers of CISF also.  \n \nFurther, in the wake of Covid -19 pandemic, CRPF has been allowed to \nrecruit General Duty Medical Officers (GDMOs) on contract basis against \nvacant posts of Specialists Medical Officers for a period of one year. \n \n(c): Offers have already been given to the candidates in the reserve list of \nlast MOSB and many candidates have joined against the vacant posts. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |329", "t": "Allocation of Budget under MGNREGS", "head": "O.I.H.  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 329 \n  ANSWERED ON 20/07/2021 \nALLOCATION OF BUDGET UNDER MGNREGS \n \n329.  SHRI HANUMAN BENIWAL: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the budget allocated to Rajasthan by the Union  Government and utilization thereof under \nMahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) during \n2019-20, 2020-21 and till June, 2021 of the current financial year, the details thereof, \nitem wise; and \n(b)  the amount which remained unspent by the Rajas than Government out of the total budget \nallocated under MGNREGS during the aforesaid period and the reasons therefor for the \nsame?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n \n(a):    The Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employmen t Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS) is a demand driven wage employment programme  which provide for the enhancement \nof livelihood security of the households in rural a reas of the country by providing at least one \nhundred days of guaranteed wage employment in every financial year to every household whose \nadult members volunteer to do unskilled manual work . Being a demand driven programme, no \nState/UT-wise (including State of Rajasthan) financial allocation is made.  \n \n The details of funds released by Central Governmen t to the State of Rajasthan for the \nimplementation of Mahatma Gandhi NREG Scheme during  the last two financial years 2019-\n20, 2020-21 and the current FY 2021-22 (as on 30.06.2021) is given below: \n(Rs. In crore) \nIndicators 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \nCentral funds released 6891.74 9129.02 3271.79 \n \n(b):        As per information provided by State Go vernment of Rajasthan, the unspent balance \nwith State was Rs.1186.46 crore as on 31.03.2020 at  the end of financial year 2019-20, and an \namount of Rs. 214.87 crore was remaining unspent as on 31.03.2021 at the end of FY 2020-21.  \n \n******  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |33", "t": "Details of Schools/Colleges", "head": " \n \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK  SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *33 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2021 \n \n \nONLINE POLICIES \n \n*33. SHRI N.K. PREMACHANDRAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that the insurance companies are denying their liability of \nindemnifying the consumer on the basis of the online policies;  \n(b) if so, the action taken by the Government in this regard;  \n(c) whether no local or regional offices are  available for insurance companies and they are not \nresponding to the notices issued by the consumer redressal forum and if so, the action taken \nthereof;  \n(d)  whether the Government proposes legislation for making the insurance companies issuing \nonline policies liable and if so, the details thereof alongwith the action taken for ensuring the \nright of policyholders to get the claims; and  \n(e) whether the Government is aware that the private insurance companies escape from the \naction of consumer forum on flimsy grounds and if so, the action taken thereon? \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h  \n¼Jh ih;w’k xks;y½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n  (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) to (e)  :   A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO  (e) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.*33 FOR 20.07.2021 REGARDING ONLINE POLICIES. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \n \n(a) & (b)  : Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India (IRDAI) has informed that \nthey have not received any complaint again st any insurer alleging rejection of a claim in respect of \ninsurance policy bought online. \n(c)     :  IRDAI has informed that the Head Offices including regional offices of the Public Sector \nInsurance Companies   respond to the notices issued by the consume r redressal forums. There are \nalso local and regional offices of private insurance companies, but for the notices issued by the \nConsumer Commissions, the Head Offices of these insurance companies respond. \nFurther, IRDAI has also informed that they have not  received any complaint alleging that the \ninsurers had not responded to the notices of consumer forums. However, in case any insurer does not \nrespond to a legal notice they are liable to legal consequences. \n(d) & (e) :  IRDAI has informed that no insurer can sell any insurance policy including online \npolicies without the approval of IRDAI and as such no separate legal framework is required to \nensure viability of online insurance products.   The terms and conditions of all  policies are also \napproved by IRDAI, so as to ensure that rights of policyholders are protected.  Consumer \nCommissions are quasi- judicial bodies that decide and resolve consumer complaints including those \nrelated to insurance claims. \n  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |330", "t": "Small and Marginal Farmers", "head": "1\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULT\nURE AND FARMERS WELF\nARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICU\nLTURE, COOPERATION A\nND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n330\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n20\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2021\n \nSMALL AND MARGINAL F\nARMERS\n \n \n330\n. \nDR. ALOK KUMAR SUMAN\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to \nState:\n \n(a) \n \nW\nhether it is fact that the Government has initiated any specific programme to help \nsmall and marginal farmers in the country including district Gopalganj of Bihar; \n \n(b) \n \nI\nf so, the number of such farmers covered under such schemes in the last two years, \nSt\nate and year\n-\nwise; and \n \n(c) \n \nT\nhe details of measures taken to mitigate the sufferings of small and marginal farmers \nin the country including Gopalganj district of Bihar?\n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE \nAND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n   \n(