e to COVID -19 pandemic lockdown in the country, had \nissued advisory to State Governments/Union Territories(UTs) to include fish and shrimp in the list of \nexempted commodities during lockdown and also allow movement of fish and fish products, fish seed \n& fish feed for fish -farmers/fishers, aquaculture farms and other commercial establishments like \naquarium shop etc. Besides, the Guidelines issued on 10th April, 2020 by the Ministry of Home Affairs \nalso allowed operations of the fishing(marine)/aquaculture industry including feeding & maintenance, \nharvesting, processing, packaging, cold chain, sale and marketing, hatcheries, feed plants, commercial \naquaria, movement of fish/shrim p and fish products, seed/feed and workers , during the lockdown \nperiod.  \n Besides, the Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying is \nimplementing a flagship scheme namely “Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) - to \nbring about Blue Revolution through sustainable and responsible development of fisheri es sector in \nIndia” with an estimated investment of Rs. 20,050 crore  for a period of 5 years  from financial year \n2020-21 to FY 2024 -25 in all States/Union Territories as a part of Aatma Nirbhar Bharat Package.  \nWhile enhancing fish production and productivi ty, PMMSY also aims to create huge employment \nopportunities both direct and indirect in fisheries and aquaculture sector. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |332", "t": "Panchayati Raj System in Assam", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 332  \nANSWERED ON 20.07.2021 \n  \nPANCHAYATI RAJ SYSTEM IN ASSAM \n \n 332.        SHRI ABDUL KHALEQUE: \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n(a)          The details of various schemes available under the Panchayati Raj system in the State \nof Assam particularly in the districts of Barpeta and Bongaigaon; and \n (b)         the total funds allocated during the last  five financial years and fund ut ilized so far, \nyear-wise the details thereof?     \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PANCHAYATI RAJ \n(SHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL) \n \n(a) The Ministry of Panchayati Raj (M oPR) implements the Centrally Sponsored Scheme -\nRashtriya Gram Swaraj Abhiyan (RGSA) and Incentivisation of Panchayats (IoP) , a Central \ncomponent of RGSA in all States including the State of Assam and its districts of Barpeta and \nBonaigaon for Panchayati Raj Institutions (PRIs) for strengthening the PRIs and incentivisation \nfor their developmental works.  \n \n(b) No prior allocation of funds is made to any States under the Schemes of Ministry of \nPanchayati Raj. The funds under the Scheme RGSA is released based on the annual action plan \nsubmitted by the State concerned including the State of Assam and funds under the scheme of \nIncentivisation of Panchyats is released to the best performing Panchayats selecte d through a \nnational competition for the National Panchayat Awards of various categories. A total of \nRs.172.17 crore  of grants -in-aid was released to the State of Assam under the two above \nschemes during the last five years, out of which Rs.1.54 crore remains unutilised. The year -\nwise releases under the two schemes is as under:   \n   \n Year Releases under RGSA \nScheme (Rs. in crore) \n   Releases under IoP Scheme \n(Rs. in crore) \n2016-17* 49.08 1.72 \n2017-18* 27.59 1.37 \n2018-19 39.21 1.30 \n2019-20 23.22 0.97 \n2020-21 26.12 1.59 \nTotal 165.22 6.95 \n*Capacity Building-Panchayat Sashaktikaran Abhiyan (CB-PSA) \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |333", "t": "Impact of Pandemic on Direct Agricultural Marketing", "head": "O.I.H\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n333\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n20\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2021\n \n \nIMPACT OF PANDEMIC ON DIRECT AGRICULTURAL\n \nMARKETING\n \n333\n.  \nSHRI CHUNNI LAL SAHU:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS\n \n \nWELFARE   \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n  \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether direct agricultural marketing was\n \nadversely affected during the \n \nlockdown period in the\n \nyear \n2020 due to the COVID\n-\n19 pandemic;\n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof; and\n \n \n(c) \n \nthe details of the number of farmers linked to the\n \nKisan Rath Mobile app so far \n \nand the number of farmers\n \nbenefited, State\n-\nwise?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS\n \nWELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH\n \nTOMAR)\n \n \n(a) & (b): \n \nNo Sir.  In view of the above, the question does not arise. \n \n(c):\n \n \nA \ntotal of 4,85,103 farmers are registered on the Kisan Rath Mobile \nApplication. A farmer linked to Kisan \nRath may benefit through various options \navailable on the app such as posting load, receiving response against these loads, \nviewing partial loads nearby, viewing service providers nearby.\n \n \nHowever, t\nhe state\n-\nwise status of registration of farmers on Kisan \nrath\n \nat Annexure\n-\nI.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nS.No.\n \nStates/Uts\n \nNo. of Farmers registered on Kisan Rath\n \n1\n \nAnd\na\nman and Nicobar Island\n \n21\n \n2\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n18728\n \n3\n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n224\n \n4\n \nAssam\n \n19060\n \n5\n \nBihar\n \n36811\n \n6\n \nChandigarh\n \n42\n \n7\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n8066\n \n8\n \nDadra\n \nand Nagar Haveli and\n \n \nDaman and Diu\n \n45\n \n9\n \nDelhi\n \n438\n \n10\n \nGoa\n \n69\n \n11\n \nGujarat\n \n16443\n \n12\n \nHaryana\n \n27565\n \n13\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n2655\n \n14\n \nJammu and Kashmir\n \n2156\n \n15\n \nJharkhand\n \n13451\n \n16\n \nKarnataka\n \n17200\n \n17\n \nKerala\n \n2420\n \n18\n \nLadakh\n \n20\n \n19\n \nLakshadweep\n \n8\n \n20\n \nMadhya \nPradesh\n \n37387\n \n21\n \nMaharashtra\n \n50401\n \n22\n \nManipur\n \n1369\n \n23\n \nMeghalaya\n \n284\n \n24\n \nMizoram\n \n778\n \n25\n \nNagaland\n \n1054\n \n\n26\n \nOdisha\n \n8082\n \n27\n \nPuducherry\n \n64\n \n28\n \nPunjab\n \n14769\n \n29\n \nRajasthan\n \n43888\n \n30\n \nSikkim\n \n219\n \n31\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n15047\n \n32\n \nTelangana\n \n12456\n \n33\n \nTripura\n \n295\n \n34\n \nUttarakhand\n \n2701\n \n35\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n114112\n \n36\n \nWest Bengal\n \n16775\n \n \n \nTotal\n \n485103\n \n*****\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |334", "t": "Wastage of Food Grain", "head": " \n \n \n \n \n \nORIGINAL IN HINDI \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 334 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2021 \n \nWASTAGE OF FOOD GRAIN \n \n334. SHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE:  \n(OIH) \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether according to an estimate food products worth Rs. 92,600 crores g ot exterminated \nevery year during the harvesting of major crops and after that similarly loss pertaining to fruits \nand vegetables is of around Rs. 40,800 crores;  \n(b)  if so, the budget allocated by the Government during the years 2017, 2018, 2019, 2020,20 21 \nfor preconditioning, pre -cooling, processing of fruits and vegetables, packing and labelling \nfacility cold chain for the storage of perishable fruits and vegetables and to develop rapid \ntransportation system and the funds spent thereon; and  \n(c)  the details thereof, State-wise including Maharashtra? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k jkT; ea=h  \n¼Jh vf”ouh dqekj pkScs½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n \n(a)     :  As per a study conducted by ICAR -CIPHET, Ludhiana on the extent of harvest and post - \nharvest losses of major agricultural produce in 2015, the estimated annual value of harvest and post -\nharvest losses of major agricultural produce at national level was Rs.  92,651 crore using production \ndata of 2012 -13 at 2014 wholesale prices. Similarly, the estimated loss for fruits was Rs. 16,644 \ncrores and for vegetables was Rs.14,842 crores. \n  \n\n \n(b) & (c)  : In order to minimize the post -harvest losses and enhance value a ddition in agricultural \nproduce, Ministry of Food Processing Industries has been implementing the Scheme for Integrated \nCold Chain and Value Addition Infrastructure since 2008. The Scheme provides for promoting \nintegrated cold chain facilities from the far m gate to the consumer, end to end, in order to reduce \nlosses by improving efficiency in the collection of farm produce, its storage, transportation and \nminimal processing.  Both horticultural and non -horticultural produce are eligible for support under \nthis scheme. \n \nYear-wise budget allocation and expenditure under the scheme is as follows: \nYear-wise Budget Allocation during the year from 2017-18 to 2021-22 \n(Rs. in crore) \nYear Revised  Estimate Expenditure \n2017-18 198.68 196.15 \n2018-19 271.59 244.74 \n2019-20 326.16 271.12 \n2020-21 252.58 207.40 \n2021-22 227.60 \n(Budget Estimate) \n56.00 \n  \nThe State -wise details of projects approved, completed and ongoing, including Maharashtra, as on \n30.06.2021 is given at Annexure. \n \n***** \n  \n  \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (b)&(c) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO.334 FOR 20.07.2021 REGARDING WASTAGE OF FOOD GRAIN. \n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \nState/UT-wise Projects details under Cold Chain Scheme [as on 30.06.2021] \n  States/ \nUTs Approved Completed Ongoing \nProject \nCost \n(Rs cr.) \nApproved \nGrant           \n(Rs cr.) \nReleased \nGrant                    \n(Rs cr.) \nInvestment \nLeveraged        \n(Rs cr.) \nEmployment \nGenerated \nFarmer \nBenefited \n1 Maharashtra 62 52 10 1,658.31 430.93 376.61 1,227.38 37,200 592,224 \n2 Uttarakhand 30 22 8 541.82 277.32 201.71 264.50 18,000 286,560 \n3 Andhra  Pradesh 28 8 10 1,179.60 242.04 94.80 937.56 16,800 267,456 \n4 Gujarat 28 19 9 1,098.15 232.31 149.82 865.84 16,800 267,456 \n5 Uttar  Pradesh 26 17 9 646.47 202.00 143.44 444.47 15,600 248,352 \n6 Punjab 23 16 7 464.34 147.89 110.02 316.45 13,800 219,696 \n7 Haryana 19 10 9 498.47 141.38 69.76 357.09 11,400 181,488 \n8 Tamil   Nadu 17 10 7 523.97 117.01 70.80 406.96 10,200 162,384 \n9 Himachal \nPradesh 16 11 5 371.27 139.07 99.69 232.20 9,600 152,832 \n10 Karnataka 16 11 5 430.83 110.97 73.18 319.86 9,600 152,832 \n11 Rajasthan 15 11 4 348.77 94.21 65.90 254.56 9,000 143,280 \n12 West  Bengal 13 8 5 358.51 89.65 62.62 268.86 7,800 124,176 \n13 Madhya  \nPradesh 12 6 6 277.27 92.19 41.19 185.08 7,200 114,624 \n14 Telangana 12 5 7 427.01 97.71 54.86 329.30 7,200 114,624 \n15 Jammu & \nKashmir 7 6 1 111.83 53.23 37.98 58.60 4,200 66,864 \n16 Bihar 6 3 3 151.59 47.19 30.64 104.40 3,600 57,312 \n17 Kerala 6 3 3 168.14 42.35 13.66 125.79 3,600 57,312 \n18 Odisha 4 2 2 193.10 32.08 18.58 161.02 2,400 38,208 \n19 Nagaland 3 1 2 47.11 22.39 6.98 24.72 1,800 28,656 \n20 Andaman   & \nNicobar 2 1 1 24.95 12.45 2.00 12.50 1,200 19,104 \n21 Assam 2 2 0 38.28 17.37 17.37 20.91 1,200 19,104 \n22 Chhattisgarh 2 2 0 39.33 11.52 11.52 27.81 1,200 19,104 \n23 Mizoram 2 2 0 22.46 12.77 12.77 9.69 1,200 19,104 \n24 Arunachal \nPradesh 1 1 0 15.32 6.46 5.28 8.86 600 9,552 \n25 Manipur 1 1 0 18.49 9.96 9.96 8.53 600 9,552 \n  Total 353 230 113 9,655.39 2,"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |335", "t": "Employment under Mega Food Park Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 335 \nANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2021 \n  \n EMPLOYMENT UNDER MEGA FOOD PARK SCHEME \n  \n335. SHRI DHARAMBIR SINGH: \n                        \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the number of employment opportunities likely to be generated under the Mega Food Park \nScheme, and the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government have any scheme to set up Mega fo od Park in Bhiwani -\nMahendragarh-Charkhi Dadri districts of Haryana, if so, the time by when it is likely to be \nset up and the details thereof; and \n \n(c) whether it is in the cognizance of the Government that the Bhiwani and Charkhi Dadri  \ndistricts have the highest processing potential of green vegetables and if so, the details \nthereof? \n  \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES  \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a). As per Mega Food Park Scheme Guidelines, each Mega Food Park on being fully \noperational, would create direct and indirect employment of about 5000 persons. However, the \nactual configuration of a project may vary depending upon the business plan. Ministry h as \naccorded final approval to 38 Mega Food Parks and in -principle approval to 3 Mega Food Parks \nin the country under the Mega Food Park scheme. About 6,66,000 direct and indirect employment \nhave been generated by the 22 operational Mega Food Parks. \n   \n(b). Ministry of Food Processing Industries has been implementing Mega Food Park Scheme \n(MFPS) to create modern infrastructure for the food processing sector. The proposals under \nscheme for setting up of Mega Food Parks in the country are invited through Expression of Interest. \nThe scheme is not State or Area specific.  So far Ministry has approved 2 Mega Food Parks in \nSonipat and Rohtak districts of Haryana being implemented by State government Agencies \n(HSIIDC and HAFED). There is no proposal with the Ministry  for setting up of Mega Food Park \nin Bhiwani-Mahendragarh-Charkhi Dadri districts of Haryana. \n \n(c). Ministry of Food Processing Industries does not maintain district wise data for processing \npotential for various products in the country. \n  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |336", "t": "Amendment of Consumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules, 2020", "head": " \n \n \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 336 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2021 \n \nAMENDMENT OF CONSUMER PROTECTION (E-COMMERCE) RULES, 2020 \n \n336. SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR BELLANA:  \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is planning to amend the Consumer Protection (E-commerce) Rules, 2020; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof along with the reasons due to which the Government is ammending the \nEcommerce Rules;  \n(c)  whether the Government seeks to ban specific flash sales or back -to-back sales that limit customers \nchoice, increase prices and distorts the level playing field; and  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k jkT; ea=h  \n¼Jh vf”ouh dqekj pkScs½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) to (d) :  The Consumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules, 2020 were notified on 23 rd July, 2020. In order \nto further strengthen the regulatory framework for prevention of unfair trade practices in e -commerce, \nCentral Government has sought views/ comments/ suggestions on the proposed amendments \nto the Rules by placing it on the website of the Department of Consumer Affairs. The proposed \namendments also include provisions related to flash sales. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |337", "t": "Farmers Agitation and MSP", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n337\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n20\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2021\n \n \nFARMERS AGITATION AND MSP\n \n337. SHRI ASADUDDIN OWAISI:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS\n \n \nWELFARE   \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n  \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the farmers agitation is continuing and there is no talk between the \n \nGovernment and farmers on the three laws passed by the \nParliament;\n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the reasons therefor;\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government has hiked Kharif MSP;\n \n \n(d) \n \nif so, the percentage increase in most Kharif crops;\n \n \n(e) \n \nthe other steps taken or being taken by the Government for the welfare of \n \nfarmers; and\n \n \n(f) \n \nthe steps taken by the Government to invite farmers for talks to address their \n \ngrievances?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS\n \nWELFARE\n \n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH\n \nTOMAR)\n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nSome of the farmers’ unions have been \nprotesting against newly enacted \nFarm laws and their main demands of agitating farmers’ unions regarding the three farm \nlaws, were for their repeal viz. “The Farmers' Produce Trade and Commerce (Promotion \nand Facilitation) Act, 2020”, “The Farmers (Empower\nment and Protection) Agreement \nOn Price Assurance and Farm Services Act, 2020” & “The Essential Commodities \n(Amendment) Act, 2020”. Various efforts have been made by Government to end the \nprotests.\n \n \nGovernment engaged itself actively and constantly with th\ne agitating farmers \nunions to end the protests and so far, 11 rounds of discussions/talks were held between \nthe Government & agitating farmers unions to resolve the issues. Details of the meetings \nare at \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n.\n \n \n \nGiving top priority to the interest of\n \nfarmers, Government vide letter dated 09\nth\n \nDecember, 2020 addressed to Farmer Unions, explained point\n \n \nby\n \n \npoint,\n \n \nhow\n \n \nthe \nconcerns expressed by the farmers were being taken care of in the proposal of the \n\n \n \nGovernment of India.\n \n \nGovernment also proposed to\n \nconstitute a small group consisting \nwith representative of farmers’ unions, which can deliberate on Farm Laws clause\n-\nwise \nin a time bound manner and can also consider other issues related to farmers, which \nrepresentatives of farmers’ unions have not agree\nd.\n \n \nHowever, Farmer Unions never agreed to discuss the farm laws excepting for \ndemanding their repeal.\n \n \nGovernment is always open to discussion with farmers Union and will remain \nopen to discussion with agitating farmers to resolve the issue. \n \n(c) & (d): \n \nAs reported by data provided by \nCommission for Agricultural Costs and \nPrices(CACP), the details is at \nAnnexure\n-\nII.\n \n \n(e):\n \n \nThe Government has adopted several developmental programmes, schemes, \nreforms and policies that focus on higher incomes for the farmer\ns. All these policies & \nprogrammes are being supported by higher budgetary allocations, non\n-\nbudgetary \nfinancial resources by way of creating Corpus Funds like Micro Irrigation Fund and Agri\n-\nmarketing scheme to strengthen eNAM and GrAMs, as also in the Mini\nstry of Animal \nHusbandry, Dairy and Fisheries to promote dairy and fishery sectors. There have been \nseveral reforms to unleash the potential and these include Market Reforms like Model \nAPLM (Promotion & Facilitation) Act, 2017; Establishment of Gramin Agri\nculture Markets \n(GrAMs); Agri\n-\nExport Policy, 2018; The Farmers Produce Trade and Commerce \n(Promotion & Facilitation) Act, 2020; The Farmers (Empowerment & Protection) \nAgreement on Price Assurance and Farm Services Act.\n \n \n2020; Amendments to Essential \nCommod\nities Act, 1955; Promotion of 10,000 FPOs with necessary financial support \nunder Atma Nirbhar Package (Agriculture)and Supplementary Income transfers under \nPM\n-\nKISAN; Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojna (PMFBY); Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchai \nYojana (PMKSY); Inc\nrease in Minimum Support Price (MSPs) for all Kharif & Rabi Crops \nensuring a minimum of 50 percent of profit margin on the cost of production; Har Med \nPar Ped; Bee\n-\nKeeping; RashtriyaGokul Mission; Blue Revolution; Interest Subvention \nScheme; Kisan Credit C\nard (KCC) that now offers production loan to even dairy & \nfishery farmers besides agricultural crops etc.\n \n \nThe latest major intervention includes the ‘Atma Nirbhar Bharat \n–\n \nAgriculture’ \nwhich includes comprehensive market reforms and creation of ‘Agricultu\nral Infrastructure \nFund (AIF)’ worth Rs. 1 lakh crores. This Fund is in synch with the needed \ntransformation of the agriculture sector, particularly at the post\n-\nharvest management \nstage. Additionally, separate allocations have been made in favour of the Mi\nnistry of \nAnimal Husbandry, Dairy & Fisheries, as also the Ministry of Food Processing Industries. \nThese market reforms and infrastructure funds are expected to "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |338", "t": "One District One Focus Product Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUN\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n3\n38\n \nTO BE ANSWER\nED\n \nON THE \n20\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2021  \n \nONE DISTRICT ONE \nFOCUS \nPRODUCT\n \nSCHEME\n \n3\n38\n.\n \nSHRI\n \nACHYUTANANDA SAMANTA\n:\n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n(\na\n)\n \nthe details of the One District One Focus Product Scheme, and the agricultural \nproducts chosen district\n-\nwise across the\n \ncountry, especially in the State of Odisha\n;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nthe \ndetails of the support to be provided to selected crops/products by the \nCentral Government;  \n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether the products selected under this scheme \nespecially from the State of \nOdisha \nwill be integrated into the e\nxport value chain\n;\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nif so, the details and timeframe thereof\n;\n \nand\n \n(\ne\n)\n \nif not, the reasons therefor?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \n& (b)\n:\n \nThe One District One Focus Produc\nt \n(ODOFP)\n \nprogramme cover\n \nproducts \nof agriculture and allied se\nctors for 728 districts of the \ncountry. The district\n-\nwise \nand \nstate\n-\nwise \nproducts \nis\n \nAnnexed. \nThe Government has decided to c\nonverge \nresources from ongoing centrally sponsored schemes such as Mission for Integrated \nDevelopment of Horticulture (MIDH), National Food Security Mission (NFSM), \nRashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana (RKVY), Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana (PKVY), \nschemes of M\ninistry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying\n \nfor ODOFP\n.\n \n \nThe\n \nMinistry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI) \nprovides financial support \n \nunder Centrally Sponsored Pradhan Mantri Formalisation of Micro Food Processing \nEnte\nrprises Scheme (PM FME Scheme\n)\n \nfor\n \nOne District One\n \nFocus\n \nProduct\n. PM \nFME\n \nprovides financial, technical and business support for upgradation of existing \n\nmicro food processing enterprises\n \netc\n.\n \nThe scheme a\ndopts \nOne District One Product \n(\nODOP\n)\n \napproach to reap the benefits of scale in terms of procurement of inputs, \navailing common services and marketing of products. \n \n \n(c) \nto (\nd\n)\n: \nThe identified products \nhave potential for both domestic demand and \nexport\n.\n \nPM FME\n \nscheme envisages strengthening backward and forward linkages, \nprovision of common facilities, incubation centres, training, research and \ndevelopment (R&D), marketing and branding.\n \nThe enhanced capacity of processing \nand value addition in agriculture and a\nllied sector products \nis for \nbetter\n \nprice \nrealization \nby\n \nthe farmers.\n \nThis scheme is being implemented for a period of five \nyears from 2020\n-\n21 to 2024\n-\n25\n.\n \n \nT\nhe State of Odisha\n,\n \nunder the supervision of\n \nAgricultural and Processed Food \nProducts Export Development Authority \n(\nAPEDA\n), has prepared Export Policy which \nenvisages integration of ODOFP/ODOP products into export value chain.\n \n \n \n(\ne\n)\n \nQuestion does not arise. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n \nReferred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. \n338 \nfor 20.07.2021 regarding ‘\nOne District One \nFocus \nProduct\n \nScheme\n’\n \nDistrict\n-\nwise & State\n-\nwise list of products: \n \n \nAndaman & Nicobar Islands (3)\n \nS.No \n(Dists)\n \nDistricts\n \nProduct\n \n1.\n \nNicobar\n \nCoconut\n \n2.\n \nNorth & Middle \nAndaman \n \nCoconut\n \n3.\n \nSouth Andaman\n \nMarine fisheries\n \n \nAndhra Pradesh (13)\n \n1.\n \nAnanthapuram\n \nGroundnut\n \n8.\n \nNellore\n \nCitrus\n \n2.\n \nChittoor\n \nTomato\n \n9.\n \nPrakasam\n \nSpices (Chilli & Turmeric)\n \n3.\n \nEast Godavari\n \nCoconut\n \n10.\n \nSrikakulam\n \nCashew nut\n \n4.\n \nGuntur\n \nSpices (Chilli & \nTurmeric)\n \n11.\n \nVisakhapatnam\n \nSugarcane\n \n5.\n \nKadapa\n \nBanana\n \n12.\n \nVizianagaram\n \nMango\n \n6.\n \nKrishna\n \nMango\n \n13.\n \nWest Godavari\n \nAqua\n \n7.\n \nKurnool\n \nOnion\n \n \n \n \n \n\nArunachal Pradesh (2\n6\n)\n \n1.\n \nAnjaw\n \nLarge Cardamom\n \n14.\n \nLower Subansiri\n \nKiwi\n \n2.\n \nChanglang\n \nArecanut\n \n15.\n \nNamsai\n \nGinger\n \n3.\n \nDibang valley\n \nKiwi\n \n16.\n \nPakke Kesang\n \nPaddy\n \n4.\n \nEast Kameng\n \nOrange\n \n17.\n \nPapum Pare (\nCapital \ncomplex\n)\n \nTurmeric\n \n5.\n \nEast Siang (Pasighat)\n \nOrange/Mandarin\n \n18.\n \nPapum Pare (Yupia)\n \nTurmeric\n \n6.\n \nKamle\n \nOrange \n \n19.\n \nShi\n-\nyomi\n \nKiwi\n \n7.\n \nKra\n-\nDaadi\n \nL. Cardamom\n \n20.\n \nSiang\n \nLarge Cardamom\n \n8.\n \nKurung Kumey\n \nL. Cardamom\n \n21.\n \nTawang\n \nWalnut\n \n9.\n \nLepa Rada\n \nPickles\n \n22.\n \nTirap\n \nMillets\n \n10.\n \nLohit\n \nSesamum\n \n23.\n \nUpper Siang\n \nOrange\n \n11.\n \nLongding\n \nGinger\n \n24.\n \nUpper Subansiri\n \nOrange\n \n12.\n \nLower Dibang\n \nvalley\n \nTurmeric\n \n25.\n \nWest Kameng\n \nApple\n \n13.\n \nLower Siang\n \nPineapple\n \n26.\n \nWest Siang \n \nPineapple\n \n \nAssam (33)\n \n1\n.\n \n \nBaksa\n \nHoney\n \n18.\n \nKamroop Metro\n \nFruits and Vegetable \n(pickles)\n \n2\n.\n \n \nBarpeta\n \nMilk Products\n \n19.\n \nKamroop Rural\n \nBanana\n \n3\n.\n \n \nBiswanath Charali\n \nPotato\n \n20.\n \nKarbi \nAnglong\n \nGinger\n \n\n4\n.\n \n \nBongaiagoan\n \nTurmeric\n \n21.\n \nKarimga"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |339", "t": "PMGSY in Tamil Nadu", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \n UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 339 \n  ANSWERED ON 20/07/2021 \nPMGSY IN TAMIL NADU \n \n339.  SHRI S. VENKATESAN: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of colonies connected so far under Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana \n(PMGSY) in the country, along with the district-wise details in respect of Tamil Nadu \nincluding Madurai; and \n(b)  the number of roads identified under PMGSY dur ing the said period?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n \n(a)& (b) Rural Roads is a State subject. Pradhan Ma ntri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) \nwas launched as a one-time special intervention to support enhancement of rural connectivity, by \nway of a single all-weather road, to the eligible unconnected habitations of designated population \nsize (500+ in plain areas and 250+ in North-Eastern  States, Himalayan States and Himalayan \nUnion Territories as per 2001 census) in the core n etwork for uplifting the socio-economic \ncondition of the rural population. Relaxation has b een provided to the Tribal (Schedule V) areas \nand Selected Tribal and Backward Districts (as iden tified by the Ministry of Home Affairs \n(MHA) and Planning Commission), and unconnected habitations in these areas with a population \nof 250 persons and above in the Core Network as per  Census 2001 are eligible for connectivity \nunder the scheme. In the critical Left Wing Extremi sm affected blocks (as identified by Ministry \nof Home Affairs), additional relaxation has been gi ven to connect habitations with population \n100 persons and above as per 2001 census.   \n        \nThe unit for PMGSY programme is habitation. Against  1,78,184 habitations of the population \nsize 250+ and 500+ identified for coverage under PM GSY, 16,086 habitations have been \nprovided connectivity by the States/UTs out of thei r own resources and 4,716  habitations have \neither been dropped or have not been found feasible . Out of the balance 1,57,382  habitations \nsanctioned for providing connectivity under the PMG SY, 1,55,245 habitations have already been \nprovided connectivity. In the State of Tamilnadu, 1 ,985 habitations of 500+ and 1,000+ \npopulation size were sanctioned for coverage under PMGSY and all the habitations have already \nbeen connected. The district-wise details of habita tions sanctioned and connected in the State of \nTamilnadu, including Madurai are attached as Annexure . \n \nThe mandate of PMGSY has been subsequently widened to include new interventions.  PMGSY-\nII was launched in the year 2013, with a target to upgrade 50,000 Kms  of the existing rural road \nnetwork to improve its overall efficiency as a prov ider of transportation services for people, \ngoods and services.  Road Connectivity Project for Left Wing Extremism Affected Areas \n(RCPLWEA) was launched in the year 2016 for constru ction/upgradation of strategically \n\nimportant roads chosen in the 44 worst affected LWE  districts and adjoining districts in the 9 \nStates of Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, Chhattisgarh, Jhar khand, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, \nOdisha, Telangana and Uttar Pradesh. PMGSY-III was launched in the year 2019 for \nconsolidation of 1,25,000 Km Through Routes and Maj or Rural Links connecting \nhabitations,  inter-alia, to Gramin Agricultural Markets (GrAMs), Higher Secondary Schools and \nHospitals.   \n \nThe status of implementation of various interventio ns/verticals of PMGSY across the country \nand in the Tamilnadu State and Madurai district as on 15 \nth  July, 2021 is as under:- \n \nOverall status in the country \n \nRoad length in Km \nName of intervention  Sanctioned Completed Balance* \nNumber \nof roads \nRoad \nlength \nNumber \nof roads \nRoad \nlength \nNumber \nof roads \nRoad \nlength \nPMGSY-I 1,64,816  6,45,644  1,58,305  6,07,871  6,511  22,710  \nPMGSY-II 6,708  49,904  5,206  44,340  1,502  5,124  \nRCPLWEA** 894  9,268  190  4,126  704  5,123  \nPMGSY-III 8,089  62,968  302  13,644  7,787  49,318  \nTotal 1,80,507  7,67,784  1,64,003  6,69,981  16,504  82,275  \n \nTamilnadu State \n \nRoad length in Km \nName of intervention  Sanctioned Completed Balance* \nNumber \nof roads \nRoad \nlength \nNumber \nof roads \nRoad \nlength \nNumber \nof roads \nRoad \nlength \nPMGSY-I 7,678  16,320  7,668  16,151  10  18  \nPMGSY-II 860  2,940  852  2,934  08  04  \nPMGSY-III 880  3,198  99  660  781  2,538  \nTotal 9,418  22,458  8,619  19,745  799  2,560  \n \n \nMadurai district  \nRoad length in Km \nName of intervention  Sanctioned Completed Balance* \nNumber \nof roads \nRoad \nlength \nNumber \nof roads \nRoad \nlength \nNumber \nof roads \nRoad \nlength \nPMGSY-I 280  512  280  512  0 0 \nPMGSY-II 36  126  36  126  0 0 \nPMGSY-III 13  42  02  06  11  35  \nTotal 329  680  318  644  11  35  \n \n*Balance road length may be less than the difference  of sanctioned and completed length due to \nvarious reasons such as reduction in curve length, change in alignment, c"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |34", "t": "Rashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 34 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19th July, 2021 \n \nRashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan \n \n34. SHRI ANURAG SHARMA: \n   \nWill the Minister of Education be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the present status of Rashtriya Madhyamik  Shiksha Abhiyan (RMSA)  in the country \nespecially in Uttar Pradesh; \n(b)  the details of funds allocated and utilized under the scheme by the State of Uttar  Pradesh, \nespecially in Jhansi and Lalitpur during last three years; \n(c)  the detail of schools promoted under the scheme in the State; and \n(d)  the other steps taken/propos ed to be taken by the Government to increase enrolment and \ncompletion of secondary school education? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION  \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a):  The Centrally Sponsored Scheme of Rashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan (RMSA) \nwas under implementation since 2009 to 2017 -18 for universalizing secondary education in the \ncountry. The RMSA along -with Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) and Teacher Education (TE) \nhave been subsumed in an integrated Centrally Sponsored Scheme for School Education - \nSamagra Shiksha from 2018 -19. It is an overarching programme for the school education sector \nextending from pre -school to class XII and aims to ensure inclusive and equitable quality \neducation at all levels of school education. \n \n(b): The funds released and ex penditure incurred by  Uttar Pradesh during last three years  \nunder Samagra Shiksha is as under: \n(Rs. in lakh) \nFinancial \nYear \nCentral Share Released Expenditure by Uttar Pradesh \n (including State Share) \n2018-19 462541.04 684631.10 \n2019-20 498559.56 683202.64 \n2020-21 457185.61 667843.10 \n \nFunds under Samagra Shiksha are released in lumpsum to the States/UTs and not district wise. \n\n(c): Samagra Shiksha aims at universalisation of secondary and higher secondary education \nby providing support to States/UTs for setting up secondary school and higher secondary school \nwithin a reasonable distance of 5 Km and 7 -10 Km, re spectively of every habitation. It also \nsupports strengthening of school infrastructure by provision of funds for new/upgraded schools, \nadditional classrooms, science laboratories, libraries, computer rooms, art and culture rooms, \ndrinking water facilities, boys' toilets, separate girls' toilets, Children with Special Needs \n(CwSN) toilets etc., as per the ga ps identified under UDISE+.  In the State of Uttar Pradesh, so \nfar 1551 secondary schools and 6 senior secondary schools have been sanctioned, out of which \n1483 secondary schools have become operational.  Under strengthening of secondary schools, \n102 science labs, 143 computer rooms, 355 art and culture rooms, 300 libraries, 937 additional \nclassrooms, 83 water drinking facilities have been sanctioned. Under strengthening of higher \nsecondary schools, 167 physics labs, 75 bio labs, 169 chemistry labs have been sanctioned.  \n \n(d): Besides the above, Samagra Shiksha also supports s pecific interventions such as ICT \ncoverage, capacity building of teachers, upgradation of KGBVs upto class XII , Vocational \nEducation, Transportation Allowance at secondary level in order to increase enrolment and \ncompletion of secondary school education . In addition, various qualitative interventions such as \nLearning Enhancement/ Enrichment Programme, Holistic Progress Card, Library grants, sports \ngrants, composite school grants, Rashtriya Avishkar Abhiyan etc. are also supported.  \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |340", "t": "Diversion of Rice Procured for Food Security for Ethanol Production", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF GONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIG DISTRIBUTION\nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED OUESTION NO. 34O\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2OrhJULY, 2O2t\nDIVERSION OF RIGE PROGURED FOR FOOD SEGURITY FOR ETHANOL\nPRODUGTION\n34O. SHRI V.K.SRIEEKANDAN:\nWill the llllinister of GONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIG\nDtsTRtBUTloN 3lrsfral arr&, urc-+ gk srd-dam Fffl{or d'* be pleased to\nstate:\n(b) whether the Government had committed about three lakh tonnes of\nrice procured by the Food Corporation of lndia for ethanol production, if sot\nthe details thereof; and\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT AND\nGONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIG DISTRIBUTIOT{\n(sADHVr NIRAHJAN JYOTI)\n(a) & (b): lndia imports about 85o/o of its crude oil requirement. Domestic\nbio.fuels like ethanol reduce the nationts dependenee on imported fossil\nfuels; in addition its use reduces vehicular pollutionr increases farmetst\nincome, brings new investment opportunity an rural areas thereby helping\nto achieve goal of Atmanirbhar Bharat.\nEthanol is being produced mainly from molasses/sugarcane and\ndamaged food-grains; but availability of these feedstocks as well as\nethanot production capacity is not suflicient to achieve blending talget\nfixed by the Government. Therefore, with a view to increase production of\nfuel grade ethanot for blending with petrol, Government is encouraging\ndistilleries to produce ethanol from surplus rice available with FCI; and\nmaize.\n._-_--2t-\n(a) whether the Government has allocated 78,OOO tonnes of rice\nprocured for food security purposes to be diverted to ethanol production\ninstead at a subsidised rate of Rs.20 per kg, if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether the Government aims to increase the blending levels to 8.5olo\nin the current year, if so, the details thereofif\n\n-2-\nThe FCI rice is sufficient in stock for food security purposes and it\nwill continue to remaln robust as procurement of paddy/rice at Minimum\nSupport Price (lrtSP) continues at expected levets to meet Publlc\nDistribution System (PDS) requirement. For the period of Decembe/2o2G\nNovembey'2ozlt 78844 MT of FGI rice has been allocated to distilleries @\nRs. 2olkg ex-FGI godown for production of ethanol out of which 22716 Ml\nhas been lifted by distilleries lill 14.7.2021.\n(c): Yes Sir. During the current Ethanol Supply Year (ESY) 2O2O'21lDec'\nNov), the Government has targeted to achieve 8.5% blending of ethanol\nwith petrol. Against the requirement of 332 crore litres ot ethanol for\nachieving 8.5% blending with petrol, contracts have been signed for\nsupplyang about 335 crore litres of ethanol by distilleries to Oi! Martetlng\nGompanies (OMGs) for btending of ethano! with petrol; against which about\n{go crore litres of ethanot have been supplied to oMcs by distilleries till\n12.7.2021 thereby achleving 7.93% blending which is the historically\nhighest figure."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |341", "t": "Beneficiaries under PDS", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF GONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIG DISTRIBUTION\nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION\nLOK SABHA\nUI{STARRED QUESTION 1{O. 34{\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 2OrHJULY,2O2{\nBENEEICIARIES UNDER PDS\n34{. SHRI PART}IIIBAN S.R.:\nWill the Minister of GOilSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIG\nDlsTRtBuTIox rquhor arr&, urc sik srffiq; fuf,{q t'* be pleased to state:\nstate:\n(a) the names of the commodities allocated to the States by the Union\nGovernment through Pubtic Distribution system (PDS) during the last three\nyears, the details thereof;\n(b) the number of househotds along with the number of beneficiaries who\nhave received these commodaties through PDS; and\n(c) the rates at which each household receives commodities through\nPDS along with the details of the commodities?\nANSUYER\nMINISTER OF STATE FOR M!ilISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT AND\nGONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIG DISTRIBUTION\n(sADHVl NIRANJAN JYOTI)\n(a) to (c): The Targeted Public Distribution System (TPDS) is governed as\nper provisions of the National Food Security Act, 2O{3 (NFSA) which\nprovides for al! lndia coverage of upto 75o/o ol the ruIal population and upto\n5O7o ol the urban population which at Gensus 2O{{ come to 8{.35 crore\npersons, for receiving subsidized foodgrains (Ricer Wheat and\nGoarsegrains). Goverage under the Act is under two categories'\nhouseholds covered under Antyodaya Anna Yoiana (a[Y) and the priority\nhousehotds (PHH) to be identified by the state Governments/ Union\nTerritories (uT) Administrations as per criteria evolved by themr within the\ncoverage limit determined for the state/uT. under the Act, while\nAntyodaya Anna Yoiana (AAY) households, are entitled to 35 kg of\nfoodgrains per family per month, priority households are entitled to 5 kg of\nfoodgrains per person per month at uniform subsidized prices of Rs' 31211\nper kg for rice/ wheat/ coarse grains respectively. The details of allocation\nand offtake of foodgrains in regard to States/UTs from year 2Ol8''19 to\n2O2O-21 under NFSA, are placed at Annexure-!.\n\nAnnexure.I\nAilNEXURE REFERRED TO tN REPLY TO PART (a) to (c) OF UNSTARRED\nQUESTIOil No. 34{ FOR ANSWER ON2O.O7.2O2{ IN THE LOK SABHA\nAllocation and Offtake ol'Foodgrains (Rice and Wheat) from year 2018-19 to 2020-21 under TPDS/NFSA\n(in thousand tons)\nsr\nNo\nStatesruTs\n2018-19 2019-20 2020-21\nAllocation Offtake Allocation Offtake Allocation Offtake\n1 1871.844 1871.844 1871.205 1871.844 2147.362\n2 Arunanchal Pradesh 88.992 87.032 88.992 92.023 88.99233 87.22328\nJ Assam 1695.t32 1691.029 1702.142 1694.324 1553.353\n4 Bihar 5493.861 5 188.923 5493.861 5404.327 5524.33 5183.855\n5 1384.056 1375.528 1384.056 1374.945 1384.056 1375.292\n6 Delhi 450.872 390.997 4s0.873 450.6901 449.4721\nGoa 59.031 59.031 59.045 59.04s s8.17318 55.99979\n8 Gujarat 2379.456 2379.462 1924.614 2r7