ount of compensation paid so far during \nthe said period, State-wise; \n \n(d)  whether the Government proposes to formulate any policy to review the  mechanism for \ncalculation of loss to give adequate compensation to the farmers; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reaction of the Government thereto? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n(SHRI  R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (e) :  No  such incident of fire has been reported in the lines owned by Inter State \nTransmission licensees including Power Grid Corporation of India Ltd during the last 3 years due to \nloose high tension wires of transmission lines. Various regulations and standa rds for safe \nconstruction, operation & maintenance of electricity infrastructure including for HT lines viz. \nminimum ground clearance, support structures, right of way, span, sag and tension, etc. have been \nnotified by the Government of India/ Electricity Regulatory Commissions/State Power Departments \nto be strictly adhered to by the Distribution and Transmission Utilities. \nIn addition, the Government of India through Skill India has taken initiative to train the \nlinemen of the DISCOMs to help them maintain the infrastructure created. \nElectricity is a concurrent subject and distribution of electricity falls primarily under the \npurview of the respective State Governments and it is the responsibility of the respective State \nPower Utility to operate and maintai n the electricity infrastructure in proper condition. \nAccordingly, the compensation for losses caused by any electricity accidents including fire in the \nfields caused by loose high-tension wires falls within the domain of the State Government/Utilities \nand is decided by the respective State Government/State Power Utility as per their policies in this \nregard. \nInformation in this regard was sought from all the States. However, only State of \nKarnataka, so far, has reported that they have paid compensation amou nting to Rs. 136 lakh \ntowards 352 incidents of fire in the field related to loose high -tension wires of the Distribution \nCompanies, as per their State policy during the periods 2018-19 to 2020-21. \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |462", "t": "Opening of Agro Industry", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 462\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2021\n \n \nOpening of Agro Industry\n \n \n462. \n \nSHRI HANUMAN BENIWAL\n: \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND\n \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to open/start Agro Industry in the Atmanirbhar Bharat \nAbhiyaan keeping in mind the number of agriculture graduates passing out every year; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(\nc) \n \nwhether the Government intends to set up any agro industry in Rajasthan as the large \nnumber of population is engaged in agriculture and spices and other agro products; and\n \n \n(d) \n \nif\n \nso, the details thereof along with the time by which the said industry is likely to be set \nup in Rajasthan and if not, the reasons therefor?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a)\n \n&\n \n(b)\n:\n \nGovernment in the Ministry of MSME is implementing since 2008\n-\n09, Prime \nMinister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP) which is a major credit\n-\nlinked subsidy \nprogramme aimed at generating self\n-\nemployment opportunities through establishment of micro\n-\nent\nerprises in the non\n-\nfarm sector by helping traditional artisans and rural/urban unemployed \nyouth. The \nScheme is in line with the \nAtmanirbhar Bharat Abhiyaan as it encourages setting up of \nnew micro\n-\nenterprises using local raw material and rural workforce.\n \nThe maximum cost of \nprojects is Rs.25 lakh in the manufacturing sector and Rs.10 lakh in the service sector with \nsubsidy varying from 15% to 35% for different categories. \n \n \nAny \nindividual can apply for PMEGP through online portal \nwww.kviconline.gov.in\n. \nEmphasis has also been given to reach out to students in various Universities including \nagriculture \ngraduates, to generate awareness about the scheme and encourage them to apply under it.\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \n \nThe number of\n \nunits assisted, disbursement of margin money and employment generated \nunder PMEGP across the country during last three years and current year is as follows:\n \nYear\n \nNo. of units \nset up**\n \nMargin Money \ndisbursed \n \n(Rs. in crore)\n \nEstimated \nEmployment \n \n(Nos.)\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n73427\n \n2070.00\n \n587416\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n66653\n \n1950.82\n \n533224\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n74415\n \n2188.80\n \n595320\n \n2021\n-\n22 \n \n(as on 09.07.2021)\n \n16639\n \n518.25\n \n133112\n \nTotal\n \n231134\n \n6727.87\n \n1849072\n \n(**\nFigure of Year\n-\nwise \nno. of units set up indicate the no. of units for which the MM has been disbursed to \nthe banks in the respective FY)\n \nAbout 25% of the\n \nunits setup under PMEGP belongs to agro and forest based industries.\n \n \n \n(c) \n& (d)\n:\n \nTarget and achievement for the last \nthree years to set up micro\n-\nenterprises under \nPMEGP\n \nin Rajasthan is as follows:\n \n \nSl\n.\n \nYear\n \nTarget\n \nAchievement \n \n \n \nNo. of Units \nset up\n \nMM \n(Rs. in \nlakh)\n \nEmploym\nent (Nos.)\n \nNo. of units \nset up\n \nMM (Rs. \nin lakh)\n \nEmploym\nent (Nos.)\n \n1\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n3097\n \n7743.65\n \n24776\n \n2359\n \n7199.28\n \n18872\n \n2\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n3398\n \n10194.20\n \n27184\n \n3025\n \n8174.68\n \n24200\n \n3\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n2697\n \n8094.02\n \n21576\n \n2772\n \n8806.83\n \n22176\n \n \nBased on the dynamic situation arising out of COVID\n-\n19 challenges and the previous years’ \nperformance, the approximate target for setting up PMEGP units during FY 2021\n-\n22 and \nachievement thereon in the state of Rajasthan is as follows: \n \n \nTarget\n \nAchievement\n \n(as on 9.7.2021)\n \nNo. of Units \nset up\n \nMM (Rs. in \nlakh)\n \nEmployment \n(Nos.)\n \nNo. of units \nset up\n \nMM \n \n(Rs. in lakh)\n \nEmployment \n(Nos.)\n \n3500\n \n10500.00\n \n28000\n \n305\n \n1216.50\n \n2440\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n: 3 :\n \n \n \n \nIn addition, Ministry is also implementing \nScheme of Fund for Regeneration of \nTraditional Industries (SFURTI). \nThe focus of the scheme is to organize traditional industries \nand artisans into clusters and provide them with sustainable employment by making their products \nmore competitive through valu\ne addition. Under the scheme, artisans are collectively supported \nthrough construction of Common Facility Centres (CFCs), new plants and machineries, training, \netc. in sectors\n \nsuch as Agro Based Industries, Honey, Khadi, Coir, Handicrafts, Textiles, Bamboo\n \netc. \n \n \nAs on 19.07.2021, 433 clusters have been approved benefitting about 2.6 lakh artisan with \nGoI assistance of Rs.1102 crore.\n \n \nIn \nRajasthan, 27 clusters have been approved under SFURTI benefitting about 15284 \nartisans with GoI assistance of 75.20 cror\ne. Of these 10 clusters are in agro processing, bamboo \nand honey. These clusters are likely to be functional within a period of 12 to 18 months from date \nof approval.\n \n \n \n*****\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |463", "t": "Projects under Smart City Mission", "head": "O.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n  LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.  463 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 22, 2021 \n \nPROJECTS UNDER SMART CITY MISSION \nNO. 463. SHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA:  \n                 SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI:  \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a)  the details of projects sanctioned for Uttar P radesh and \n Rajasthan  under Smart City Mission; \n(b)  the names of projects which have been complete d in Uttar \n Pradesh and Rajasthan out of these projects; \n(c)  the details of expenditure incurred thereon; \n(d)  the number of pending projects in Uttar Prades h and Rajasthan \n under Smart City Mission so far; and  \n(e)  the time limit fixed/needed for their completi on? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE  IN THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)   The status of projects under Smart Cities Mission (SCM) being \nimplemented in the States of Uttar Pradesh and  Rajasthan is \ngiven in Annexure-I. \n \n(b)  &(c) List of projects completed and expenditure the reon under \n SCM in the States of Uttar Pradesh and Rajasthan a re given in \n Annexure-II.  \n \n(d)  &(e) The Smart Cities are expected to complete thei r projects \n within 5 years from the date of their selection.  \n***** \n\nAnnexure-I in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. †463 for answer on \n22.07.2021 regarding Projects under Smart City Mission \n \nStatus of projects of Smart Cities in Uttar Pradesh \n \n(Amount in ₹ crore) \nSmart City Selection \nRound \nTender Stage Work Order Stage Work Completed Total \nnumber \nof \nProjects \nTotal \nAmount \nNo. of \nProjects \nAmount No. of \nProjects \nAmount No. of \nProjects \nAmount \nAgra 2 0 0 18 835.29 41 1533.61 59 2368.90 \nAligarh 3 4 105.24 21 1031.25 18 445.57 43 1582.06 \nBareilly 4 15 232.68 30 875.22 19 115.35 64 1223.25  \nJhansi 3 22 440.00 26 751.16 19 562.04 67 1753.20 \nKanpur 2 3 246.26 22 2195.23 17 985.47 42 3426.96 \nLucknow Fast \nTrack \n8 243.74 24 732.38 41 1065.24 73 2041.36 \nMoradabad 4 4 260.70 14 172.82 3 570.86 21 1004.38 \nPrayagraj 3 14 332.03 50 357.46 25 1185.14 89 1874. 63 \nSaharanpur 4 6 394.62 13 516.31 5 44.68 24 955.61 \nVaranasi 2 2 158.87 25 651 73 2403.78 100 3213.65 \nTotal  78 2,414.14 243 8,118.12 261 8,911.74 582 19 ,444 \n(Source: Geo-Spatial Management Information System) \n \nStatus of projects of Smart Cities in Rajasthan \n(Amount in ₹  crore)  \n \nSmart City \nSelection \nRound \n Tender Stage Work Order Stage Work Completed Total \nno. of \nProjects \nTotal \nAmount \n  No. of \nProjects Amount No. of \nProjects Amount No. of \nProjects Amount \nAjmer 2   0 0 69  1324.9  101  713.75  170  2038.65  \nJaipur 1   17  452.56  79  1409.16  43  1222.89  139  3084.61  \nKota 2   6 39.73  24  1178.20  42  896.35  72  2114.28  \nUdaipur 1   11  129.47  31  926.11  89  785.01  131  1840.59  \n           \nTotal  34  621.76  203  4,838.37  275  3,618  512  9,078.13  \n(Source: Geo-Spatial Management Information System) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-II in reply to part (b) & (c) of  Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. †463 for \nanswer on 22.07.2021 regarding Projects under Smart City Mission \nSmart City-wise list of projects completed in Uttar Pradesh \n \nS.No. Name of Project  \n \n \n    Project Cost \n(Amount in \n ₹  crore) \nAgra \n1 Selection of Master system Integrator (MSI) 288 \n2 Development of Taj Orientation Centre – Main Comp lex, Parking Complex, \nHorticulture, Open Air Theatre, Restaurant \n231.8 \n3 Enhancing experience of accessing through improve ment of west, east and south gate \ncorridors, underground cabling, C.C. roads, footpat hs, landscaping, CCTVs, Firefighting \nSystem, \n197.27 \n4 Smart Electric Buses for Agra City 160 \n5 Development of Mughal Museum including Auditorium  and underground parking 141.9 \n6 rebuilding livelihoods and upgrading slums – rede velopment of slums 116.3 \n7 in situ house upgradation 114.3 \n8 Laying of sewer line 74.84 \n9 Provision of household sewer connections 34.95 \n10 LED Light Energy Saving Project 28.45 \n11 Taj East Drain Improvement plan 27.9 \n12 Solar water heater (SWH) on Hotels 26.53 \n13 Development of corridor connecting Agra and Taj M ahal 21 \n14 Conversion of streetlights to LED 7.05 \n15 Junction Improvement- Taj view crossing, Bijli Gh ar and Purani Mandi junctions 6.98 \n16 Smart Signages advertisement screen mounted on st reetlight poles 6.92 \n17 Rainwater harvesting pits 6.7 \n18 Agra-Etawah lion safari cycle highway 4.2 \n19 Public e- toilets 3.99 \n20 Recycle or Bring Back Centre 3.83 \n21 Improvement of vicinity of lesser known heritage monuments – Kali Masjid, Diwaniji \nKa  Maqbara, Dargah of Lala Bukhar, Teli Pada mosqu e, Traditional Houses \n3.68 \n22 Conversion of shanshanghat to e-shamshanghat 3 \n23 Up gradation of Municipal Schools 3 \n24 Taj Park 2.4 \n25 Junction redesign 2.22 \n26 Micro Skill Development Centre 2.01 \n27 Roadside Plantation 2 \n28 In-situ house upgradation 2 \n29 Development of green walkway between Agra Fort an d Taj Mahal along "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |464", "t": "Green Tariff Policy", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-464 \nANSWERED ON-22/07/2021 \n \nGREEN TARIFF POLICY \n \n464. SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE \n        SHRI MANICKAM TAGORE B \n        DR. DNV SENTHILKUMAR. S \n        SHRI SUNIL DATTATRAY TATKARE \n        SHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA \n        DR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE \n        DR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to formulate a green tariff policy that will help \nelectricity distribution companies (DISCOMs) to supply electricity generated from clean \nenergy sources at a cheaper rate as compared to power from conventional fuel sources \nsuch as coal and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether t he Government also proposes to formulate a set of rules and guidelines to \nenable such a mechanism and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the green tariff will be slightly lower than the conventional fuel sources and if \nso, the steps taken by the Government to further bring down the green tariff so that use of \nclean energy could be enhanced; \n(d) whether India is running the world‘s largest clean energy programme to achieve 175 \ngigawatt (GW) of renewable capacity, including 100 GW of solar power by 2022 and if \nso, the details thereof along with the steps taken to achieve the target; and \n(e) the other steps taken by the Government to attract large green electricity consumers for \nsetting up of their own captive green energy plants? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n(a) to (b) Yes Sir. \n  \n(c) &(d) Green tariff would depend upon the actual cost of procurement of renewable power by \nthe distribution company and additional costs including in transmission, distribution etc.  \n \nIndia’s renewable energy programme is one of the largest in the world. The Government has \ntaken several steps to promote renewable energy in the country and reduce its cost. These \ninclude:- \n• permitting Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) up to 100 percent under the automatic route,  \n\n \n \n• waiver of Inter State Transmission System (ISTS) charges for inter-state sale of solar and \nwind power for projects to be commissioned by 30th June 2025,  \n• declaration of trajectory for Renewable Purchase Obligation (RPO) up to the year 2022,  \n• setting up of Ultra Mega Renewable Energy Parks to provide land and transmission to RE \ndevelopers on a plug and play basis,  \n• schemes such as Pradhan Mantri Kisan Urja Suraksha evam Utthaan Mahabhiyan (PM-\nKUSUM), Solar Rooftop Phase II, 12000 MW CPSU Scheme Phase II, etc, \n• laying of new transmission lines and creating new sub-station capacity for evacuation of \nrenewable power, \n• notification of standards for deployment of solar photovoltaic system/devices,  \n• setting up of Project Development Cell for attracting and facilitating investments, \n• Standard Bidding Guidelines for tariff based competitive bidding for procurement of \nPower from Grid Connected Solar PV and Wind Projects.  \n• Introduction of Green Term Ahead Market (GTAM) \n• Issuance of  Rights of Consumer rules  to make the Consumers producing renewable \nenergy to be recognized as Prosumers \n• Government has issued orders that power shall be dispatched against Letter of Credit \n(LC) or advance payment to ensure timely payment by distribution licensees to RE \ngenerators. \n \n(e) The Electricity Act 2003 grants captive power generators, the right to open access (Section \n9); and exempts captive power generators from cross subsidy charges.  \n \nFurther, Government of India has promulgated Electricity (Rights of Consu mers) Rules, 2020 \nlaying down the rights of power consumers which inter -alia, include the rights to set up \nRenewable Energy (RE) generation unit including roof top solar photovoltaic (PV) systems. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |465", "t": "Non Conventional Solar Energy", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-465 \nANSWERED ON-22/07/2021 \n \nNON CONVENTIONAL SOLAR ENERGY \n465. SHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK \n         SHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU \n         SHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO  \n         SHRI MANOJ TIWARI \n         SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV \n         SHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE \n         SHRI SUDHEER GUPTA \n         SHRI RAVI KISHAN \n         SHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether all the States are not naturally endowed with natural resources to generate non-\nconventional solar energy and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether the Government has made any study to identify natural resource potential for generation \nof nonconventional energy of various States and if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) whether the Government has asked all the States to abide by a naturally determined uniform and \nsub categorized Renewal Purchase Obligation (RPO);  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the response of the States thereto;  \n(e) whether States with low potential of nonconventional energy has been demanding to exclude \nthem for compliance of RPO targets; and  \n(f) if so, the details thereof and the action taken thereto? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) & (b) The Renewable Energy potential varies from State to State. The State -wise details of \nestimated renewable energy potential (excluding large hydro) identified for various renewable energy \nsources in the country are given in Annexure.  \n \n(c) & (d) Ministry of Power has notified uniform Renewable Purchase Obligation (RPO) trajectory \nup to the year 2021 -22 under two broad categories  of solar and non -solar seeking balanced growth \nof Renewable Energy (excluding large hydro) in all States. The uniform RPO trajectory has been \nfinalized keeping in view the national targets for solar and non -solar (wind, biomass, small hydro \netc.) renewable energy capacity.  \n \nIn terms of Section 86(1)(e) of the Electricity Act 2003, respective State Electricity Regulatory \nCommissions can also notify state-specific RPO targets from time to time. \n \n(e) & (f) The Ministry of New & Renewable Energy has not received request from any State to exclude \nthem from compliance of RPO targets. \n \n***** \n \n \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) & (b):  of Lok Sabha Un-starred Question No.465 for 22/7/2021 regarding \n„Non Conventional Solar Energy‟ \n“Source and State-wise details of estimated potential of Renewable Energy in MW” \nSl. \nNo. \nSTATES / UTs \nWind Power  \nSmall \nHydro \nPower \nBio-Energy \nSolar Total Biomass \nPower \nBagase \nCogenerati\non \nWaste To \nEnergy \n  \n  \n(MW) @ 120m (MW) (MW) (MW) (MW) (MW) (MW) \n1 Andhra Pradesh 74906 409.32 578 300 123 38440 114756 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 274 2064.92 8     8650 10997 \n3 Assam 246 201.99 212   8 13760 14428 \n4 Bihar  3650 526.98 619 300 73 11200 16369 \n5 Chhattisgarh 348 1098.20 236   24 18270 19976 \n6 Goa 8 4.70 26     880 918 \n7 Gujarat 142560 2