nProject\n \nMM\n \nEmpl.\n \n1\n \nAngul\n \n119\n \n283.57\n \n807\n \n91\n \n219.23\n \n505\n \n108\n \n327.08\n \n864\n \n80\n \n326.45\n \n981\n \n \n78\n \n238.16\n \n781\n \n2\n \nBalasore\n \n122\n \n280.79\n \n825\n \n88\n \n190.52\n \n320\n \n138\n \n329.55\n \n1104\n \n155\n \n342.62\n \n1061\n \n \n203\n \n543.34\n \n1565\n \n3\n \nBargarh\n \n119\n \n244.81\n \n806\n \n64\n \n153.95\n \n290\n \n71\n \n238.81\n \n568\n \n70\n \n233.55\n \n784\n \n \n99\n \n378.1\n \n1081\n \n4\n \nBhadrak\n \n132\n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |508", "t": "Tonnage Capacity of Indian Shipping Industry", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING & WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 508\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 202'I\nTONNAGE CAPACITY OF INDIAN SHIPPING INDUSTRY\n508. SHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE:\nWillthe Minister of PORTS. SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS be pleased to state:\nrfl{, +( cR:€{ +{ {dcFi {*(a) whether the Government proposes to increase the tonnage capacity of lndian\nshipping industry;\n(b) ifso, the details thereof; and\n(c) the tonnage capacity of the shipping industry in the country during the last three\nyears, year-wise?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRYOF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(SHRI SHRIPAD NAIK)\n(a) & (b) Yes sir. The various steps taken by the Government, to increase the tonnage\ncapacity of lndian Shipping industry are as below:\n1. The Directorate General of Shipping has issued a Circular vide DGS\nCircular No. 02 of 2021 which deals with the Right of First Refusal (RoFR).\nThrough this circular, it was stated clearly that the order of grant of RoFR\nshall be (i) lndian built, lndian flagged (lndian owned), (ii)Foreign built,\nlndian flagged (lndian owned) and (iii) lndian built, Foreign\nflagged(Foreign owned). This attracts the business entities to build their\nvessel in lndia and to get them registered under lndian flag, which leads to\nincrease in lndia's Tonnage Capacity.\n2. Further, to increase lndian Shipping tonnage, Government has started\n'Make in lndia' programme, which encourages domestic ship-building by\nproviding a level playing field vis-a-vis foreign ship-building yards. The\nGovernment has been implementing, the Shipbuilding Financial\nAssistance Policy for lndian ship-building yards, since December 2015.\nThis policy has a provision for providing financial assistance to shipyards\nover a period of 10 years for contracts secured between 01.04.2016 and\n31.O3.2026.\n3. lndian and Foreign entities, intending to engage foreign flag vessels, for\ncoastal trade of lndia/Exim trade, need to have a license under section\n406 of MS Act, 1958, from, DG Shipping, Gol. The license is issued to\nsuch foreign flag vessel, only after ensuring that no matching lndian\nvessel is available, for such trade/function, intended to be performed by\nforeign flag vessel. Accordingly, lndian ships are given priority above any\nforeign ships for such transportation. Because of this facility, the business\nentities\nContd...2/-\n\n-2-\nand even shipping companies, are intended to make efforts, to build own\n(or register) their vessels under lndian flag. This helps to increase the\nlndian shipping tonnage.\n4. lndian Shipping enterprises have also been allowed to acquire ships\nabroad and also flag them in the country of their convenience, under the\nlndian Controlled Tonnage [TC] scheme.\n(c) The details of tonnage capacity of the country in the last three years, are as\nbelow:\nYear Coastal Overseas Total\nShips Gross\nTonnage\nShips Gross\nTonnage\nShips Gross\nTonnage\n3'1.12.2018 945 14,79,U3 456 1,12,04,598 1401 1,26,83,941\n31.12.2019 972 14,79,692 457 1,12,66,341 1,429 1,27,46,033\n31.12.2020 998 16,72,581 465 1,13,37,811 1463 1 ,30,1 0,392\n30.06.202'1 1021 15,44,522 467 1,'t4,18,526 1488 1,29,63,048"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |51", "t": "Financial Support for Climate Resilient Infrasturcture", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.51 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2021 \n \nSWACHH BHARAT MISSION (GRAMEEN) \n \n*51. SHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY: \nSHRI SANJAY KAKA PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has recently approved  the Annual Implementation Plan of \nSwachh Bharat Mission (Grameen) [SBM (G)] for States and UTs; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise; \n \n(c) whether SBM(G) (Phase 2) has a target to  support over two lakh villages to achieve \nSolid and  Liquid Waste Management (SLWM) systems through an  investment of over \nRs.40,700 crores during the present financial year 2021-22; and \n \n(d) if so, the details of mob ilisation of the above amount and the share of the Centre vis -a-\nvis the States therein? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n \n \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nStatement referred to in the reply to Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 51 due for reply \non 22.07.2021. \n \nGovernment had launched Swachh Bharat Mission (Grameen) [SBM  (G)] with \neffect from 2nd October, 2014, with the main aim to make the country Open Defecation \nFree (ODF) by 2nd October, 2019, by providing access to toilets to all the rural \nhouseholds in the country.  Having achieved the outcomes  of the ODF, Phase -II of \nSBM(G) is now being implemented with the focus on ODF sustainability and to make \narrangements for Solid and Liquid Waste Manage ment (SLWM) in all villages i.e. ODF \nPlus, by 2024-25.   \n \nFor implementation of the programme, each year State Governments submit their \nAnnual Implementation Plans (AIPs) to the Government of India .  The AIPs are to be \nprepared by consolidation of the annual plans of all the districts /Gram Panchayats.  The \nAIPs contains the details of activities planned to be undertaken by the States during the \nyear and the estimated funds requirement for the same.  The AIP s are approved by the \nNational Scheme Sanctioning Committee (NSSC) constituted for the purpose. AIPs of the \nStates/UTs for 2021-22 have been approved by the NSSC in its meeting held on 27 th May, \n2021. The overall targets set by the States/UTs in their AIPs are as under: \n \nEstimated No. of Individual Household Latrines (IHHLs) (subject to \nactual demand) \n51.05 lakh \nNo. of Community Sanitary Complexes (CSCs) 1.03 lakh \nNo. of villages to be covered with Solid Waste Management (SWM) 2.07 lakh \nNo. of villages to be covered with Grey Water Management (GWM) 1.82 lakh \nNo. of Blocks in which Plastic Waste Management Units (PWMUs) \nto be set up \n2,458 \nNo. of Districts in which GOBAR-dhan Projects to be taken up 386 \nNo. of Districts in which Faecal Sludge Management (FSM) Plants to \nbe set up \n264 \n \nState/UT-wise details of the AIP targets are given at Annexure.  The total \nestimated funds requirement for all these activities as projected by the States/UTs is Rs. \n40,705 crore out of which Centre  and States shares are Rs. 13,982 crores and Rs. 8,332 \ncrore, respectively. Funds to the tune of Rs.  12,730 crore will be made available through \nthe 15th Finance Commission grants for rural local bodies and Rs.  4,138 crore through \nconvergence with Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme \n(MGNREGS).  Rs.  1,522 crore will be invested by the States through other sources e.g. \nBusiness Model, Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR), other schemes, etc.   \n \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \n State/UT-wise targets under SBM (G) for 2021-22 as per their AIPs  \nState/UT Estimated \nNo. of \nIHHLs  \n(subject \nto actual \ndemand) \nNo. of \nCSCs \nNo. of \nVillages \nto be \ncovered \nwith \nSWM \nNo. of \nvillages \nto be \ncovered \nwith \nGWM \nNo. of \nBlocks \nin which \nPWMUs \nto be set \nup \nNo. of \ndistricts \nin which \nGOBAR-\ndhan \nprojects \nto be \ntaken up \nNo. of \ndistricts \nin \nwhich \nFSM \nPlants \nto be \nset up \nA & N Islands 333 0 18 0 2 0 0 \nAndhra Pradesh 74,891 11,470 4,402 7,997 20 4 13 \nArunachal Pradesh 3,307 300 1,073 1,073 30 2 2 \nAssam 2,80,000 6,000 22,953 33 199 10 5 \nBihar 10,00,000 8,000 7,636 7,636 30 2 2 \nChhattisgarh 1,54,418 6,031 4,853 4,853 82 28 28 \nDadra & Nagar \nHaveli and Daman \n& Diu \n5,199 15 52 52 3 2 1 \nGoa 3,000 0 191 191 12 0 2 \nGujarat 1,16,939 2,232 3,607 3,607 51 22 20 \nHaryana 17,130 1,836 3,146 2,671 0 21 0 \nHimachal Pradesh 25,719 10,845 5,338 5,338 81 12 12 \nJammu & Kashmir 60,000 1,000 1,000 1,000 100 3 20 \nJharkhand 1,93,749 100 6,165 6,165 54 24 9 \nKarnataka 1,10,465 200 7,411 3,776 206 30 30 \nKerala 16,022 500 500 500 70 12 2 \nLadakh 2,000 193 228 50 18 2 2 \nMadhya Pradesh 2,70,000 9,500 22,060 22,061 52 52 0 \nMaharashtra 2,19,699 11,127 22,173 22,173 351 34 11 \nManipur 0 82 1,536 1,531 14 0 0 \nMeghalaya 61,893 5,353 4,776 4,776 39 4 3 \nMizoram 5,482 148 259 259 7 6 8 \nNagaland 7,745 110 175 175 25 0 5 \nOdisha 2,40,450 1,776 14,036 14,036 94 0 30 \nPuducherry 1,100 20 108 21 1 2"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |510", "t": "MSME Adversely Affected during Covid", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 510\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2021\n \n \nMSME Adversely Affected during Covid\n \n \n510.     SHRI LALLU SINGH:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to \nstate:\n \n \n(a)  \n \nwhether the startups, small and medium \nindustries\n \nare adversely affected by the\n \nsecond wave of corona in the country and if so, the details thereof;\n \n(b) \n \nwhether many star\ntups as well as small industries have closed down or they are \nat\n \nthe verge of closing down;\n \n(c)  \n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n(d) \n \nwhether the Government has formulated a scheme for providing assistance to \nthese industries; and\n \n(e) \n \nif \nso\n, the details \nthereof?\n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a)to(e)\n:\n \nThe \nsecond wave of corona has \naffected t\nhe economy,  including start\n-\nups, small and medium industries. For providing assistance to these industries the \nGovernment has taken several initiatives. Some of these are as under:  \n \n \ni.\n \nRs. 20,000 crore Subordinate Debt for MSMEs.\n \nii.\n \nRs.3 lakh crores Collatera\nl free Automatic Loans for business, including \nMSMEs.\n \niii.\n \nRs. 50,000 crore equity infusion through MSME Self\n-\nReliant India Fund \n \niv.\n \nNew Revised criteria of classification of MSMEs.\n \nv.\n \nNew Registration of MSMEs through ‘Udyam Registration’ for Ease of Doing \nBusiness.\n \nvi.\n \nNo global tenders for procurement up to Rs. 200 crores.\n \n \n \n \nAn Online Portal “Champions” has been launched on 01.06.2020 by Hon’ble \nPrime Minister. This covers many aspects of e\n-\ngovernance including redressal of \ngrievances and handholding of MSMEs. \n \n \n \nRBI \nhas also announced several measures to Reduce Financial Stress of \nMSMEs.\n \n \n******\n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |511", "t": "Projects under Smart Cities Mission", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n  LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 511 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 22, 2021 \n \nPROJECTS UNDER SMART CITIES MISSION \nNO. 511. DR. MOHAMMAD JAWED:  \n  SHRI K. MURALEEDHARAN:  \n  SHRI NALIN KUMAR KATEEL:  \n  SHRI D.K.SURESH:  \n  ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH:  \n  SHRI GAURAV GOGOI:  \n  DR. A. CHALLAKUMAR:  \n  SHRI M. SELVARAJ:  \n  SHRI ANTO ANTONY:  \n  DR. T. SUMATHY (a) THAMIZHACHI THANGAPANDIAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total number of projects under the Smart C ities Mission \n which have been completed as on June, 2021 and the  details of \n targets set and achievements/ progress made under the Mission \n so far;  \n(b)  the total number of projects for which work or ders have been \n issued as on June, 2021 and the number of cities c onverted into \n smart cities so far;  \n(c)  the quantum of funds allocated, released and s pent as of \n June,2021 out of the total commitment made by the Centre to \n spend Rs 48000 crore over a period of five years; \n(d)  whether the Government intends to meet its sta ted deadline of \n completion of projects for the first round of citi es selected under \n the Smart Cities Mission in 2021 and if so, the de tails thereof and \n if not, the proposed altered deadline;  \n...2/ \n\n-2- \n(e)  the impact of COVID-19 on the mission and the efforts being \n made by Government to deal with the same; and  \n(f)  the number of projects expected to benefit the  most \n marginalized and low-income groups and the total n umber of \n beneficiaries from these groups, city-wise? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n    (SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE)  \n \n(a) to (c) Government of India launched the Smart C ities Mission \n(SCM) on 25 June 2015 for development of 100 cities as Smart Cities. \nThe selection of 100 Smart Cities was completed thr ough 4 rounds of \nselection from January, 2016 to June, 2018.  \nAs of June 30, 2021, the 100 Smart Cities have tend ered out 5,956 \nprojects worth \n\u00011,79,413 crore, out of which work orders for 5,314 \nprojects worth \u0001 1,48,029 crore have been issued and 2,734 projects  \nworth \u000146,769 crore have been completed. \nCentral Government has released \u0001 23,925 crore to States/Union \nTerritories for its Smart Cities, of which \u000120,410.14 crore (85%) has \nbeen utilized by them.  \n \n(d)  The status of project in the 20 Smart Cities s elected in Round-1 \nin January, 2016 is given below:- \n \n(Amount in \u0001crores) \nSmart City \nTender Stage Work Order Stage Work Completed \nTotal \nProjects \n \nTotal \nAmount \nNo. of \nProjects Amount No. of \nProjects Amount No. of \nProjects \n Amount \nRound -1 Cities  \n(20 cities) 127  11,750  548  26,966  1,119  18,408  1,794  57,124  \n(Source: Geo-Spatial Management Information System) \nOut of 1,794 projects worth \u000157,124 crore that were part of the Smart \nCity Plans of Smart Cities selected in Round-1, 1,667 projects (93%)  \n...3/ \n\n-3- \nworth \u000145,374 crore (79%) have either been completed or ar e under \nimplementation. All efforts are being made for time ly completion of \nprojects undertaken in these cities. \n(e)  COVID-19 pandemic related lockdowns and other unprecedented \nsituations have led to temporaryinterruptions in ex ecution of Smart \nCity works across the country. However, most of the Smart Cities have \ncommenced works after un-lockdown and are trying their best to make \nup for the time lost due to such unprecedented and unforeseen \ncircumstances. \n(f)  Under SCM, the focus is on sustainable and inc lusive \ndevelopment. The Mission is implementing projects i n diverse areas \nsuch as mobility, built environment, non-motorized and public \ntransport, water and wastewater, renewable energy, solid waste \nmanagement, affordable housing, safety and security  of citizens, \nhealth and education. Comprehensive development thr ough a \ncombination of area-based and pan-city approaches i mpacts quality of \nlife, creates employment and enhances income for al l, especially the \nmost marginalized and low-income groups in cities and urban areas. \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |512", "t": "State Maritime Board", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 512\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22NO JUIY,2021\nSTATE MARITIME BOARD\n512. SHRIACHYUTANANDASAMANTA:\nWill the Minister of PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS be pleased to state:\nTf,{, fi-fr qft-{e{ ei-( q-mqr{i t'*\n(a) whether the Government is aware of the setting up of the odisha state Maritime\nBoard;\n(b) whether the Draft lndian Ports }il,2021 does not contain any provision to include a\nState Maritime Board constituted at a later date by a State enactment and if so, the reasons\nfor such omission; and\n(c) whether the draft lndian Ports Bill amplifies 2021 lhe powers of the Maritime States\nDevelopment Council (MSDC); if so, the details and the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(SHRISHRIPAD NAIK)\n(a) Yes, Sir.\n(b) The draft of lndian Ports bill, 2021 vide chapter V requires every state Government\nto constitute a state Maritime Board for all non-major ports by way of notification in the\nofficial gazefte within six months from the date of commencement of the Bill.\n(c) The draft of the Bill propose to statutory empower the Maritime State Development Council\nlirf'SOCI to become the nodal agency for coordinated development of all ports (major and non-major\nports) in lndia. The Bill is aimed at synergy between coastal states and centre through collaborative\neffort through integrated approach for development of Ports. The proposed legislation is under\nconsultative stage."}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |513", "t": "Housing for Homeless", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 513 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 22, 2021 \n \nHOUSING FOR HOMELESS \n \nNO.   513.  SHRI ASHOK MAHADEORAO NETE: \n \n  Will the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be  \npleased to state:- \n \na)  whether the Supreme Court has given direction to th e Government \nto assess the availability of housing for homeless and to take steps \nto provide them temporary houses; \n \nb)  if so, the details thereof; \n \nc)  whether the Court was not satisfied with the implem entation of \nNational Urban Livelihood Mission and had issued ap propriate \ndirection in this regard; \n \nd)  if so, the details thereof; \n \ne)  the number of Below Poverty Line (BPL) card holders  allotted \npermanent/temporary houses after direction of court; and \n \nf)  the details of various facilities provided by State s/UTs to the BPL \ncard holders? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nHOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n    (SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE)  \n \n(a)to(d) There are two Writ Petitions [WP(C) No. 55 /2003 and WP(C) \nNo. 572/2003] before Hon’ble Supreme Court of India  on the \nissue of urban homeless. Hon’ble Court has given di rections in \nthis regard from time to time.   \n…2/- \n\n-2- \n \n In its order dated 13.11.2014, Hon’ble Court direc ted the \nerstwhile Ministry of Housing and Urban Poverty All eviation to \nfind out ways and means to provide shelters to all the shelter-\nless and homeless people in different States/ Union  \nTerritories.  \nVide its order dated 04.12.2015, it expressed its \ndissatisfaction with the steps taken for the implem entation of \nShelter for Urban Homeless (SUH) component of Deend ayal \nAntyodaya Yojana - National Urban Livelihood Missio n (DAY-\nNULM).   \nVide order dated 11.11.2016, Hon'ble Court constituted a  \nthree-memberCommittee headed by Justice Kailash Gam bhir, \nRetired Judge, High Court of Delhi. The mandate of the \nCommittee included, inter-alia,  to suitably advise the State \nGovernments in the matter. Hon’ble Court also direc ted the \nState Governments to comply with the recommendation s of \nthe Committee. The Committee directed the States/UT s, inter-\nalia, to conduct third party survey of urban homele ss by the \nUrban Local Bodies to assess the number of homeless  and \nprepare a plan for providing shelters in a phased manner.  \nSubsequently, Hon’ble Court vide itsorder dated 10. 01.2018, \ngave directions for constitution of State Level She lter \nMonitoring Committees (SLSMC) under the chairmanshi p of an \nofficer, who has retired at the level equivalent to  Secretary to \nthe Government of India, to monitor the progress of Shelter for \nUrban Homeless Scheme. \n  \n(e)&(f) Presently, 1,602 NULM Shelters are function al in States/UTs \nwith a capacity of 87,745 persons.   \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |514", "t": "Schemes of Khadi Gramodyog", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 514\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2021\n \n \nSchemes of Khadi Gramodyog\n \n \n514. \n \nSHRI VIJAY BAGHEL: \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND\n \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe schemes being run by Khadi Gramodyog in Chhattisgarh during the last three \nyears; \n \n(b) \n \nthe quantum of allocated funds for the said schemes; and \n \n(c) \n \nthe details of number of beneficiaries including espec\nially that of Durg Parliamentary \nConstituency along with the state of the scheme expansion at present, district\n-\nwise?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF\n \nMICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nKhadi\n \nand Village Industries Commission (KVIC) is a statutory organization \nunder the Ministry of MSME and engaged in promoting and developing Khadi and Village \nIndustries through the following Schemes viz., i) Khadi Vikas Yojana, ii) Gramodyog Vikas \nYojana and \niii) Prime Minister’s Employment Generation Programme\n.\n \n \ni) Khadi Vikas Yojana (KVY):\n-\n \nKVIC provides assistance to Khadi Institutions for \ndevelopment of Khadi and generation of employment opportunities in the country under \nvarious components, viz., Market D\nevelopment Assistance to the Khadi Institutions and \nKhadi Artisans, Interest Subsidy Eligibility Certificate (ISEC) Scheme to ensure availability \nof working capital on reduced rate of interest, Work\n-\nsheds to Khadi artisans for providing \nthem sufficient spa\nce and congenial work environment, assistance to strengthen weak Khadi \nInstitutions, assistance for modernization of sales outlets of Khadi Institutions, training to the \nartisans, etc. \n \n \nii) Gramodyog Vikas Yojana\n \n(GVY):\n-\n \nGVY\n \nfocuses on development of Vill\nage based \nindustries under six verticals such as i) Agro Based & Food Processing Industry, ii) Mineral \nBased Industry, iii) Handmade Paper, Leather & Plastic Industry, iv) Rural Engineering and \nNew Technology Industry, v) Wellness & Cosmetics Industry, and\n \nvi) Service & Textile \nIndustry.  The following two verticals comes under GVY:\n \n \n1)\n \nHoney Mission (Beekeeping Programme) (\nAgro Based & Food Processing Industry)\n \nKVIC has launched Honey Mission in 2017\n-\n18 to supplement income of farmers, \nunemployed youth, adiva\nsis, etc.\n \n2)\n \nKumbhar\n \nSashaktikaran Programme \n(Mineral Based Industry)\n: \nUnder the \nprogramme, for up\n-\nliftment of the rural potters’ livelihood, skill up\n-\ngradation training \nand new home scale energy efficient equipment like electric pottery wheels, blunger, \npug \nmill, kiln etc. are provided for producing good quality products.\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \n \niii) \nPrime Minister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP)\n \nis a credit linked \nsubsidy scheme, for setting up of new micro\n-\nenterprises and to generate employment \nopportunities in rural as well as urban areas of the country through KVIC, State Khadi & \nVillage Industries Board (KVIB) and District Industries Centre (\nDIC).  General category \nbeneficiaries can avail Margin Money subsidy of 25% of the project cost in rural areas and \n15% in urban areas. For beneficiaries belonging to special categories such as \nSC/ST/Women/PH/Minorities/Ex\n-\nServicemen/NER, the Margin Money s\nubsidy is 35% in \nrural areas and 25% in urban areas. The maximum cost of projects is Rs.25 lakh in the \nmanufacturing sector and Rs.10 lakh in the service sector.\n \n \nScheme\n-\nwise fund disbursed for implementation and development of Khadi and \nVillage \nIndustries\n \nactivities in Chhattisgarh State, during last three years is as follows:\n \n(Rs. in lakh)\n \nSr. \nNo.\n \nName of Scheme\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n \n(Provisional)\n \nI.\n \nKhadi Vikas Yojana\n \n \n \n \ni\n \nModified Market Development Assistance \n(\nMMDA)\n \n134.73\n \n1649.09\n \n450.01\n \nii\n \nInterest Subsidy Eligibility Certificate\n \n(ISEC)\n \n42.36\n \n41.09\n \n28.69\n \niii\n \nKhadi Reform Development Programme \n(KRDP)\n \n-\n \n851.48\n \n137.40\n \niv\n \nWorkshed Scheme for Khadi Artisans\n \n-\n \n73.20\n \n45.00\n \nII.\n \nGramodyog Vikas Yojana\n \n \n \n \ni\n \nHoney Mission\n \n62.83\n \n55.98\n \n22.43\n \nii\n \nKumbhar Sashaktikaran Mission\n \n1.53\n \n0.11\n \n22.50\n \niii\n \nPolymer & Chemical based Industry \n(PCBI)\n \n-\n \n16.20\n \n23.40\n \niv\n \nRural Engineering\n \n-\n \n4.13\n \n \nv\n \nHand Made Paper & Fibre Industry \n(HMPFI)\n \n-\n \n5.40\n \n-\n \nvi\n \nVillage Industry Coord. (National Level \nAwareness Programme\n \nNLAP)\n \n-\n \n1.60\n \n0.00\n \nIII.\n \nPrime Minister’s Employment Generation \nProgramme (PMEGP) \n \n6784.52\n \n6098.28\n \n6062.77\n \n \n(c):\n \nDistrict\n-\nwise number of beneficiaries benefited under PMEGP in Chhattisgarh State \nincluding Durg District is placed at \nAnnexure\n.\n \n \n \n*******\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \nAnnexure\n \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (c) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 514 \nfor answer on 22.07.2021\n \n \nDistrict\n-\nwise list of beneficiaries under PMEGP in Chhattisgarh during last three years\n \n \n(Rs. in lakh)\n \nSr. \nNo.\n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |516", "t": "Waste to Energy Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-516 \nANSWERED ON-22/07/2021 \nWASTE TO ENERGY PLANTS \n \n516. SHRI DIPSINH SHANKARSINH RATHOD \n        SHRI MOHANBHAI KALYANJIKUNDARIYA \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether  the  Government  has  set  up  waste  to energy  plants in  Gujarat; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the  total  number  of  waste  to  energy  plants operating in Gujarat, district-wise; \n(d) whether the said plants are functioning to meet the desired objectives of their installation; \nand \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n(a) to (e) A total of 22 Waste -to-Energy plants with aggregate capacity of 22.58 MWeq have \nbeen set up by private developers in Gujarat to generate power/biogas/ BioCNG from \nagricultural, urban and industrial waste. The technologies for the recovery of Biog as/Power \nfrom these wastes are well established and with  sustained supply of requisite quality \nfeedstock and proper operation & maintenance on regular basis, these plants run properly and \nefficiently. The district-wise number of Waste to Energy plants inst alled in the State of \nGujarat, as on 30.06.2021 are given in Annexure. \n***** \n \n  \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred to in reply to parts (a) to (c) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo. 516 for 22/07/2021 regarding “Waste to Energy plants” \n \nDistrict-wise number and installed capacity of Waste-to-Energy plants set up in \nGujarat, as on 30.06.2021  \n \n District Total Installed capacity \n(MWeq) \nNumber of  Waste to \nEnergy plants \n1.  Anand  1.36 1 \n2.  Surendranagar  1.25 1 \n3.  Ahmedabad 6.06 8 \n4.  Bharuch 4.33 3 \n5.  Surat 3.96 3 \n6.  Rajkot 1.07 1 \n7.  Sabarkantha 1.89 2 \n8.  Valsad 0.21 1 \n9.  Kheda 1.50 1 \n10.  Kutch 0.95 1 \n Grand Total 22.58 22 \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |517", "t": "Floods in Bihar", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 517\nANSWERED ON 22.07.2021\nFLOODS IN BIHAR\n517. DR. ALOK KUMAR SUMAN\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is a fact that the floods are natural calamity which can not be controlled permanently despite\ntechnological advancement;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, especially in Bihar including Gopalganj district;\n(c) whether it is fact that the main reason for floods in the State of Bihar is increased discharge in rivers of\nNorth Bihar like Gandak, Burhi Gandak, Bagmati, Kamla, Kosi and Mahananda due to heavy rainfall in the\nupper catchment areas mainly lying in Nepal;\n(d) if so, the steps taken to control floods in North Bihar including Gopalganj district; and\n(e) the details of total number of representations received and the action taken for flood contol in Bihar\nincluding district Gopalganj?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI\n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT)\n(a) & (b) Floods are natural calamity that the country faces almost every year, in varying degrees of\nmagnitude. The occurrence of floods can be attributed to various factors, including wide variations in\nrainfall both in time and space with frequent departures from the normal pattern, inadequate carrying\ncapacities of rivers, river bank erosion and silting of river beds, landslides, poor natural drainage in flood\nprone areas, snowmelt and glacial lake out -bursts. Water Resource Department, Government of Bihar has\nreported severe erosion on the embankments of River Gandak in Gopalganj District of Bihar due to heavy\nrainfall in catchme nt area of River Gandak in Nepal during monsoon year of 2020. Several breaches in\nembankment along River Gandak occurred in Gopalganj District of Bihar at Bhaisahi Purrena Charki,\nSaramn, Bandhauli Shitalpur Faizullahpur Jamindari Bundh, and Baikunthpur Retired Line.\n(c ) Main reason for floods in the State of Bihar is on account of increased discharge in rivers of North Bihar\nlike Gandak, Burhi Gandak and Bagmati, Kamla, Kosi and Mahananda due to heavy rainfall in the upper\ncatchment areas which mainly li e in Nepal. The management of floods due to these rivers has been a\nconcern. The related issues are discussed in the existing Indo -Nepal bilateral mechanisms comprising of (i)\nJoint Ministerial Level Commission on Water Resources (JMCWR) (ii) Joint Committee on Water\nContd…P/2\n\n//2//\nResources (JCWR) (iii) Joint Standing Technical Committee (JSTC), (iv) Joint Committee on Gandak &\nKosi Projects (JCKGP) and  (v) Joint Committee on Inundation and Flood management (JCIFM).\nGovernment of India is having regular dialogue with the Government of Nepal for construction of dams on\nthese rivers for mutual benefit of the two countries which includes flood control.\n(d) & (e) Flood management schemes are formulated and implemented by concerned State Gov ernments as\nper their priority. The Union Government supplements the efforts of the States by providing technical\nguidance and also promotional financial assistance for management of floods in critical areas. However, as a\nnon-structural measure of ﬂood management, Central Water Commission (CWC) issues ﬂood forecasts for\nrivers common to Nepal and India  namely  Sharda,  Ghaghra, Rapti, Gandak, Burhi Gandak, Bagmati,\nKamala, Kosi and Mahananda using meteorological and hydro -meteorological data available in the website\nof Department of Hydrology & Meteorology, Government of Nepal.  At present, CWC has 40 level\nforecasting stations (for villages/towns on the bank of the  rivers) and 3 in ﬂow forecasting stations (for\nDams/ Barrages) in the State of Bihar. Durin g monsoon season of year 2020 ending 31 st December  2020, a\ntotal of 3223 forecasts have been issued for  Bihar State out of  which 3192 have been found to be within the\naccuracy limit which works out to 99.04%.\nCWC is maintaining a level forecasting station at Dumariaghat on Gandak River in Gopalganj\nDistrict of Bihar. This station ﬂowed in Extreme Flood Situation during 2020 when the River ﬂowed above\nHighest Flood Level and Severe Flood Situations were witnessed during the last 5 years including thi s year\nwhen the River flowed above Danger Level. CWC is formulating 5 day advisories for ﬂood using real time\nrainfall estimates and 5 day rainfall forecast through Numerical Weather Prediction models being shared by\nIMD on a seamless fashion. Mathematical model has been used for formulation of advisories and these\nadvisories are shared with stake holders using dedicated website.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |518", "t": "Solar Disconnect Photovoltaic Power Projects", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-518 \nANSWERED ON-22/07/2021 \n \nSOLAR DISCONNECT PHOTOVOLTAIC POWER PROJECTS \n \n518. SHRI KESINENI SRINIVAS \n \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the scheme of solar disconnect photovoltaic (PV) power projects for the islands \nof Andaman & Nicobar and Lakshadweep is still being operated; \n(b) if so, the details of developments made so far and the expected deadline for the scheme \nthereof; \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor; \n(d) whether any solar related schemes are under consideration for the North East; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to( c) The Ministry of New and Renewable Energy (MNRE)’s scheme for setting up of \ndistributed grid -connected solar PV power projects in Andaman & Nicobar & Lakshadweep \nwith capital subsidy from MNRE was available till 31.03.2021. The scheme is now closed for \nnew sanctions. \n \nA 20 MW solar PV project with 8 MWh battery energy storage system in Andaman & Nicobar \nIslands and a 1.95 MW solar PV project with 2.15 MWh battery energy storage system in \nLakshadweep Islands have been supported under the scheme. \n \n(d)&(e) A number of solar related schemes like CPSU Scheme Phase -II, PM-KUSUM and \nSolar Roof Top Scheme Phase -II are available for beneficiaries across India, including the \nNorth East. PM -KUSUM Scheme has special dispensation for special category States \nincluding North East by way of provision for higher incentives.  \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |519", "t": "Audit of National Sports Federations", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n  MINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRRED QUESTION NO. 519 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2021 \n   \nAudit of National Sports Federations \n \n519. DR. SHASHI THAROOR  : \n \n Will the Minister of YOUTH  AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate: \n \na) the details of the funding share of the National Sports Federations \nin the Union Government’s overall sports budget since 2018 (year-\nwise and federation-wise); \n \nb) whether the Government has undertaken an audit of all the \nNational Sports Federations on an annual basis and has uncovered \nany case of financial irregularities and if so, the details thereof; \n \nc) whether the Government has granted recognition to 54 national \nfederations till September, 2020 and if so, the details thereof; \n \nd) whether the usual recognition to National Sports Federations \n(NSFs) is not done annually; and  \n \n(e)   if so, the reasons for the Government to offer it only till September, \n2020? \n \n \n \n\nANSWER \nMINISTER FOR YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n (SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n \n(a) This Ministry supports the National Sports Federations  (NSFs) \nprimarily through ‘Assistance to NSFs Scheme’, the data of which is as \nbelow:-  \n \nScheme of Assistance to NSFs \n                                                                               (In ₹ crore)                                                                                     \nFinancial \nYear \nBE RE Actual Expenditure \n2017-18 302.18 302.18 277.68 \n2018-19 342.00 245.13 243.63 \n2019-20 245.00 300.85 300.85 \n2020-21 245.00 132.00 132.00 \n2021-22 280.00 - 34.93(till date) \n \n \nThe Ministry provides some assistance to NSFs through the Scheme of \nHuman Resource Development in Sports (HRDS) to support the training \nand development of their coaches and technical officials, based on \nproposals submitted by the NSFs from time to time. Details of  such \nfinancial support is placed at Annexure I. \n \nThe Ministry also provide some assistance through Khelo India National \nProgramme for Development of Sports Scheme, based on the proposal \nsubmitted by them for organizing of sports events. Details of financial \nsupport is placed at Annexure-II. \n \nFederation wise d etails of the funding share of the National Sports \nFederations in the Union Government’s overall sports budget since 2018 \nis placed at Annexure-III. \n \n(b)Since 2010, in order to ensure transparency and accountability in \nfunctioning of NSFs, this Ministry introduced a system of annual \nrecognition procedure, under which recognition is renewed on a year to \n\nyear basis, on submission of detailed documentation including audited \nannual accounts of NSFs. Accounts of the NSFs receiving grant of more \nthan ₹ 1crore in a year are subject to audit by the Comptroller & Auditor \nGeneral of India (CAG). \nAudit of select NSFs is done by Comp troller and Auditor General and \naudit findings are sent to NSFs for necessary action at their end. \nSettlement of observations and audit paras are taken up by the \nrespective NSFs with the Office of the CAG. \n \n(c)to(e) In the year 2020, the exercise of renewal of recognition of NSFs \nfor 2020 was held up due to outbreak of COVID -19 pandemic and \ncountry-wide lockdown to contain the spread of COVID-19. Considering \nthis unprecedented situation which had arisen due to COVID -19 \noutbreak and taking into account the interest of sports and \nsportspersons, this Ministry had issued letter dated 02.06.2020 \nrenewing the annual recognition for 2020 provisionally till 30.09.2020 of \nthe recognized NSFs. However, in compliance of the order  dated \n24.06.2020 passed by Hon’ble High Court of Delhi in Writ Petition (C) No. \n195 of 2010, the Ministry on 25.06.2020 had withdrawn the said letter \ndated 02.06.2020. Subsequently the Ministry appealed against the Order \nof the Delhi High Court before the  Hon’ble Supreme Court and the \nSupreme Court vide its order dated 17.9.2020 allowed the Ministry to \ngrant annual recognition to NSFs by informing Delhi High Court. After \nHon’ble Supreme Court’s Order, the Ministry renewed the recognition of \nNSFs on a case to case basis.     \n \n******************** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure – I  \nInformation as per para (a) of the Unstarred Question No. 519 to be \nanswered on 22.07.2021 asked by Dr. Shashi Tharoor \n \nStatement showing the details of funding share of the National Sports \nFederations under Human Resource Development in Sports Scheme \nduring the financial years 2017-18 to 2020-21 \n \nF.Y NSFs Amount  \n2017-18 All India \nChess \nFederation \n₹16.47 \nlakh \nAll India \nFootball \nFederation \n₹18.75 \nlakh \nBoxing \nFederation of \nIndia \n₹5.03 lakh \nTable Tennis \nFederation of \nIndia \n₹18.59 \nlakh \n2018-19 All India \nChess \nFederation \n₹16.10 \nlakh \nAll India \nFootball \nFederation \n₹6.25 lakh \nBoxing \nFederation of \nIndia \n₹38.80 \nlakh \nFencing \nAssociation \nof India \n₹4.92 lakh \n\nSpecial \nOlympic \nBharat \n₹45.15 \nlakh \nSquash \nRackets \nFederation of \nIndia \n₹1.63 lakh \nTable"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |52", "t": "Preservation of Indian Languages in Modern Age", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 52 \nANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2021 \n \nFOUR-LANING OF SONEPUR-ARERAJ NH \n \n*52.  SHRI RAJIV RANJAN SINGH ALIAS LALAN SINGH \n SHRI KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR:  \n \n Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a)  whether the Government has finalised the alignment of Sonepur -\nAreraj four-lane National Highway (NH) which is connecting \nPatna (Bihar) to Nepal border under Bharatmala Pariyojana and if \nso, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether the Government of Bihar has submitted a proposal to \nmake it through east side of Gandak river to con nect Buddhist \nCircuit in Vaishali and Kesaria and if so, the action taken by the \nGovernment thereon; and \n(c)  the time schedule fixed for completion of this project? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n (a) to (c)  A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (C) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 52 ON 22 ND JULY, 2021 ASKED BY \nSHRI RAJIV RANJAN SINGH ALIAS LALAN SINGH AND SHRI \nKAUSHALENDRA KUMAR REGARDING ‘ FOUR-LANING OF SONEPUR -\nARERAJ NH’ \n------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \n(a)  Yes Sir. Government of India has recently notified vide \nnotification no. S.O. 2738(E) dated 8.07.2021 the stretch from its \njunction with NH -139 near Patna (AIIMS) connecting Bakarpur, \nManikpur, Sehebganj, Areraj and terminating at its junction with \nNH-727 near Bettiah in the State of Bihar as National Highway \nNH-139W. The same has also been included in Bh aratmala \nPariyojana. \n(b) Yes Sir. This alignment mentioned above passes through eastern \nside of Gandak River to connect Buddhist  Circuit in Vaishali and \nKesaria. \n(c)  The Detailed Project Report (DPR) has been initiated. The \nprojects are targeted for award  in the year 2021 -2022 (Bakarpur \nto Areraj stretch) and 2022-23 (Areraj to Bettiah stretch).  \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |521", "t": "Maulana Azad National Fellowship", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 521 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2021 \n \nMAULANA AZAD NATIONAL FELLOWSHIP \n \n521. SHRI TALARI RANGAIAH: \n        SHRI T.R.V.S. RAMESH: \n            \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total number of fellowships sanctioned during  the last two years for the \nMaulana Azad National Fellowship (MANF), State-wise; and \n(b) the total funds sanctioned during the last two  years for the State of Andhra \nPradesh and Tamil Nadu for the purpose? \n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS  \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a):  The Ministry of Minority Affairs implements the Maulana Azad National \nFellowship (MANF) Scheme for educational empowerment of students belonging to \nsix notified minority communities i.e. Buddhist, Christian, Jain, Muslim, Sikh, \nZoroastrian (Parsi)  who pursue regular and full time M. Phil and Ph.D courses.  \nDuring the last seven years i.e. from 2014-15 to 2020-21, a total of 5,750 fellowships \nhave been sanctioned while during the last two years i.e. 2019-20 to 2020-21, a total \nof 1,726 fellowships have been sanctioned under the scheme  (both data \nprovisional). \n  \nThe details of State/UT-wise fellowship s sanctioned during the last two years i.e. \n2019-20 and 2020-21 are annexed. \n \n(b):  Funds are not sanctioned State/UT -wise under the Scheme. However , the \nfellowships amounting to Rs.  14,70,28,978/- and Rs. 8,14,90,253/- were disbursed  \nto the beneficiaries from the States of Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu during  the \nlast two years i.e. 2019-20 and 2020-21. \n***** \n \n  \n\nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. \n521 for 22.0 7.2021 asked by Shri Talari Rangaiah and Shri T.R.V.S. Ramesh  \nregarding ‘Maulana Azad National Fellowship'. \n \nThe details of State/UT -wise fellowships sanctioned during the last two \nyears i.e. 2019-20 and 2020-21 under Maulana Azad Fellowship Scheme \nS. \nNo. \nStates/UTs Fellowships  \nSanctioned \n1 Andhra Pradesh 7 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 6 \n3 Assam 52 \n4 Bihar 40 \n5 Chhattisgarh 2 \n6 Goa 2 \n7 Gujarat 19 \n8 Haryana 26 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 9 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 336 \n11 Jharkhand 15 \n12 Karnataka 25 \n13 Kerala 213 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 24 \n15 Maharashtra 63 \n16 Manipur 19 \n17 Meghalaya 12 \n18 Mizoram 4 \n19 Nagaland 8 \n20 Orissa 5 \n21 Punjab 106 \n22 Rajasthan 45 \n23 Sikkim 10 \n24 Tamil Nadu 26 \n25 Tripura 2 \n26 Uttar Pradesh 320 \n27 Uttrakhand 17 \n28 West Bengal 158 \n29 Andaman & Nicobar 0 \n30 Chandigarh 5 \n31 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 0 \n32 Daman & Diu 0 \n33 Delhi 126 \n34 Lakshadweep 0 \n35 Puducherry 4 \n36 Telangana 13 \n37 Ladakh 7 \nTotal 1726* \n* Data Provisional (process yet to be completed for 2020-21 due to non-conduct \nof the second cycle of examination). \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |522", "t": "CSR Projects under Namami Gange Programme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 522\nANSWERED ON 22.07.2021\nCSR PROJECTS UNDER NAMAMI GANGE PROGRAMME\n522. SHRI RITESH PANDEY\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) the status of CSR projects being offered under Namami Gange Programme; and\n(b) the promised financial outlay and the expenditure plan for the same?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI AND TRIBAL AFFAIRS\n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU)\n(a) & (b) Government of India has set -up Clean Ganga Fund (CGF), as a trust under the Indian Trust Act,\n1882, to allow resident Indians, Non Resident Indians ( NRIs) and Persons of Indian Origins (PIOs),\nCorporates (Public as well as private sector) to contribute towards the conservation of the river Ganga. The\ncontributions to Clean Ganga Fund falls within the purview of Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR)\nactivity as defined in Schedule VII to the Comp anies Act, 2013. The Corporate Sector Contribution to the\nClean Ganga Fund till 30th June, 2021 amounts to Rs.365.38 crore. The amounts accrued to the CGF can be\nused for different projects & activities as approved by the trust.\nThere is a dedicated website under Clean Ganga Fund on Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR),\ninviting corporate to participate in the Namami Gange Programme. The following activities are offered\nunder CSR:-\n1) Ghats Construction /Modification / Extension.\n2) Cleaning of Ghats.\n3) Providing Amenities at important Ghats.\n4) Crematoria Construction / Modification / Extension.\n5) Ganga Gram.\n6) Bioremediation of Nallas & Drains.\n7) Information Education Communication (IEC) Activities.\n8) River Surface Cleaning using Trash Skimmers\n9) Solid Waste Management.\n10) Tree Plantation.\nThe following projects are financed by the Corporate under the CSR initiatives:-\nI. Development of Har ki Pauri Complex in Haridwar at a cost of Rs.34.00 crore under CSR\ninitiative of Indian Oil Corporation Limited;\nII. Development of Karnwas Banger Ghat at a cost of Rs.3.21 crore under CSR initiative of\nState Bank of India;\nIII. Development of Ghat & Crematoria at Badrinath & Gangotri at a cost of Rs.26.64 crore\nunder CSR initiative of Indo-Rama Trust.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |524", "t": "Awareness about Urban Poverty Alleviation Schemes", "head": "O.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 524 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 22, 2021 \n \nAWARENESS ABOUT URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION SCHEMES \n \nNO.   524.  SHRI SADASHIV KISAN LOKHANDE: \n   \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate:- \n \na)   whether there is a lack of awareness amongst the u rban poor \n about the advantages and efficacies of the urban p overty \n alleviation schemes; \nb)   if so, the reasons for not comprehensively publici sing these \n schemes; and \nc)   the details of various steps taken to create aware ness \n amongst the urban poor in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n    (SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE)  \n(a)   No, Sir.    \n \n(b)   Does not arise.  \n \n(c)   Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs is implement ing the \n“Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana- National Urban Livelih oods \nMission (DAY-NULM)”, through respective States/ UTs , to \nreduce poverty and vulnerability of the urban poor households \nby enabling them to access self-employment and skilled wage  \n \n...2/- \n\n \n-2- \n \nemployment opportunities, resulting in an appreciab le \nimprovement in their livelihoods on a sustainable b asis. The \nmission aims at building strong grass root level in stitutions of \nthe urban poor, providing shelters equipped with es sential \nservices to the urban homeless persons and addressi ng \nlivelihood concerns of the urban street vendors by facilitating \naccess to suitable spaces, institutional credit, so cial security, \netc. \n \nAs per the Mission Guidelines, 3 percent of its all ocation can \nbe utilized for the purpose of IEC (Information, Ed ucation & \nCommunication).For creating awareness Ministry has advised \nthe States/ UTs to undertake IEC activities which i nclude \nbroadcasting radio jingles in Hindi and regional la nguages \nthrough radio; newspaper advertisement/ hoardings/ banners; \ndistribution of Leaflets/Pamphlets/Brochure; Worksh ops, \nConferences and training; use of social media platf orms and \nrallies by Self Help Groups and their federations i n the urban \nareas. In order to complement their efforts, Minist ry organizes \nMelas / exhibitions at Centre/State/City level to extend the \noutreach of the Mission and showcase its initiatives. \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |526", "t": "Promotion of Employees", "head": "O.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 526 \nDUE FOR ANSWER ON JULY 22, 2021 \n \nPROMOTION OF EMPLOYEES \n \nNo. 526  SHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT: \n \nWith the Minister of Housing and Urban Affairs be pleased to state: \n \n(a) Whether the promotion of employees particularly  the \n employees of  OBC category is pending in Directora te of \n Printing, and \n \n(b) If so, the details thereof and the reasons ther efor including the \n date  from which these pending promotions are like ly to be \n done? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \n HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE)  \n \n(a) & (b): Yes, Sir. Promotion cases of all categor ies of employees \nincluding OBC category, were  \ni)  put on hold in 2017 by the Directorate of Printing in \nthe Government of India Presses due to Government of \nIndia decision for rationalisation/merger of 17 \nGovernment of India Presses (GIPs) into 5 GIPs. \nSubsequently, the promotions in the Government of \nIndia Presses have been commenced and done in GIP \nNashik. Action is now being taken for conducting  \n...2/- \n\n \nDepartmental Promotion Committees (DPCs) in the \nother Presses where promotions are pending viz. GIP  \nMinto Road (New Delhi), GIP Ring Road (New Delhi) \nand GIP Temple Street (Kolkata). The pending \npromotions are likely to be done within a period of  six \nmonths. \nii)  In so far as the post of Manager in the Directorate  of \nPrinting Headquarter (HQ) is concerned, Hon’ble \nCentral Administrative Tribunal (CAT), Principal \nBench, New Delhi vide Order dated 29.1.2020 has \ngranted an interim stay on the operation of inter-s e \nseniority until further orders. Directorate of Prin ting \nhave filed application for vacation of stay which i s yet \nto come up for hearing. Hence, process for promotio n \nin this post will be made upon vacation of stay by the \nHon’ble Tribunal. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |527", "t": "Construction of New NH", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 527 \nANSWERED ON 22ND  JULY, 2021  \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF NEW NH \n527.    SHRI ABDUL KHALEQUE: \n \n            Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has received a proposal dated 11th   \n March 2020 from public representative regarding construction \n of a new National Highway (NH) connecting Goroimari in \n Barpeta district with Kabaitari in Bongaigaon district;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details and the present status thereof along with the \n action taken by the Government in this regard;  \n \n(c)  whether the Government proposes conversion of State \n Highway No. 2 from Barpeta to North Salmara via Jania and \n Lengtisinga into National Highway; and \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) & (b):   Yes sir, the proposal dated 11th   March 2020 regarding \ndeclaration of a new National Highway (NH) connecting Goroimari in \nBarpeta district with Kabaitari in Bongaigaon district  was received \nin the Ministry. The Ministry considers declaration of some state \nroads as new NHs from time to time based on requirement of \nconnectivity, inter-se priority and availability of funds.  \n \n(c) & (d):    No proposal for conversion of State Highway No. 2 from \nBarpeta to North Salmara via Jania and Lengtisinga into National \nHighway has been received from State Government. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |528", "t": "Cities Selected under AMRUT Scheme", "head": "Page 1 of 5 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 528 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 22, 2021 \n \nCITIES SELECTED UNDER AMRUT SCHEME \n \nNO. 528.       SHRI MANOJ KOTAK: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be p leased to \nstate:  \n(a)  the names of the cities of Maharashtra selecte d under Atal \n Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT) \n Scheme; \n(b)  the funds allocated, sanctioned and released u nder this Scheme \n to  the State of Maharashtra during the last three  years, year-\n wise; \n(c)  the details of authority assigned with the res ponsibility to monitor \n the utilization of these funds; and \n(d)  the details of the completion status of works under the schemes, \n city-wise? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nHOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a) A total of 44 cities of the State of Maharashtr a are covered Under \n Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transforma tion (AMRUT), \n list of which is given at ANNEXURE – I. \n \n\nPage 2 of 5 \n \n(b) The total approved State Annual Action Plan (SA AP) size of State \n of Maharashtra under AMRUT, for the entire Mission  period, \n amounts to \u00017,759.32 crore, which includes Central Assistance \n (CA) of \u00013,534 crore. So far, CA amounting to \u00012,776.25 crore \n have been released to the Government of Maharashtr a for AMRUT \n projects. Under AMRUT, funds are released under th e heads of \n Administrative and Office Expanses (A&OE), Reform Incentives, \n GIS based Master Plan and Local Area Plan – Town P lanning \n Scheme (LAP-TPS). Funds released to Government of \n Maharashtra under AMRUT during the last 3 years, y ear-wise, is \n given below: \n(in Rs. crore) \nHead Year 2018-19 Year 2019-20 \n Year 2020-21 \nAMRUT Projects 825.71 - 1,244.21 \nA&OE 23.52 32.68 - \nReform Incentives 77.36 42.08 - \nGIS based Master Plan  - - - \nLAP-TPS - 0.4 0.8 \nTotal:  926.59 75.16 1,245.01 \n \n(c)  Under AMRUT, States are empowered to plan, app rove and \n implement the projects.  Respective State Governme nts monitor \n the implementation of the Mission and utilization of the funds \n under AMRUT.  A State level High Powered Steering Committee \n (SHPSC) chaired by the State Chief Secretary monit ors the \n progress of the implementation of the Mission. In addition, \n Independent Review and Monitoring Agency (IRMA) ha ve been \n appointed by each State/ Union Territory.  \n(d)  The city-wise information on project completio n status is given at \n ANNEXURE-II. \n***** \n\nPage 3 of 5 \n \nANNEXRUE – I REFERRED TO IN ANSWER TO PART (a) OF L OK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 528 FOR ANSWER ON 22 JULY 20 21 \nASKED BY SHRI MANOJ KOTAK: \n \nList of 44 cities of the State of Maharashtra, covered Under Atal \nMission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT) \n1 Achalpur 16  Ichalkaranji 31  Parbhani \n2 Ahmadnagar 17  Jalgaon 32  Pimpri Chinchwad \n3 Akola 18  Jalna 33  Pune \n4 Ambarnath 19  Kalyan Dombivali 34  Sangli-Miraj Kupwad \n5 Amravati(MH) 20  Kolhapur 35  Satara \n6 Aurangabad(MH)  21  Latur 36  Shirdi \n7 Badlapur 22  Malegaon 37  Solapur \n8 Barshi 23  Mira Bhayandar 38  Thane \n9 Bhiwandi 24  Nagpur 39  Udgir \n10  Bhusawal 25  Nanded Waghala 40  Ulhasnagar \n11  Bid 26  Nandurbar 41  Vasai-Virar City \n12  Chandrapur 27  Nashik 42  Wardha \n13  Dhule 28  Navi Mumbai 43  Yavatmal \n14  Gondiya 29  Osmanabad 44  Greater Mumbai \n15  Hinganghat 30  Panvel \n \n\nPage 4 of 5 \n \n \nANNEXRUE – II REFERRED TO IN ANSWER TO PART (d) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 528 FOR ANSWER ON 22 JULY 20 21 \nASKED BY SHRI MANOJ KOTAK: \nSl. \nNo.  City \nWork \nCompleted \nContract \nAwarded Total \nNos. Amount  Nos.  Amount Nos.  Amount \n1 Achalpur 5 27.78 - - 5 27.78 \n2 Ahmadnagar 2 2.88 3 273.92 5 276.8 \n3 Akola 3 4.5 2 192.72 5 197.22 \n4 Ambarnath 3 4.55 2 82.17 5 86.72 \n5 Amravati(MH) 4 119.31 1 81.87 5 201.18 \n6 Aurangabad(MH) 3 4.08 - - 3 4.08 \n7 Badlapur 3 3.19 2 65.2 5 68.39 \n8 Barshi 3 4.5 1 112.4 4 116.9 \n9 Bhiwandi 3 4.55 - - 3 4.55 \n10 Bhusawal 3 4.47 1 90.84 4 95.31 \n11 Bid 3 2.94 2 280.43 5 283.37 \n12 Chandrapur 3 4.29 2 313.88 5 318.17 \n13 Dhule 3 5.02 2 300.67 5 305.69 \n14 Gondiya 3 1.46 1 134.07 4 135.53 \n15 Hinganghat 2 1.58 3 143.78 5 145.36 \n16 Ichalkaranji 3 4.38 1 70.3 4 74.68 \n17 Jalgaon 3 4.49 2 448.93 5 453.42 \n18 Jalna 3 2.93 1 68.59 4 71.52 \n19 Kalyan Dombivali 3 4.5 3 480.4 6 484.9 \n20 Kolhapur 3 4.5 2 185.57 5 190.07 \n21 Latur 4 50.64 1 139.42 5 190.06 \n22 Malegaon 4 84.51 1 98.43 5 182.94 \n23 Mira Bhayandar 3 4.55 2 298.61 5 303.16 \n24 Nagpur 3 4.47 1 273.78 4 278.25 \n25 Nanded Waghala 1 24.18 3 4.5 4 28.68 \n26 Nandurbar 3 4.84 - - 3 4.84 \n27 Nashik 4 33.15 1 53.46 5 86.61 \n28 Navi Mumbai 3 4.5 1 132.87 4 137.37 \n29 Osmanabad 3 72.29 1 1 4 73.29 \n30 Panvel 4 55.16 1 173.27 5 228.43 \n31 Parbhani 4 108.18 - - 4 108.18 \n32 Pimpri Chinchwad  3 4.5 3 391.88 6 396.38 \n33 Pune 2 2.5 2 237.95 4 240.45 \n34 Sangli-Miraj 3 4.5 1 104.66 4 109.16 \n\nPage 5 of 5 \n \nSl. \nNo.  City \nWork \nCompleted \nCo"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |529", "t": "Productivity of Irrigated Agriculture", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI,\nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 529\nANSWERED ON 22.07.2021\nPRODUCTIVITY OF IRRIGATED AGRICULTURE\n529. DR. T. SUMATHY (A) THAMIZHACHI THANGAPANDIAN\nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the World Bank has sanctioned a loan amount of Rs.3300 crores for Tamil Nadu to increase the\nproductivity of irrigated agriculture for sub-basin stakeholders in a sustainable water resource management\nframework and if so, the details thereof;\n(b) the details of the disbursement of loan as on date for the said project;\n(c) whether it is a fact that the State component for the said project would be around Rs.1 000 crores and if\nso, the details of the amount disbursed and spent till 31 March 2021; and\n(d) whether any amount has been sanctioned by the Government in this regard, if so, the details thereof and\nif not, the reasons therefor?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI AND TRIBAL AFFAIRS\n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU)\n(a) World Bank has sanctione d a loan of USD 318 Million (Rs. 2073 crores i.e . 70 % of the total project\ncost) for the Tamil Nadu Irrigated Agriculture Modernization project (TNIAMP IBRD Loan No.8797 -IN)\nfor a period of 7 Years (2018-2025) to improve the productivity of irrigated agriculture and water resources\nmanagement.\n(b) Disbursement of USD 133.18 Million has been made up to 07.07.2021 from the IBRD loan.\n(c) The state counterpart funding for the project is USD 136 Million which is 30% of the project cost. As\non 31.03.2021, the disbursement for counterpart funding is Rs.409.88 Crores.\n(d) Since it is a state sector project, question of sanction of funds by the Union Government does not arise.\n*****"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |53", "t": "Dealing in Crypto Currency", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUN-STARRED QUESTION NO. 53 \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, JULY 19, 2021/ ASHADHA 28, 1943 (SAKA) \n DEALING IN CRYPTO CURRENCY \nQUESTION \n53. SHRI LAVU SRI KRISHNA DEVARAYALU: \n      SHRIMATI CHINTA ANURADHA: \n      SHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY: \n      SHRI POCHA BRAHMANANDA REDDY: \n      SHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI: \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS                                          कारपोर े ट कार्य मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:                                                \n(a) whether the Government has made it mandatory for Indian companies to \ndisclose their dealings in crypto-currency or digital currency; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, and \n(c) the details of the number of companies that have disclosed their dealing in \ncrypto-currency with MCA as on date?                     \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF STATISTICS \nAND PROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION; MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT \nCHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING; AND MINISTER OF STATE FOR \nCORPORATE AFFAIRS \n[RAO INDERJIT SINGH] \n(a)&(b): Ministry of Corporate Affairs has amended Schedule III to the Companies \nAct, 2013 effective from 1 st April, 2021 vide Notification dated 24.03.2020 which \nmandates various disclosures by the companies in their Financial Statements \nincluding the following disclosures with regard to virtual currency/ Crypto currency \ntransactions undertaken by them during the financial year:- \ni. Profit or loss on transactions involving Crypto Currency or Virtual Currency; \nii. Amount of Currency held as at the reporting date; \niii. Deposits or advances from any person for the purpose of trading or investing in \nCrypto Currency/ Virtual Currency. \n(c): The filings of Financial Statements for the Financial Year 2021 -22 are not yet \ndue. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |530", "t": "Construction of Multistoried Flats", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 530 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 22, 2021 \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF MULTISTORIED FLATS \n \nNO. 530. SHRI UPENDRA SINGH RAWAT: \nSHRI JANARDAN SINGH SIGRIWAL: \n \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware that a large number  of \nmultistoried flats have been constructed/are under \nconstruction by various private builders in the cou ntry \nespecially in Uttar Pradesh, NCT of Delhi and metro cities many \nof which have been constructed in a very haphazard manner \nwithout any proper planning/provision of faulty inf rastructure \ninside these flats or in violation of building norm s of the \nGovernment and if so, the details thereof; \n(b)  whether these flats are being built with proper san ction and by \nfollowing all building norms as stipulated by Gover nment and if \nso, the details thereof along with the entire proce dure and the \nagencies involved in the process of construction/au thorization \nof these flats; and \n(c)  whether  the Government is taking any measures to e nsure \nthat all necessary building norms are followed and necessary \nsanctions and approvals are properly taken and impl emented \nby the concerned private builders to promote transp arency and \nensure proper planned development in the country an d if so, \nthe details thereof? \n \n…2/- \n \n \n\n \n \n-2- \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n \n(a) and (b)  ‘Land’ and ‘Colonization’ are State su bjects. The matter \nfalls within the domain of the respective State and  UT Governments \nand related data is available with them.   \n However, in respect of the National Capital Territ ory of Delhi \n(NCTD), whose land matters are under the jurisdicti on of the Central \nGovernment, multistoried apartments are constructed  with the \napproval of building plans by the Urban Local Body concerned as per \nprovisions of Unified Building Bye Laws-2016 and Ma ster Plan of \nDelhi-2021. In case any violation is noticed, actio n is taken under \nthe provisions of Delhi Municipal Corporation Act, 1956 or New Delhi \nMunicipal Council Act, 1994, as the case may be. \n In respect of the Development Area notified under the Delhi \nDevelopment Act, 1957, the Delhi Development Author ity (DDA) \nallows construction only when all necessary sanctio ns and \npermissions have been obtained. DDA has prepared a Standard \nOperating Procedure (SOP) for taking action against  unauthorized \nconstructions from time to time.   \n \n(c)  The responsibility to ensure implementation of  the building \nnorms vests with the respective Urban Development A uthority/ \nUrban Local Body, which function under the jurisdic tion of the \nStates/ UTs. \nFor the guidance of the States/UTs, the Ministry ha s issued \nModel Building Bye Laws, 2016 and Urban & Regional Development \nPlans Formulation and Implementation Guidelines (URDPFI), 2014.   \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |531", "t": "Awareness about Road Safety", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.531 \n        ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2021 \n  \nAWARENESS ABOUT ROAD SAFETY \n  \n 531. SHRI GAURAV GOGOI: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n           औ               \n  \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a)      the total number of road accidents that took place in the \ncountry during each of the last three years; and \n  \n(b)      the measures being taken by the Government to increase \nawareness about road safety? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a)     As per the data received from Police Departments of various \nStates/UTs the total  number of road accidents that took place in the \ncountry for the calendar year, starting from 2017 to 2019 are shown in \nthe table given below: \n  \nYear Total Road Accidents \n2017 4,64,910 \n2018 4,67,044 \n2019 4,49,002 \n\n \n(b)      To increase awareness about road safety, the following steps \nhave been taken by the Government:     \n  \n(i)      The Ministry of Road Transport & Highways has notified “Member \nof Parliaments’ Road Safety Committee” in each district of the country \nto promote awareness amongst road users under the Chairmanship of \nHon’ble Member of Parliament (Lok Sabha) from the district. \n  \n(ii)    The Ministry imple ments a scheme for undertaking publicity \nmeasures and awareness campaigns on road safety to create \nawareness among road users through the electronic media, print \nmedia, NGOs and other organizations, etc. \n  \n(iii)     Observance of National Road Safety Month /Week every year \nacross the country for spreading awareness and strengthening road \nsafety. \n  \n(iv)  A Certification Course for Road Safety Auditors has been \ncommenced in Indian Academy of Highway Engineers (IAHE). \n  \n(v)   Issue of Guidelines for protection of Good Samaritans and draft \nrules as per MV (Amendment) Act, 2019 has been published. \n  \n***** \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |532", "t": "Promotion of Khadi and Village Industires", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 532\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2021\n \n \nPromotion of Khadi and Village Industires\n \n \n532. \n \nSHRI JUAL ORAM: \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND\n \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n(a) \n \nwhether the steps have been taken by the Government to promote Khadi and Village \nIndustries in different States; \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details of centrally sponsored schemes implemented in States; \n \n(c) \n \nthe funds ea\nrmarked for the purpose; and \n \n(d) \n \nthe extent of employment generated by such schemes in the Khadi and Village \nIndustries?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a)\n:\n \nYes Sir.  Khadi\n \nand Village Industries Commission (KVIC) is playing a pivotal role \nto promote Khadi and Village Industries in the different States through the implementation of \nKhadi Vikas Yojana (KVY), Gramodyog Vikas Yojana (GVY), Prime Minister’s \nEmployment Generation\n \nProgramme (PMEGP), and Scheme of Fund for Regeneration of \nTraditional Industries (SFURTI). \n \n \n \n \nKVY provides support to the Khadi sector through market development assistance, \ninterest subsidy on working capital, creation of physical infrastructure, modern\nization of sales \noutlets and training.  GVY focuses on development of Village based industries.  PMEGP is a \ncredit linked subsidy scheme, for setting up of new micro\n-\nenterprises and to generate \nemployment opportunities in rural as well as urban areas of th\ne country.  Under SFURTI, \ntraditional Khadi industries and artisans are organized into clusters, to make them more \nproductive and competitive through setting up of Common Facilities Centre, procurement of \nnew machinery, training, etc.  \n \n \n \n \nThe scheme\n-\nwise \nfunds earmarked for the last three years and current financial year \nand the cumulative employment generated under the schemes are placed at \nAnnexure\n.\n \n \n(b)\n:\n \nMinistry of MSME does not implement any Centrally Sponsored Schemes in the \nStates.  \n \n \n(c)\n \n&\n \n(d):\n    \n \nDoes not arise.\n \n \n* * *\n \n \n \n\n \nAnnexure\n \n \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 532 \nfor answer on 22.07.2021\n \n \n \nScheme\n-\nwise funds earmarked for the last three years and current financial year 2020\n-\n21\n \n \n                                                                                                              \n(Rs. in crore)\n \nName of the Scheme\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \nRE\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \nRE\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \nRE\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \nBE\n \nKhadi Vikas Yojana\n \n-\n \n366.46\n \n258.44\n \n250.00\n \nGramodyog Vikas Yojana\n \n75.00\n \n82.92\n \n37.35\n \n50.00\n \nPrime Minister’s Employment Generation \nProgramme\n \n2118.80\n \n2464.44\n \n1905.80\n \n2000.00\n \nScheme of Fund for Regeneration of \nTraditional Industries\n#\n \n86.15\n \n185.00\n \n350.00\n \n170.00\n \n         \n#Total fund allocated under SFURTI for all Nodal \nAgencies including KVIC.\n \n \nCumulative employment under Khadi & Village Industries activities, including PMEGP, is \n158.68 lakh persons.\n \n \nUnder SFURTI, nearly 2.6 lakh traditional artisans have been supported with financial \nassistance.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |533", "t": "Scheme for Auto Industry", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 533 \nANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2021 \n \nSCHEME FOR AUTO INDUSTRY \n \n533.  SHRI S. MUNISWAMY.: \n   \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any plan to launch a new scheme for \nauto industry in the country; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the Government has received any proposal in this regard \nand if so, the details thereof; and \n(d)   the time by which it is likely to be implemented along with the \nestimated funds likely to be allocated? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) to (d) The Government, on 11th November, 2020, has approved the \nProduction-Linked Incentive (PLI) Scheme in 10 specified key sectors for \nenhancing India’s Manufacturing Capabilities and Enhancing Exports - \nAtmanirbhar Bharat, including the Automobile and Auto Components \nsector. This is within the purview of the Ministry of Heavy Industries. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |534", "t": "Credit Linked Capital Subsidy Scheme", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 534\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON\n \n22.07.2021\n \n \nCredit Linked Capital Subsidy Scheme\n \n \n534\n.    \n \nSHRI NALIN KUMAR KATEEL:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nthe aims and objectives of Credit Linked Capital Subsidy Scheme (CLCSS) \n–\n \nTechnology Upradation Scheme;\n \n(b)\n \nthe manner in which the scheme has become successful for MSME sector;\n \n(c)\n \nthe\n \nfunds released to MSMEs during 2019\n-\n20 and the number of MSMEs benefitted;\n \n(d)\n \nwhether the total outlay to all the Technology upgradation and quality certification \nschemes has been slashed from Rs.683.91 crore to Rs. 330.31 crore in 2021\n-\n22;\n \n(e)\n \nif so, the manner\n \nin which the Government is planning to manage the scheme with \ndepleted allocation; and\n \n(f)\n \nwhether the Government proposes to expand CLCSS to benefit more small businesses \nand if so, the details thereof?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM \nENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI NARAYAN RANE)\n \n \n(a) & (b)\n:\n \n \nCredit Linked Capital Subsidy (CLCS) component of Credit Linked Capital \nSubsidy \n–\n \nTechnology Upgradation (CLCS\n-\nTU) Scheme was aimed at facilitating technology \nup\n-\ngradation in Micro and Small Enterprises (MSEs) b\ny providing an upfront capital subsidy \nof 15 percent to the MSEs on institutional finance availed by them for induction of well\n-\nestablished and improved technology in the specified sub\n-\nsectors/products approved under \nthe scheme. The scheme has benefitted a\nround 87,989 units by providing them with total \ncapital subsidy of Rs 5685.87 crore since its inception in the year 2001\n-\n02. \n \n \n(c)\n:\n \nDuring the period 2019\n-\n20, funds to the tune of Rs 554.69 crore\n \nwere released to the \nimplementing agencies/banks for settling the subsidy claims submitted by 7779 Micro and \nSmall Enterprises. \n \n \n(d) to (f)\n:\n \nTechnology Upgradation and Quality Certification is the Budget head under \nwhich funds are allocated for Credit Li\nnked Capital Subsidy & Technology Up\n-\n \ngradation \nScheme. This scheme has two components namely Credit Linked Capital Subsidy \nComponent and Technology Upgradation Scheme Component. The first component i.e Credit \nLinked Capital Subsidy Component, as approved \nby the Government, was in operation till \n31.03.2020 only. The allocation under this component in FY 2020\n-\n21 was meant for settling \nthe subsidy claims submitted till 31.03.2020.  As bulk of the subsidy claims under the scheme \nhas been liquidated during prev\nious years, the allocation for FY 2021\n-\n22 has been reduced. \n \n \n*\n***\n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |535", "t": "Repairing Work on Borigumma-Jeypore NH", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 535 \nANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2021 \n  \nREPAIRING WORK ON BORIGUMMA-JEYPORE NH \n  \n535. SHRI SAPTAGIRI SANKAR ULAKA: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n           औ               \n \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the repair work on dilapidated stretch from Borigumma \nto Jeypore on NH-26 has been completed; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) the details and the present status of land acquisition for Koraput \nbypass on NH-26; \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to realign the said project in \ncase of problem in land acquisition and if so, the details thereof; \nand \n \n(e) the time by which the Koraput bypass project is likely to be \ncompleted? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a)&(b)  The work of widening to 2 -lane with paved shoulders from \nBorigumma to Jeypore  from km 319/280 to km 345/480 of   NH-26 in \na length of 26.20 km is in progress with present physical progress of \n48%.  Repairs have been done in this stretch under the contract of \nthe widening work and the same is in traffic worthy condition.  \n  \n(c) to (e )  Indian Railways raised the issue of shifting of approved \nalignment of the bypass as the proposed ROW is falling in Railway \nland. The issue raised by Railways is being verified at site jointly by \nthe Railways and local district administration of Koraput.  At \npresent there is no proposal to change the approved alignment. The \nwork of Koraput bypass will be taken up after comp letion of the \n\nland acquisition depending on inter -se priority and availability of \nresources.     \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |536", "t": "Removal of Physical Toll Booths", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 536 \nANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2021 \nREMOVAL OF PHYSICAL TOLL BOOTHS \n536. SHRI PRABHUBHAI NAGARBHAI VASAVA: \n  Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to remove all physical toll booths in \nthe country within a period of one year and also complete the \nimplementation of FASTag system during this period;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the details of work undertaken in this regard so far and the time by \nwhich it is likely to be completed; and  \n(d) the other changes likely to be made on the vehicles besides \ncompulsory airbags for the front seats of the cars? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n(a) to (c) Government has declared all lanes of fee plazas on National \nHighways as “FASTag lane of the fee plaza”  for collection of user fee \nthrough digital payment via FASTag w.e.f. midnight of 15th/16th February \n2021. At present, a ll fee plazas on National Highways are fully equipped \nwith FASTag system. After declaration of all lanes as FASTag Lanes, the \noverall FASTag penetration has reached approximately 96%, from 80% \non 14th Febru ary, 2021. As on 14th July, 2021, more than 3.54 crore \nFASTags have been issued. \n  Improvement in process/technology for collection of user fee is a \ncontinuous process. Efforts are also made towards adoption of new \ntechnology & process. \n(d) Mandatory provision of an airbag for the driver and co -driver has been \nmade under the Central Motor Vehicle Rules, 1989. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |537", "t": "Funds for Welfare of Minority Communities", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 537 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22.07.2021 \n \nFunds for Welfare of Minority Communities \n \n537.  SHRI M. BADRUDDIN AJMAL:  \n SHRIMATI PRATIMA MONDAL: \n \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of expenditure incurred on schemes meant for welfare of minority communities \nalong with the funds allocated and released for these schemes during the last three years, \nscheme-wise;  \n(b) the details of ratio of benefit obtained by the various communities; \n(c) whether percentage of increase in funds for minority welfare is very low as compared with \nother Ministries; \n(d)  if so, whether this Ministry ever approach with request of increase in funds; and \n(e) if so, the details and the response thereof, if not, the reason therefor? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n(SH. MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) Scheme-wise details of the fund allocated and released under various schemes to meet the \nscheme objective during the last three years i.e.  2018-19, 2019-20 and 2020-21 is at Annexure. \n \n(b) As per the 2011 census, the percentages of six notified minority communities out of the total \nMinority population is Buddhists -3.61%, Christians -11.9%, Jains -1.9%, Muslims -73.66%, \nParsis-0.02 and Sikhs -8.91. The M inistry maintains data of community wise beneficiaries \nonly for the scholarship schemes . The ratio of beneficiaries in the scholarship schemes  \namong the minority communities  during the last three years is Muslims 75.58%, Chr istians \n12.15%, Sikhs 8.19%, Buddhists 2.80%, Jains 1.27% and Parsis 0.01%. \n \n \n(c) to (e) The allocation of Ministry of Minority Affairs increased over the years by 7% from Rs. \n4700.00 crore in 2018-19 to Rs.5029.00 crore in 2020-21.  \n \n \n \n****** \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure \n \nStatement referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo.537 for answer on 22.07.2021 \nSl. Name of the Scheme/Project/ Programme 2018-19 to 2020-21 \nBudget \nallocation \nActual \nExpenditure \n1 2  3  4 \n1 Contribution to equity of NMDFC 435.03 435.00 \n2 Grants-in-aid to Maulana Azad Education Foundation. 293.76 144.42 \n3 Research /studies, monitoring & evaluation of \ndevelopment schemes for Minorities including \npublicity 130.00 89.50 \n4 Merit cum means Scholarship 1163.51 943.14 \n5  Free Coaching & Allied Scheme  for minorities  139.00 77.02 \n6 Grants in aid to state Channelizing Agencies (SCA) \nengaged for implementation in NMDFC programme. 5.00 4.90 \n7 Pradhan  Mantri Jan Vikas Karyakaram(Earlier MsDP) 3880.23 3946.29 \n8 Pre-matric scholarship  3798.82 3826.59 \n9 Post matric scholarship  1517.66 1296.48 \n10 Secretariat  70.40 74.40 \n11 Maulana Azad National Fellowship for minority  \nstudents 383.00 271.35 \n12 Qaumi Wakf Boards taraqquiti Scheme. 40.94 23.82 \n13 Sahari Waqf Sampati Vikas Yojana(Earlier GIA to \nWaqf) 9.32 9.32 \n14 Scheme for Leadership Development of Minority \nWomen. 33.00 26.93 \n15 Interest subsidy on educational loan for overseas \nstudies for students belonging to minority \ncommunities. 92.00 79.62 \n16 Scheme for containing population decline of small \nminority community.  12.00 11.86 \n17 Skill Development Initiative 690.00 541.28 \n18 Support for students clearing Prelims conducted by \nUPSC, SSC, State Public    Services Commissions \netc. 26.00 18.88 \n19 Upgrading Skill and Training in Traditional Arts /Crafts \nfor Development (USTTAD) 170.00 142.48 \n20 Hamari Dharohar 14.20 6.89 \n21 Nai Manzil 280.00 188.01 \n22 National Commission for Minorities 28.51 25.26 \n23 Special officer for Linguistic Minorities 6.33 5.41 \n24 Haj Management 186.29 167.86 \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |538", "t": "Shipping Channels", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.538\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 22ND JULY, 2021\nSHIPPING CHANNELS\n538. PROF. SAUGATA RAY:\nWill the Minister of PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS be pteased to slate:\nFn-{, +( cfo{€{ +{ rcqr{i d*\n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to set up new shipping channels for\ntransportation of goods on cost efficient manner across lhe couniry;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whelher any State Government has sent new proposal in this regard; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and the action taken thereon?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PORTS, SHIPPING AND WATERWAYS\n(SHRI SHRIPAD NAIK)\n(a) & (b) lnland Waterways Authority of lndia (lWAl) is mandated for regulation and\ndevelopment of National Waterways for the purpose of shipping and navigation under\nthe lnland Waterways Authority of lndia Act, 1985. To promote intand water transporl\n(lWT) in the country, 111 wateruJays (including 5 existing NWs and 106 new wateMays)\nhave been declared as National Waterways (NWs) under the National WateMays Act,\n2016 spread over 24 States in the country which came into effect from 12h April, 2016.\nBased on the outcome of techno-economic feasibility and Detailed Project Reports\n(DPRS) of NWs, lnland Waterways Authority of lndia (lWAt) has conctuded that 23 NWs\nhave been found viable for cargo movement. The list of the 23 NWs viable for cargo\nmovemenl is at Annexure.\n(c) & (d) Government of Tripura has proposed for declaration of Gumti River as\nNalional Waterway. However, the proposed wateMay does not have potential for\ndeclaration as National Waterway (NW) in terms of cargo potential and economic lnland\nWater Transport (lWT) operation. The only notable factor is that lhe above stretch of\nGumti river shall connect Tripura State hinterland with Bangladesh through IBP route I\n& 10 (Daudkandi - Sonamura stretch ofthe river in Bangladesh).\nContd...2^\n\n-2-\nA proposal to declare waterway stretches from Kollam (Quilon) to Kovalam\nin South and Kottapuram to Hosdurg in Norlh of Kerala as National Waterway was\nreceived from Hon'ble Chief Minister, Kerala in May, 2016. A Techno-Economic\nFeasibility (TEF) study through National Transportation Planning and Research Centre\n(NATPAC- a Govt. of Kerala lnstitution) was conducted in 2014 with regard to extension\nof West Coast Canal in the South of Kollam (Quilon) up to Kovalam and North of\nKottapuram up to Kasargod. TEF study found only Kottapuram - Kozhikode (Calicut)\nstretch in the North of Kottapuram technically feasible for development of shipping and\nnavigation and accordingly extension of West Coast Canal up to Kozhikode (Calicut) in\nthe North of Kottapuram was considered in the National Waterways Bill, 2015. Calicut-\nNeeleswaram stretch which is part of Kottapuram-Hosdurg slretch was not found\nfeasible for development of shipping & navigation as per TEF report. The Kollam\n(Quilon) - Kovalam stretch (South of Kollam) was also not found technically feasible\ndue to the presence of Varkala tunnel. The above position was conveyed to the Hon'ble\nChief Minister of Kerala in July, 2016. However, later Govt. of Kerala foMarded a\nproposal for formation of SPV for developing lnland WateMays from Kollam to Kovalam\nand Kozhikode to Kasargod at a cost of Rs.6,000 crore.\nHon'ble Chief Minister of Kerala was informed by Hon'ble Minister for\nRoad Transport Highways and Shipping in March 2018 that in the absence of any\nscheme of Govt. of lndia, the Shipping Ministry would find it difficult to provide financial\nassistance to implement faiMays development of stretches which are not part of\ndeclared National Waterways (NWs).\n\n-3-\nArf nexure\nLIST OF 23 NATIONAL.WATERWAYS FOUND FEASIBLE FOR CARGO MOVEMENT\nsl.\nNo.\nNational Watenray\nNo. Details of Watenrays STATES\n1 NationalWaterway 1\nGanga-Bhagirathi-Hooghly\nRiver System (Haldia -\nAllahabad)\nUttar Pradesh, Bihar,\nJharkhand & West Bengal\n2 NationalWaterway 2 Brahmaputra River (Dhubri -\nSadiva) Assam\n3 NationalWatenuay 16 Barak River Assam\n4 NationalWaterway 3\nWest Coast Canal\n(Kottapuram - Kollam),\nChampakara and\nUdyogmandal Canals\nKerala\n5 NationalWaterway 4 Krishna River (Vijayawada -\nMuktyala) Andhra Pradesh\n6 NationalWaterway 5 Dhamra-Paradio via\nMangalagadi to Pankopal Odisha\n7 NationalWaterway 8 Alappuzha- Changanassery\nCanal Kerala\nI NationalWaterway 9 Alappuzha-Kottayam\nAthirampuzha Canal\nKerala\nAlternate route: 1 1.Skm\n9 NationalWaterway 27 Cumberjua River Goa\n10 NationalWaterway 68 Mandovi River Goa\n11 NationalWaterway 86 Rupnarayan River West Bengal\n12 NationalWaterway 97 Sunderbans Waterway West Bengal\n13 National Waterway\n111 Zuari River Goa\n14 NationalWatenruay 10 Amba River Maharashtra\n15 Nati