��������������������������������������\n������������� ����������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������� ����� ���� ����� ������� ������� ����� ���� ����� ��� ���� ��������� ��� ������� �����\n����������� �\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������� ������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |930", "t": "Clearance to Exploration Projects", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 930 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26th JULY, 2020 \n  \nClearance to Exploration Projects \n930. SHRI RAHUL RAMESH SHEWALE:  \n        SHRI GIRISH BHALCHANDRA BAPAT:  \n        DR. PRITAM GOPINATHRAO MUNDE:   \nपै\u0000ो\u0000लयम व \u0000ाकृ\u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether delayed licences and clearances have been at the heart of slow progress in \nexploration project in the country  \n \n(b) if so, the facts in this regard;  \n \n(c) whether the explorers have to obtain several clearances from multiple Government \nauthorities, resulting in missed deadlines and increased liabilities  \n \n(d) if so, the facts thereof;  \n \n(e) whether a new proposal has been prepared by the Government for getting clearances \nfrom multiple government authorities; and   \n \n(f) if so, the details thereof and the time by which the new proposal is likely to be \nimplemented?  \n  \nANSWER  \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम और \u0000ाकृ\u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय म\u0000  रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी \n (\u0000ी रामे\u0000वर तेल\u0000) \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI  RAMESWAR TELI) \n  \n(a) to (d) : Contractors/Operators of Oil & Gas blocks have to obtain statutory \nclearances and approvals from various State and Central Government authorities to \nstart Exploration & Production activities.  These clearances and approvals include \nPetroleum Exploration License (PEL), Petroleum Mining Lease (PML), environment \nrelated clearances like Environment Clearance, Forest Clearance , Wildlife clearance \netc., Clearances from Ministry of Defence, Clearance from  Department of Space , \nConsent to Establish (CTE) , Consent to Operate (CTO) and many other clearances.  \nDelay in obtaining such clearances and approvals affects the overall timelines and \nprogress of the Exploration & Production projects. \n…2/-… \n\n- 2 –  \n \n(e) & (f) : The Government has been making continuous efforts to ensure timely \nclearances and approvals. Various efforts made by the Government in this regard are: \ni. The Empowered Coordination Committee (ECC) has been constituted under \nthe Cabinet Secretary for streamlining and speeding up clearances and \napprovals. \n \nii. Ministry of Petroleum & Natural Gas (MOP&NG) vide OM dated 28.02.2020 \nconveyed approval of Self Certification  of Production Sharing Contracts \n(PSCs) processes. Twenty two processes were identified where documents \nfrom the Contractors shall be accepted on Self-Certification basis and no \napproval is required.   \n         \n        iii. Ministry of Environment, Forest & Climate Change (MoEF&CC) has launched \nPARIVESH, a web based, role based workflow application which has been \ndeveloped for online submission and monitoring of the proposals submitted by \nthe proponents for seeking Environment, Forest, Wildlife and CRZ Clearances \nfrom Central, State and district level authorities.   \n \n       iv. Directorate General of Hydrocarbons (DGH) in consultation with Ministry of \nDefence/ Ministry of Home Affairs (MHA) has developed and implemented, e-\nsubmission of all applications for MOD related vessel clearance and MHA \nrelated expat clearance for Exploration & Production operations. \n \n      v. Regular meetings are being held in presence of officials from MOP&NG, DGH, \nState Government to discuss pending issues related to grant of licenses or \nclearances  pertaining to respective States.  \n \n      vi.  URJA PRAGATI  (Upstream Response by Joint Action for proactive \nGovernance and Timely Implementation) web-based interactive portal has been \nlaunched  recently by DGH to prioritize and flag the long pending issues \nconcerning the upstream hydrocarbon sector involving various stakeholders \nsuch as Operators, Central Ministries and State Governments. \n \n                                                             ********** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |931", "t": "Agreement with Atal Innovation Mission", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n \nLOK SABHA  \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 931  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nAGREEMENT WITH ATAL INNOVATION MISSION \n \n931. DR. ALOK KUMAR SUMAN: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the details of the names of institutes with wh om NitiAayog has entered into \nagreement for Skill Development in the country unde r Atal Innovation Mission \nincluding in Bihar;  \n \n(b) whether it is fact that the number of youths co nnected with skill development \nschemes till date is low in Bihar in comparison to other States;  \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereon;  \n \n(d)  the details of the scope provided by the Gover nment to connect the youth of \ndistrict Gopalganj of Bihar with this programme; and  \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP  \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) The Government has set up the Atal Innovation M ission (AIM) at NITI Aayog \nwith an objective to create scientific temper and c ultivate the spirit of curiosity, \ncreativity, imagination and innovation among young minds and inculcate skills such \nas design mind-set, computational thinking, adaptiv e learning, physical computing, \nrapid calculations, measurements, etc. NITI Aayog h as not entered into agreement \nwith this Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepr eneurship in the country under \nAIM. \n \n(b) No, Sir. The Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship (MSDE) is \nimplementing following schemes viz., the Pradhan Ma ntri Kaushal Vikas Yojana \n(PMKVY), Jan Shikshan Sansthan (JSS) Scheme and Nat ional Apprenticeship \nPromotion Scheme (NAPS) for short-term skill traini ng AND Craftsmen Training \nScheme (CTS) through the Industrial Training Instit utes (ITIs) for long-term skill \ntrainingacross the country including the State of Bihar.  \n \n(c) Analysis of the training data of last three yea rs for the above mentioned \nschemes in respect of Bihar reveal that the State o ccupies 8 \nth , 5 th , 21 st  and 5 th  \nposition under PMKVY Scheme, JSS Scheme, NAPS and CTS respectively. \n \n(d) Under PMKVY, the District Skill Committee (DSC)  of Gopalgunj looks after the \nskill development in the district with support of Training Centres (TCs) in the district.  \n \n(e) As on 10.07.2021, 15,843 candidates have been t rained in the District \nGopalganj of Bihar, out of which 4,609 candidates have been reported placed. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |932", "t": "Reduction in Courses in a Semester", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 932 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nReduction in Courses in a Semester \n \n932. SHRIMATI VANGA GEETHA VISWANATH: \n SHRI KOMATI REDDY VENKAT REDDY: \n SHRI KOTHA PRABHAKAR REDDY: \n SHRI MANNE SRINIVAS REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the students may take up to 40% of the total courses in a semester in a \nprogramme through Swayam (Study Webs of Active Learning for Young Aspiring \nMinds); and \n(b) if so the det ails thereof/response received/ suggestions received, State wise also and \naction taken as on date and proposals rejected by Government so far and details of \npending in this regard and by when action will be taken? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a) & (b)  The University Grants Commission (UGC) has notified Credit Framework for Online \nLearning Courses through SWAYAM ( Study Webs of Active Learning for Young Aspiring \nMinds) Regulations, 2021 on 25 th March, 2021 in suppression to the UGC Credit Framework \nfor Online Learning Courses through SWAYAM R egulation, 2016 whereby it facilitate to allow \nup to 40% of the total courses being offered in a particular programme in a semester through \nthe online learning courses offered through SWAYAM Platform. \nThe credits/marks obtained   by the candidate enrolled in universities for SWAYAM Certificate \nwill be counted in the transcript of the Candidate, only if the University has adopted MOOCs \nCourses offered on SWAYAM Platform for Credit Transfer as per the UGC (Credit Framework \nfor Online Learning Courses through SWAYAM Regulations, 2021. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |933", "t": "Banking Licences for Agriculture Sector", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 933 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 26TH JULY 2021/ SRAVANA 4, 1943 (SAKA)  \nBanking Licences for Agriculture Sector  \n933. SHRI S. JAGATHRAKSHAKAN: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is planning to seriously consider differentiated banking licences \nfor the agriculture sector; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, along with the measures taken/being taken by the Government \nkeeping in view the fact that there will be many takers for the rural sector, who will do a better \njob of providing and spreading credit for much -needed reforms, such a s crop diversification; \nand  \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor?  \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FINANCE \n(DR. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n(a) to (c): The National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (NABARD) has been \nestablished by the NABARD Act, 1981 as a development bank, to provide and regulate credit \nand other facilities for the promotion and development of agriculture, small scale industries, \ncottage and village industries, handicrafts and other rural crafts and other allied economic \nactivities in rural  areas with a view to promoting integrated rural development and securing \nprosperity of rural areas. \nThe institutional framework for deployment of rural credit comprises the cooperatives, \nscheduled commercial banks and the Regional Rural Banks. NABARD sanc tions credit limits \nand refinance to State Cooperative Banks, Land Development Banks and the Regional Rural \nBanks for supplementing their resources for short and medium term loans of various \nagricultural and non-agricultural purposes, including investment credit provided by them under \nvarious schemes. NABARD also provides refinance to commercial banks against the term \nloans issued by them under schematic lending for agricultural and certain non -agricultural \npurposes. \n***  "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |934", "t": "Central and State Tax on Fuel", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \n***** \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.934 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY THE 26TH JULY, 2021 \n                                                 SRAVANA 4, 1943 (SAKA)  \n \nCENTRAL AND STATE TAX ON FUEL \n \n \n934. SHRI K. MURALEEDHARAN:  \n        SHRI BENNY BEHANAN:  \n        SHRI UTTAM KUMAR REDDY NALAMADA:  \n        SHRI D.K.SURESH:  \n        SHRI VINCENT H. PALA: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Ministry has initiated di scussions with the Goods and Services Tax \n(GST) Council to assess if fuel should be brought under GST;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof, and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n \n(c) whether the Ministry is considering reforming the Central and State tax on fuel; \nand \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) to (d): Article 279 A (5) of the Constitution prescribes that the Goods and \nService Tax Council shall recommend the date on which the goods and services tax \nbe levied on petroleum crude, high speed diesel, motor spirit (commonly known as \npetrol), natural gas and aviation turb ine fuel (ATF). Also, as per section 9(2) of the \nCGST Act, inclusion of these products in GST will require recommendation of the \nGST Council. So far, the GST Council, in which the states are also represented, has \nnot made any recommendation for inclusion of these goods under GST. \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |935", "t": "Import of Oil", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 935 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26th July, 2021 \n \nImport of oil \n  \n+ 935.SHRI  LALLU SINGH \n  \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम और \u0000ाकृ\u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी \n  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n                                \n (a)  whether the Government has asked the oil companies of the country to consider   \nabout reducing the import of oil from Saudi Arabia; \n(b)   if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether the Government has kept other countries in mind as alternative to fulfill \nthe requirement of oil in the country; and  \n(d)    if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम और \u0000ाकृ\u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी \n ( \u0000ी रामे\u0000वर तेल\u0000) \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM & \nNATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a)& (b):  Oil Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs) have term contract with National \nOil Company of Saudi Arabia for import of crude oil. During 2020-21, the \nupliftment of crude from Saudi Arabia reduced due to Covid-19 and cuts imposed \nby Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) on crude oil \nproduction. \n(c) &(d):  To ensure security of crude supplies, apart from Saudi Arabia, Oil PSUs \nhave contracts with other countries located at various geographical locations viz. \nMiddle East, Africa, North America , South America etc. \n \n                                                        ****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |936", "t": "Skill Development Centres in Gujarat", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 936 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT CENTRES IN GUJARAT \n \n† 936.  SHRIMATI POONAMBEN MAADAM: \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP be \npleased to state: \n \n(a)      the number of Skill Development Centres fu nctioning in Gujarat under Pradhan \nMantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana at present and district-wise details thereof; \n \n(b)      the number of students who have enrolled i n Skill Development Centres for training \nin Gujarat and the number of people who have received training till date; \n \n(c) whether the Government has made any efforts to provide employment to students \nwho have received the said training; \n \n(d) if not, whether the Government is making any ef fort at the central level to provide \nemployment to the trained students; and \n \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n  \nANSWER  \n  \nMINISTER OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) Under Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKV Y), short term training is \nundergoing in the 186 training centres including 28  PMKK in the State of Gujarat. The \ndistrict-wise number of training centres is given at Annexure . \n  \n(b)       Under PMKVY, 2.09 lakh and 1.83 lakh candidates have been enrolled and trained, \nrespectively, in the empanelled training centres under short term training (STT) component \nin the State of Gujarat. Also, 1.99 lakh and 1.94 l akh candidates have been enrolled and \noriented, respectively, in Recognition of Prior Lea rning (RPL) component of PMKVY in the \nState of Gujarat.  \n  \n(c) Under the scheme, placement has been linked wit h payout to training providers. \nThe last tranche, i.e., 30 percent of total payout is only disbursed to training providers on \nensuring placement of the candidates. Further, Rozg ar Melas are organised with the \nsupport of Sector Skill Councils, training provider s and implementing agencies to directly \nlink the candidates with the potential employers. \n \n(d) In view of (c) above, question does not arise. \n \n(e) In view of (c) above, question does not arise.  \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure \n \nThe district-wise number of operational training centres in the state of Gujarat under \nPradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) \n \nS. No.  District  Number of TCs  \n1.  Ahmedabad 29 \n2.  Anand 5 \n3.  Aravalli 1 \n4.  Banaskantha 6 \n5.  Bharuch 7 \n6.  Bhavnagar 8 \n7.  Chhota Udepur 5 \n8.  Dahod 3 \n9.  Devbhoomi Dwarka 3 \n10.  Gandhinagar 3 \n11.  Gir Somnath 2 \n12.  Jamnagar 9 \n13.  Junagadh 7 \n14.  Kachchh 8 \n15.  Kheda 6 \n16.  Mahisagar 2 \n17.  Mehsana 8 \n18.  Morbi 1 \n19.  Narmada 4 \n20.  Navsari 2 \n21.  Panch Mahals 2 \n22.  Patan 6 \n23.  Porbandar 2 \n24.  Rajkot 9 \n25.  Sabar kantha 5 \n26.  Surat 22 \n27.  Tapi 5 \n28.  Vadodara 13 \n29.  Valsad 3 \nTotal 186 \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |938", "t": "Rural Economy", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 938  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nRURAL ECONOMY \n \n938.  SHRI G.M. SIDDESHWAR: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether Union Government has taken note of reports that consumption in rural India has shown a slowdown  \n     during the last Covid affected months in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether it is a fact that rural economy has been plagued by falling crop prices and declining incomes, resulting  \n     in a severe slump in demand; and \n(d) if so, the steps taken by Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) to (d): As per the Provisional Estimates of Gross Domestic Product (GDP) for the year 2020 -21 released by the \nNational Statistical Office, the Gross Value Added (GVA) of agriculture has shown a growth of 3.6 per cent, \nsignalling better prospects of consumption in rural India. Total Foodgrain production is estimated to rise 2.7 per \ncent to a new record of 305.4 4 million tonnes in the current crop year 2020 -21, on record output of rice, wheat, \nmaize and gram. Domestic tractor sales, an indicator of rural demand, reco rded a growth of 27 per cent in FY \n2020-21 over previous year.  The forecast of a normal south -west monsoon in 2021-22 and recent trends in kharif \nsowing are further expected to boost rural incomes. \n \nIn line with t he announcement made in  Union Budget, 2018-19 to keep M inimum Support Prices (M SPs) at levels \nof one and half times of the cost of production  to ensure remunerative prices to the farmers for their produce , \nGovernment has increased the MSP for Rabi and Kharif crops for the marketing season 2021-22. Higher MSPs \nhave been supported by record levels of procurement by the Government at 869.0 LMT of paddy benefitting \n128.22 lakh farmers with MSP value of Rs.1,64,066 crore  during Kharif Marketing Season 2020 -21 and 433.32 \nLMT of wheat benefitting 49.16 Lakh farmers with MSP value of Rs.85,581.02 crore during Rabi Marketing Season \n2021-22. \n \nGovernment had announced a special economic and comprehensive package of Rs. 29.87 lakh crore under \nAtmaNirbhar Bharat (ANB) to combat the impact of the COVID -19 pandemic, to revive economic growth and to \nbolster employment. Union Budget 2021 -22 has announced number of measures to support broad -based and \ninclusive economic development . Government announced a relief  package of Rs 6.29 lakh crore in June 2021 to \nstrengthen public health and provide impetus for growth and employment measures.  \n \nKey measures of the above steps, inter-alia, include Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Yojana, additional Emergency \nWorking Capital Funding for farmers through NABARD,  concessional credit through Kisan Credit Cards, fund \ntransfer under PM-KISAN, PM Fasal Bima Yojana claim payments, additional subsidy for DAP & P&K fertilizers, \nextension of SVAMITVA S cheme to all States/UTs, enhancing agricultural credit and infrastructure funds, interest \nsubvention for dairy cooperatives, liquidity support under Rural Infrastructure Development Fund to states, PM \nGarib Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyaan, enhanced MGNREGA budgetary support, PM SVANidhi, Agri -Infrastructure Fund \nfor farm gate infrastructure, scheme for Formalisation of Micro Food Enterprises (MFE), Pradhan Mantri Matsya \nSampada Yojana, National Animal Disease Control Programme, Animal Husbandry Infrastructure Develo pment \nFund, Promotion of Herbal Cultivation and Beekeeping and extension of Operation Greens from Tomatoes, Onion \nand Potatoes (TOP) to all fruits and vegetables (TOTAL).  \n*****                                                    \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |939", "t": "Migration Commission", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 939 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nMIGRATION COMMISSION \n \n939.  DR. A. CHALLAKUMAR: \n  ADV. ADOOR PRAKASH:  \nSHRI K. MURALEEDHARAN: \n  DR. MOHAMMAD JAWED: \n  DR. AMAR SINGH: \n  SHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN:  \nSHRI ANTO ANTONY: \n  SHRI M. SELVARAJ:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)the deadline by which the Government intends to finalise and \nimplement the draft National Policy on Migrant Workers prepared by \nthe NITI Aayog; \n(b)whether workers unions representing workers, NGOs and other civil \nsociety organistations were consulted while drafting the Policy; and \n(c)whether the draft Policy will take forwar d the proposal to set up a \nMigration Commission as mooted by the Government in May, 2020? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (c):   NITI Aayog  has constituted a sub -group to prepare a \nNational Action Plan for Migrant Work ers. The sub -group comprises  \nmembers from various Ministries of Govt. of India, subject experts, \nNGOs and civil society organisations, to prepare a tangible action plan \nto address issues related to migrant workers. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |94", "t": "Banking Facilities in Rural Areas", "head": "POSITION: 14 \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE  \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *94 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26th JULY, 2021 / SRAVANA 4, 1943 (SAKA) \n \n‘Credit Support to MFIs’ \n* 94: SHRI NAMA NAGESWARA RAO:  \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Micro Finance Institutions (MFIs) have sought emergency credit line support \nto mobilise funds to tide over the crisis of lower collections due to the business \ndisruptions caused by the Corona pandemic and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) the steps taken by the Government to facilitate flow of funds from commercial banks \nto micro finance institutions;  \n(c) whether the Government has identified any priority States including Telangana to \nincrease the coverage of micro finance credit facility in such States;  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n(e) the extent to which this  initiative is likely to help achieve the target of financial \ninclusion of the Government?   \nANSWER \nFINANCE MINISTER \n(SMT. NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a) to (e):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n****** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN  REPLY TO  PART (a) TO (e) OF THE  LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *94  FOR ANSWER ON 26th JULY 2021, TABLED BY SHRI \nNAMA NAGESWARA RAO REGARDING CREDIT SUPPORT TO MFIs. \n(a) to (e):  \nRepresentations have been received by Government from  industry stakeholders regarding \nrequirement of liquidity support by NBFC-MFIs, especially smaller ones following the second \nwave of the pandemic . In recognition of the business disruptions caused by the pandemic and \nthe resultant liquidity stress being felt by MFIs on account of lower loan collections, rise in credit \ndelinquencies, drying up of  funds for making fresh disbursements a nd meeting operational \nexpenses, Government, have announced a scheme , namely, Credit Guarantee Scheme for \nMicro Finance Institutions (CGSMFI). This scheme provides Credit Guarantee of 75% of default \namount for a maximum period of three years , for loans given by lenders  to Non-Banking \nFinancial Companies- Micro Finance Institutions (NBFC-MFIs) and Micro Finance Institutions \n(MFIs). The guarantee cover under the scheme is being provided by National Credit \nGuarantee Trustee Company Ltd. (NCGTC). \nThis scheme has been designed to encourage lenders to provide liquidity to NBFC -MFIs/ \nMFIs to enable such NBFC-MFIs/ MFIs to provide credit facility at lower rates to an estimated \n25 lakh small borrowers , thereby supporting the Government’s endeavour towards financial \ninclusion.  \nThe CGSMFI facility is applicable across all states including the State of Telangana. \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |940", "t": "Assessment of Loss Caused by Covid-19 Pandemic", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 940 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nASSESSMENT OF LOSS CAUSED BY COVID-19 PANDEMIC \n \n \n940. SHRI DILIP SAIKIA: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any assessment of the loss to the country‘s economy resulting from the Corona \npandemic has been made by the Government; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the action plan of the Government to tide over this situation? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) and (b) As per the latest estimates released by National Statistical Office (NSO), Ministry of \nStatistics and Program Implementation (MoSPI), India’s real Gross Domestic Product (GDP) is \nestimated to contract by 7.3 per cent during Financial Year (FY) 2020 -21. This reflects the \nunparalleled effect of the ‘once in a century’ COVID -19 pandemic and the containment \nmeasures that were taken to control its spread. \n \n(c) In FY 2020 -21, Government announced a special economic and comprehensive package \nunder AtmaNirbhar Bharat (ANB) to combat the impact of the COVID -19 pandemic and to \nrevive economic growth. The package involved a total amount of Rs.29,87,641 crore (including \namount provided for the Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Package, Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan \nAnna Yojana from July to November, and measures announced by RBI). \n \nThe Union Budget 2021 -22 has announced number of measures to support broad -based and \ninclusive economic development under six pillars of Health and Wellbeing; Physical & Financial \nCapital, and Infrastructure; Inclusive Development for Aspirational India; Reinvigorating Human \nCapital; Innovation and R&D; and Minimum Government and Maximum Governance.   \n \nFurther, in June 2021, Government has announced a relief package of Rs 6.29 lakh crore to \nsupport the Indian economy in its fight against the second wave of COVID -19 pandemic, \nstrengthen public health and provide impetus for growth and employment.  \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |941", "t": "Amount Spent on Maintenance of Historical Buildings", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.941 \nANSWERED ON 26.7.2021 \n \n \nAMOUNT SPENT ON MAINTENANCE OF HISTORICAL BUILDINGS \n \n \n+941. SHRI CHANDESHWAR PRASAD: \nSHRI VINAYAK RAUT:  \nSHRIMATI REKHA VERMA: \n  \n  \nWill the Minister of Culture be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number and details of historical buildings in the country, State -wise  \nincluding Bihar, Maharashtra and Uttar Pradesh; \n(b) the details of amount allocated and spent for the maintenance of these        \nbuildings during the last three years; \n(c) the number of dilapidated historical buildings in the country especially in Bihar,   \nMaharashtra and Uttar Pradesh and the number of buildings repaired during the \nlast three years, year -wise and State -wise along with the details  of the funds \nspent on the repair and maintenance of such buildings during the last three  \nyears; \n(d) whether the Government has conducted any survey regarding the buildings of  \nhistorical importance and if so, the number of such buildings which are in ne ed \nof repair, State-wise especially in Bihar, Maharashtra and Uttar Pradesh; and \n(e) the number of tourists who have visited these monuments and the amount of \nrevenue generated from these historical buildings during the last three years? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF CULTURE, TOURISM \nAND DEVELOPMENT OF NORTH EASTERN REGION \n \n(SHRI G.KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) \n \nThe details of centrally protected monuments/sites including Heritage \nBuildings in country (State/UT –wise) are at Annexure-I. \n \n(b)&(c) The details of allocation and funds spent State -wise on repair and \nmaintenance of such monuments/sites including Heritage Buildings are at \nAnnexure-II. There is no dilapidated monument in the country especially in \nBihar, Maharashtra and Uttar Pradesh.  The  conservation and maintenance of \nCentrally protected monuments/sites is a regular process and necessary steps \nare taken by the Archaeological Survey of India (ASI) to conserve these \nmonuments/sites including monuments/sites in Bihar, Maharashtra and Uttar \nPradesh as per approved Conservation programme. \n \n \ncontd…. \n\n \n \n \n(d) \n-2- \n \n \nInspection of centrally protected monuments/sites is undertaken by ASI from time to \ntime and conservation measures are taken up as per the need and priority within \navailable resources.   \n \n5244 works under Annual Conservation Programme have been approved for centrally \nprotected monuments in the country during 2021 -22. Details of maintenance and \nspecial repair works identified for Bihar, Maharashtra and Uttar Pradesh are as under: \n \nBihar 101 works approved \nMaharashtra 529 works approved \nUttar Pradesh 638 works approved \n \n \n(e) The tourist flow at centrally protected ticketed monuments in the country and  \nrevenue collected therefrom during the last three years is as under:  \n \nYear  Total Visitors Total Revenue \n(Amount in Rupees) \n2018-19 5,31,52,423 317,20,00,000 \n2019-20 4,82,66,190 336,23,23,642 \n2020-21 1,19,12,580 40,32,17,585 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 941 FOR 26.7.2021 \n \nNUMBER OF CENTRALLY PROTECTED MONUMENTS/SITES UNDER THE \nJURISDICTION OF ARCHAEOLOGICAL SURVEY OF INDIA IN THE COUNTRY  \n \nSl.No. Name of State Nos. of Monuments \n1.  Andhra Pradesh 135 \n2.  Arunachal Pradesh 03 \n3.  Assam 55 \n4.  Bihar 70 \n5.  Chhattisgarh 46 \n6.  Daman & Diu (U. T.) 11 \n7.  Goa 21 \n8.  Gujarat 203 \n9.  Haryana 91 \n10.  Himachal Pradesh 40 \n11.  Jammu & Kashmir (U. T.) 56 \n12.  Jharkhand 13 \n13.  Karnataka  506 \n14.  Kerala 29 \n15.  Ladakh (U. T.) 15 \n16.  Madhya Pradesh 291 \n17.  Maharashtra 286 \n18.  Manipur 01 \n19.  Meghalaya 08 \n20.  Mizoram 01 \n21.  Nagaland 04 \n22.  N.C.T. Delhi 173 \n23.  Odisha 80 \n24.  Puducherry (U. T.) 07 \n25.  Punjab 33 \n26.  Rajasthan 163 \n27.  Sikkim 03 \n28.  Telangana 08 \n29.  Tamil Nadu 412 \n30.  Tripura 08 \n31.  Uttar Pradesh 743 \n32.  Uttarakhand 43 \n33.  West Bengal \n \n135 \n TOTAL 3693 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE-II \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b)&(c) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 941 FOR 26.7.2021  \nDETAILS OF EXPENDITURE INCURRED FOR CONSERVATION, PRESERVATION AND ENVIRONMENTAL  \nDEVELOPMENT  OF PROTECTED MONUMENTS DURING THE LAST THREE YEARS  \n                                                                                                         (Amount Rs. in Lakhs) \nSl. \nNo. \nName of \nState/UT \nCircle / Branch 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 \nAllocation Expenditure Allocation Expenditure Allocation Expenditure \n1. Uttar Pradesh Agra Circle 860.00 858.61 1355.00 1355.00 1300.00 1296.00 \nLucknow Circle 482.00 481.99 560.00 560.00 388.00 388.00 \nSarnath Circle 255.00 254.57 320.00 320.00 438.00 438.00 \n*Meerut Circle - - - - 0.00 0.00 \n2. Maharashtra Aurangabad \nCircle \n430.00 430.00 608.85 608.85 316.00 316.00 \nMumbai Circle 890.00 889.97 950.00 950.00 1100.00 1100.00 \nNagpur Circle 470.00 470.00 540.00 "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |942", "t": "Skill India Workers in Abroad", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 942 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nSKILL INDIA WORKERS IN ABROAD \n942   SHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU: \n          Will the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AN D ENTREPRENEURSHIP be pleased \nto state: \n(a)   whether it is a fact that the Government has identified job opportunities for skilled Indian \nworkers abroad in the blue grey-collared categories. \n \n  \n(b)   if so, the details thereof along with the num ber and details of countries with whom \nnegotiations is underway in this regard;  \n  \n(c)   the details of the meetings held with various  countries and parameters, if any, mutually \nacceptable to the Government and the potential fore ign partners worked out for creation of \nthese job opportunities.  \n  \n(d)  whether the Government has fixed any time sche dule for finalization of this programme \nfor job opportunities for skilled Indian workers abroad; and  \n  \n(e)     if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?  \n  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTRREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN)  \n \n(a) Yes, Sir. The Government through National Skill  Development Corporation (NSDC) had \ncommissioned the Global Skill Gap Study in 2019 for  mapping international job opportunities for \nskilled India workers in the blue and grey-collared categories.  \n \n(b) The study highlights nations that would face wo rkforce shortage in the foreseeable future \nand presents sectoral demand in top 15 countries until 2030 as below: \n \nI. In United Arab Emirates (UAE), Kingdom of Saudi Arabia (KSA) and Qatar shall be \napproximately 2.63 million across major sectors suc h as Construction (1.17 million), \nWholesale and Retail Trade (0.42 million), Real Est ate and Rental and Business \nServices (0.28 million). \nII. In Germany, Netherlands, United Kingdom, Sweden  and Switzerland shall be \napproximately 0.30 million across major sectors such as Health and Social Care (0.11 \nmillion), Manufacturing (0.05 million). \nIII. In Singapore, Australia, New Zealand, Canada 9 5 million across major sectors such \nas Health Care and Social Assistance (United States  of America (USA), Japan and \nMalaysia shall be approximately 0.0.22 million), Construction (0.10 million). \nA letter of intent is underway between the Ministry  of Immigration, Quebec and MSDE to launch \na Recruitment Mission to mobilize skilled Indian wo rkers from India to Quebec in the sectors of \nHealthcare, retail and hospitality. The process has slowed down due to COVID-19. \n\n \n(c)  NSDC is working with the Ministry of Human Res ource and Emiritization (MoHRE) and \nAbu Dhabi Quality and Conformity Council (ADQCC) fo r benchmarking qualifications, \nassessment and certification. In October, 2018, ben chmarking of 16 UAE skill qualifications to \n13 Indian skill qualifications in construction and automotive sectors was conducted by the \ntechnical teams of NSDC and ADQCC. In August, 2019,  a pilot programme was initiated. 133 \ncandidates were assessed on mutually recognized qua lifications. 63 candidates were \ninterviewed and selected by the UAE employer out of which 18 candidates were deployed in the \nUAE.  \nIn respect of KSA, in the first phase, 5 occupation s were benchmarked between India \nand KSA. Pilot programme for 100 workers was conduc ted and Recognition of Prior Learning \nassessment has been undertaken, out of which 81 can didates have successfully cleared the \nassessment and were issued joint certification in February, 2020.  \nUnder the Joint Working Group Mechanism, meetings w ith Japan, Germany, Australia \nand United Kingdom have been held. \n(d)      No, Sir.  \n(e)  No time schedule is fixed for finalization of job opportunities for skilled Indian workers \nabroad as this is a continuous activity. All intern ational initiatives have slowed down due to \nCOVID-19. \n \n****** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |943", "t": "Inclusion of Gorkha into ST List", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n(MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS) \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED No. 943 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nINCLUSION OF GORKHA INTO ST LIST \n \n 943. SHRI GAURAV GOGOI:  \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government of Assam has sent the recommendation on Gorkha sub-Castes of \nAssam; \n (b) if so, the details thereof; \n (c) the Status of the proposal for Scheduled Tribes of Gorkha sub-castes of Assam?  \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA)   \n \n \n (a): - Ministry of Tribal Affairs has not received any proposal from Government of Assam \nfor inclusion of ‘Gorkha’ Community in the Scheduled Tribe list of Assam.  \n \n(b) & (c):- In view of (a), question do not arise. \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |944", "t": "Primary Education of Tribals in NER", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n(MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 944 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n  \nPRIMARY EDUCATION OF TRIBALS IN NER \n  \n944.    SHRI REBATI TRIPURA: \n  \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the number of tribals who have access to education and details thereof along with enrolment in the primary education \nin the country especially in North East Region; \n(b) whether the Government proposes to establish more schools in tribal and remote areas of NER for the benefit of \nstudents of these areas, if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the present status of the scholarship schemes implemented by the Government for tribal students of NER; and \n(d) the amount of funds allocated/utilized along with the number of students benefited under the said schemes till date? \n  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA)   \n \n(a) As per UDISE+ 2019-20 of the Ministry of Education, 2,47,93,474 ST students are enrolled in different schools \n(I to XII) including 1,28,01,880 students enrolled in primary classes (I-V). State wise figure is at Annexure-I. \n(b) The Government, through the scheme of Eklavya Model Residential Schools (EMRS) funded by the Ministry of \nTribal Affairs, provides quality education to the tribal students in a residential environment from class VI to XII. \nCurrently 119 EMRSs have been sanctioned in the NER and another 31 EMRSs are to be sanctioned during the \ncurrent financial year. \n(c) Ministry implements following ongoing schemes for providing scholarships to ST students in the country \nincluding NER: - \ni. Pre-Matric Scholarship for ST students (Class IX and X) \nii. Post Matric Scholarship for ST students.(Class XI onwards) \niii. National Fellowship and Scholarship for Higher Education for ST students. \niv. National Overseas Scholarship for ST students. \n Out of the schemes mentioned above, funds under those at Sl. No. (i) & (ii) are released to the State \nGovernments/ Union Territory Administrations. These are demand driven open ended Centrally Sponsored Schemes.  \nThe release of funds to States/UTs is done based on the proposal and expenditure details received from the State along \nwith the submission of other requisite documents. \n  \nThe scheme at Sl. No. (iii) above is Central Sector Scheme implemented by Ministry of Tribal Affairs where \nscholarship amount is released directly to Students/Institutes in DBT mode. The scheme at Sl. No. (iv) is also \nCentral Sector Scheme implemented by the Ministry through Ministry of External Affairs. \n \n(d)  The amount of fund released during last five years under Pre-Matric and Post-Matric Scholarship Scheme to \nNER States and utilization by NER States along with the number of students benefited is enclosed as Annexure-\nII. \n  \n  \n\n \nAnnexure-I as referred to at part (a) in the reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.944 Shri Rebati Tripura \nto be answered on 26.07.2021 regarding “Primary Education of Tribals in NER”. \n  \nState/UT ST Students enrolled in Classes I – XII \nGirls Boys Total \nAndaman & Nicobar Islands 2955 2944 5899 \nAndhra Pradesh 262514 275840 538354 \nArunachal Pradesh 117953 112583 230536 \nAssam 494515 494625 989140 \nBihar 310585 317485 628070 \nChandigarh 249 281 530 \nChhattisgarh 867878 863116 1730994 \nDadra & Nagar Haveli 21217 21635 42852 \nDaman & Diu 1697 1760 3457 \nDelhi 8436 9887 18323 \nGoa 12258 12452 24710 \nGujarat 928523 997480 1926003 \nHaryana 98 193 291 \nHimachal Pradesh 38974 41212 80186 \nJammu & Kashmir 133487 151135 284622 \nJharkhand 998052 1005753 2003805 \nKarnataka 420437 445701 866138 \nKerala 46468 48703 95171 \nLadakh 22525 21633 44158 \nLakshadweep 5598 5613 11211 \nMadhya Pradesh 1739385 1883975 3623360 \nMaharashtra 1192515 1302551 2495066 \nManipur 130856 135347 266203 \nMeghalaya 441761 416458 858219 \nMizoram 130705 134518 265223 \nNagaland 159545 159272 318817 \nOdisha 1037673 1069857 2107530 \nPuducherry 310 330 640 \nPunjab 1450 2014 3464 \nRajasthan 1178404 1333276 2511680 \nSikkim 21753 21735 43488 \nTamilnadu 87384 93855 181239 \nTelangana 346338 379395 725733 \nTripura 125864 130178 256042 \nUttar Pradesh 212969 225583 438552 \nUttarakhand 31048 32843 63891 \nWest Bengal 559164 550713 1109877 \nTotal 12091543 12701931 24793474 \n \n\n \nAnnexure-II as referred to at part (d) in the reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.944 Shri Rebati Tripura to be answered on 26.07.2021 regarding “Primary \nEducation of Tribals in NER”. \nThe N.E State-wise details of funds released and utilized during the last Five years (2016-17 to 2020-21) under the scheme of Pre Matric Scholarship for ST \nstudents \nRs. in Lakh \nSl. \nNo State/ UT \n2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 \nFund \nRelease \nFund \nUtilized Beneficiaries Fund \nRelease \nFund \nUtilized Beneficiaries Fund \nRelease \nFund \nUtilized Beneficiaries Fund \nRelease \nFund \nUtilized Beneficiaries Fund \nRelease \nFund \nUtilized Beneficiaries \n1 Arunachal \nPradesh 0   2594 0   3794 0   0 0   1979 0   5771 \n2 As"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |945", "t": "Rename of IIT Madras", "head": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 945 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nRename of IIT Madras \n \n945.  DR. T.R. PAARIVENDHAR: \n SHRI A. GANESHAMURTHI: \n \nWill the Minister of Education be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Union Government has received any proposal from any \n educational institution/individual or any organizations to change the \n name of IIT-Madras into IIT Chennai; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  the action taken/being taken by the Union Government on such \n proposals? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a) to (c): Any such proposal for amending the name of the Indian Institute  of \nTechnology, Madras is not under consideration of the Government.  \n \n \n \n \n***** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |946", "t": "Clean Energy cooking fuel Innovations", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 946 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n \nClean Energy cooking fuel Innovations \n \n946. SHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK: \nSHRI MANOJ TIWARI: \nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA: \nSHRI RAVI KISHAN: \nSHRI SUBRAT PATHAK: \nSHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA: \nSHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU: \nSHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO: \nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम और \u0000ाकृ\u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether clean energy is going to play a large rolein fuelling the growth of the Indian \neconomy to managethe ambitions and needs of our population; \n \n(b) if so, whether the Government has been pitchingfor clean energy cooking fuel innovations \nand extendedopen invitation to entrepreneurs for the said purposeand if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the details of the existing gas-based economy ofthe country as on date along with the details \nof targets,if any, fixed by the Government for its future extension; \n \n(d) the details of corrective steps taken by theGovernment for developing the nation-wide gas \ngrid andother infrastructure required for achieving such targets;and \n \n(e) the details of expenditure likely to be incurred fordeveloping such national assets along with \nthe detailsof funds allocated and released so far by the Governmentin this regard? \n \n \n ANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयमऔर\u0000ाकृ\u0000तकगैसमं\u0000ालयम\u0000रा\u0000यमं\u0000ी \n(\u0000ीरामे\u0000वरतेल\u0000  )  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a)to (d):  As on 31.03.2021, Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB) has \nauthorized approximately 33,764 Km Natural Gas Pipeline Network across the country with the \naim to create a national gas grid and increase the availability of natural gas across the country. \n\nThe authorized Natural Gas pipeline entity is allowed to lay spurlines as per the provision of the \nregulations. Accordingly, 19,998 km of Natural Gas pipeline (including sub-transmission \npipeline & tie in connectivity pipeline) are operational and 15,369 km are under various stages of \nconstruction. The existing and upcoming pipelines will form a basic National Gas Grid in the \ncountry. However, expansion of pipeline infrastructure is a continuous effort based on gas \ndemand assessment of various regions. \nGovernment has taken a series of decisions to promote use of Clean Energy: \n(i) Development of City Gas Distribution (CGD) networks supports the availability and \naccessibility of natural gas in form of Piped Natural Gas (PNG) to households, \nindustrial uses and Compressed Natural Gas (CNG) for transportation uses. PNGRB \ngrants authorization to the entities for developing of CGD network in Geographical \nAreas (GAs) as per PNGRB Act, 2006.  PNGRB identifies GAs for authorizing the \ndevelopment of CGD network in synchronization with the development of natural gas \npipeline connectivity and natural gas availability. So far 232 GAs have been \nauthorized for development of CGD network across country up to 10th CGD Bidding \nRound covering more than 400 districts in the country in 27 States/Union Territories \n(UTs), which covers around 71% of India’s population and 53% of its area. PNGRB \nwebhosted public notice dated 04.02.2020 wherein a tentative list of 44 identified \nGAs to be covered under upcoming 11th CGD bidding round has been prepared. \n \n(ii) With the aim to promote and sustain an efficient and robust gas market and to foster \ngas trading in the country, PNGRB has granted authorization to Indian Gas Exchange \nLimited on 02.12.2020, to set up and operate Gas Exchange. \n \n(iii) Furthermore, in order to make natural gas affordable in far flung areas, PNGRB has \nnotified Unified Tariff Regulations on 23.11.2020 by way of Petroleum and Natural \nGas Regulatory Board (Determination of Natural Gas Pipeline Tariff) Second \nAmendment Regulations, 2020. \n \n(iv) To reduce fuel emissions by increasing the share of LNG in India's energy mix and to \npromote the usage of cleaner fuels in the future, PNGRB has allowed any entity to \nestablish and operate LNG Stations in any GA or anywhere else in the country for \ndispensing LNG in liquid state, only to the transport sector. \n \n(v) In order to increase competition in CGD areas, PNGRB has notified Guiding \nPrinciples for declaring City or Local Natural Gas Distribution Networks as common \ncarrier or contact carrier Regulations to provide non- discriminatory open access to \nthird party entities. \n \nFurther, an initiative, Sustainable Alternative Towards Affordable Transportation (SATAT) was \nlaunched on 1st October 2018 aiming to establish an ecosystem for production of Compressed \n\nBio Gas (CBG) from various waste/ biomass sources in the country. SATAT has envisaged \ndeveloping 5000 CBG plants with total CBG production capacity of 15 Million Metric TonnePer \nAnnum (MMTPA).  Under SATAT, Indian Oil Corporation Limited, Bharat Petroleum \nCorporation Limited, Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited, Gail (India) Limited and \nIndra"}, {"k": "questions|ls|17|6|UNSTARRED |947", "t": "Pradhan Mantri Janjatiya Vikas Mission", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n(MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS) \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 947 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 26.07.2021 \n  \nPRADHAN MANTRI JANJATIYA VIKAS MISSION \n  \n947.     DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE: \nSHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE: \nDR. SUJAY RADHAKRISHNA VIKHE PATIL: \nDR. HEENA GAVIT: \nSHRI UNMESH BHAIYYASAHEB PATIL: \n  \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n  \n (a) whether Government has launched the Pradhan Mantri Janjatiya Vikas Mission and if so, \nthe details thereof and the aims and objective behind the move; \n(b) the number of most backward tribal dominated districts that will be targeted under this \nmission; \n(c) the estimated number of tribals in remote and maoist hit areas who will be provided \nemployment opportunities; and \n(d) the other steps taken by the Government for the welfare of tribals living in most backward \nareas and maoist hit areas? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SMT. RENUKA SINGH SARUTA)   \n  \n(a) to (c) Ministry of Tribal Affairs has not so far launched Pradhan Mantri Janjatiya Vikas \nMission. The Ministry has envisaged a comprehensive livelihood Mission Mode development \nprogramme catering to STs for a period 2021-22 to 2025-26. \n(d) The Ministry of Tribal Affairs implements various schemes for the welfare and \ndevelopment of Scheduled Tribes (STs) including education, health & nutrition, livelihood, \nagriculture, horticulture, skill & entrepreneurship development, drinking water & sanitation, \nroad connectivity etc. and infrastructure incidental thereto.  Details of funds provided by the \nMinistry to the States and other implementing agencies under various schemes for the last \nthree years is at Annexure. \n  \n  \n\n \nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred to in part (d) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 947 for answer on \n26.07.2021 \nRelease to States during 2018-19 \nSl. \nNo \nState/ UT Article 275 \n(1) \nSCA to TSS PVTGs Pre \nMatric \nPost \nMatric \nMSP to M